[{"id": "1427", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 1427 : Sort Bookmarks 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   NeoAngelic      :  quot;0.2.149.27 (1583) quot; Please implement a feature to sort bookmarks by name. I like to keep all of my bookmarks organized in folders  and within those folders are sometimes 10-30 bookmarks as well as sub-folders. As it is now I can not sort my bookmarks by name  which makes trying to find bookmarks confusing. As a further suggestion I think it would be cool if I could right-click on a bookmark folder on the bookmark bar and be able to select  quot;Sort By Name. quot; Thanks  keep up the good work  NeoAngelic \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:12:08.787494619Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to determine if it describes a security vulnerability or a non-security issue.\n\n**Analysis:**\n- The report is a **feature request** asking for the ability to sort bookmarks by name\n- The user describes wanting to organize bookmarks within folders and sub-folders\n- They suggest a right-click context menu option \"Sort By Name\"\n- There is no mention of any security-related concepts such as memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, DoS, or any exploitable vulnerability\n- This is purely a usability/organization feature request\n\n**Classification:**\nThis clearly falls under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) as it is a feature request related to user experience and UI functionality, with zero security implications.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about implementing a feature to sort bookmarks by name. The user is requesting:\n1. A feature to sort bookmarks by name\n2. The ability to right-click on a bookmark folder and select \"Sort By Name\"\n\nThis is clearly a feature request. The user wants a new functionality added to the application - the ability to sort bookmarks alphabetically.\n\nLooking at the classification criteria:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, **feature requests**, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact.\n- Class 1 (Security Bug): Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability.\n\nThis is a feature request, which falls squarely under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug). There is no security implication here - no memory corruption, no information disclosure, no DoS, no privilege escalation, etc.\n\nThe answer is clearly (A) Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 8331227238, "prompt_eval_count": 480, "eval_count": 367}}, {"id": "8227", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 8227 : ALT+F4 does not work at application shortcut. 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   nejsh21    Chrome Version : 2.0.166.1  ? 1. Create any application shortcut 2. click the shortcut 3. Press ALT+F4 to close the window.  ? The window should be closed.  ? Nothing.     This happens for any keyboard shortcut action. Once I use mouse to click something  or reload the page (press F5). Then everything works as usual. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:12:12.445689369Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether it describes a security vulnerability or a functional defect.\n\n**Analysis:**\n- The issue describes that ALT+F4 (and other keyboard shortcuts) don't work when an application shortcut is first opened\n- The workaround mentioned is clicking with the mouse or pressing F5, after which shortcuts work normally\n- This is a keyboard input handling issue in the UI layer\n\n**Key indicators:**\n- The bug involves a \"shortcut\" not working - explicitly mentioned in Class 0 indicators\n- There's no mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, information disclosure, or any exploitable security impact\n- The behavior described is a functional defect where keyboard events aren't properly registered until user interaction occurs\n- No confidentiality, integrity, or availability compromise is described\n\nThis is a UI/UX functional defect where keyboard focus isn't properly established when launching an application shortcut. There's no security vulnerability here - it's simply a usability issue with keyboard shortcut registration.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe issue describes:\n- ALT+F4 does not work at application shortcut\n- When clicking an application shortcut, keyboard shortcuts (like ALT+F4 to close) don't work initially\n- After clicking something with the mouse or pressing F5 to reload, keyboard shortcuts start working again\n\nThis is clearly a UI/UX functionality issue related to keyboard shortcuts not working properly in application shortcuts. There's no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Sandbox bypass\n- Any security vulnerability\n\nThe bug is about a functional defect where keyboard shortcuts don't respond until the user interacts with the page in some way (mouse click or reload). This is a usability/functional issue, not a security vulnerability.\n\nBased on the classification criteria:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to \"shortcut\" and user experience.\n\nThis clearly falls under Class 0 - Non-Security Bug because:\n1. It's about keyboard shortcuts not working (explicitly mentioned in Class 0 examples)\n2. No security impact is described\n3. No exploitable vulnerability is present\n4. It's a functional defect in the user interface interaction\n\nAnswer: (A) Non-Security Bug"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 11972151613, "prompt_eval_count": 444, "eval_count": 504}}, {"id": "2386", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 2386 : Installation error 1603: Chrome gears failed to install. 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   shruthi.murthy    The following specs may not be applicable as this is an installation problem.   :  lt;see about:version gt; URLs (if applicable) :    : Safari 3: Firefox 3: IE 7: =================  ? 1. On Windows XP SP2  go to http://www.google.com/chrome on FF3 2. Follow the chrome installation wizard  ? Successful installation.  ? Installation fails with: Installation failed. Component ChromeGears failed to install. Detailed message: The installer encountered error 1603: Fatal error during installation.     \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:12:13.980406077Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let me analyze this bug report step-by-step:\n\n1. **Content Analysis**: The report describes an installation failure of the Chrome browser (specifically the ChromeGears component) on Windows XP SP2. The error message is \"Installation failed. Component ChromeGears failed to install. Detailed message: The installer encountered error 1603: Fatal error during installation.\"\n\n2. **Security Indicators Check**:\n   - No mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap-related issues\n   - No information disclosure or data leakage concerns\n   - No denial of service vulnerability (the failure is local installation, not a remote exhaustion)\n   - No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n   - No cross-context scripting, redirects, or injection vectors\n   - The word \"Fatal\" in the error message is just Windows Installer's standard terminology for error code 1603, not an indication of a security exploit\n\n3. **Classification Assessment**: This is purely a functional defect in the installation process. The user cannot successfully install the software due to an installer error. This is a common software installation problem with no exploitable security impact.\n\n4. **Conclusion**: The bug clearly falls under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) as it's a functional defect related to the installation process failing, without any security implications.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully to determine if it's a security bug or a non-security bug.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Issue 2386: Installation error 1603\n- Chrome gears failed to install\n- On Windows XP SP2, trying to install Chrome from Firefox 3\n- The installation fails with: \"Installation failed. Component ChromeGears failed to install. Detailed message: The installer encountered error 1603: Fatal error during installation.\"\n\nThis is clearly an installation failure issue. Let me evaluate this against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- This is a functional defect - the installation process fails\n- There's no mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, information disclosure, or any security vulnerability\n- There's no exploitable security impact described\n- This is a general application/installation problem\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- None of the security-related keywords are present (no \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", privilege/sandboxing bypasses)\n- No indication of confidentiality, integrity, or availability compromise in a security sense\n- The \"fatal error\" mentioned is just the standard Windows installer error message format, not an indication of a security vulnerability\n\nThis is a straightforward installation failure - a non-security bug. The error 1603 is a generic Windows Installer error that indicates a fatal error during installation, but this is about the installation process failing, not about a security vulnerability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 13480274055, "prompt_eval_count": 482, "eval_count": 610}}, {"id": "5834", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general applicati