 should. This is a functional defect where a feature (Java applet games) is not working properly.\n\nLooking at the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact.\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS).\n\nThis bug report is about Java applets not loading - this is a functional defect. There's no indication of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Buffer overflow\n- Any security vulnerability\n\nThe issue is simply that the applets don't load, which is a functionality problem, not a security vulnerability. Even though one could argue that applets not loading might be related to security settings or sandbox restrictions, the report itself doesn't describe any exploitable security impact - it's just describing that a feature doesn't work.\n\nThis is clearly a Class 0 - Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 5908672169, "prompt_eval_count": 391, "eval_count": 606}}, {"id": "1402", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 1402 : html parsing problem URGENT!!! 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   DaxBooysen      :  lt;see about:version gt; URLs (if applicable) :    : Safari 3: OK Firefox 3: OK IE 7: OK  ? 1. http://www.ctrlaltdel-online.com/index.php 2. the page here is displayed incorrectly  that is correct on all other browsers. 3.  ?  ?     \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:12:23.057223883Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze the content carefully:\n\n**Key details from the report:**\n- Title: \"html parsing problem\"\n- The reporter indicates a webpage is \"displayed incorrectly\" in one browser\n- The same page works correctly in Safari 3, Firefox 3, and IE 7\n- This is a comparison of how content is visually rendered across different browsers\n\n**Analysis against classification criteria:**\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) indicators present:**\n- \"displayed incorrectly\" - directly relates to visual display/rendering issues\n- HTML parsing for display purposes - this is about how content is visually presented\n- Cross-browser compatibility concern - typical UI/UX rendering problem\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug) indicators absent:**\n- No mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap overwrite\n- No information disclosure concerns\n- No denial of service or resource exhaustion\n- No sandbox/privilege bypass\n- No cross-context issues or javascript: protocol concerns\n\nThis is a straightforward rendering/compatibility bug where a webpage doesn't display correctly in one browser compared to others. It's about visual presentation, not security vulnerabilities.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about:\n- Issue 1402: \"html parsing problem\"\n- The reporter is saying that a webpage (http://www.ctrlaltdel-online.com/index.php) is displayed incorrectly in one browser while it displays correctly in Safari 3, Firefox 3, and IE 7.\n- The issue is about how the page is rendered/displayed incorrectly compared to other browsers.\n\nLet me check against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- This involves a \"visual display\" issue - \"the page here is displayed incorrectly\"\n- This is a rendering/parsing issue related to how HTML is displayed\n- Keywords like \"displayed incorrectly\" point to UI/UX rendering issues\n- No mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, or other security impacts\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- Would need evidence of memory corruption, information disclosure, DoS, etc.\n- Would need keywords like \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", etc.\n- None of these are present in this report\n\nThis is clearly a rendering/display issue where a webpage doesn't look correct in one browser compared to others. This is a typical web compatibility/rendering bug, which falls squarely under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug).\n\nThe report mentions:\n- \"html parsing problem\" - this is about how HTML is parsed for display\n- \"displayed incorrectly\" - this is explicitly about visual rendering\n- Comparison with other browsers showing it works fine there - this is a compatibility/rendering issue\n\nThere is no security implication mentioned or implied in this report. It's purely about how the page looks (visual display issue).\n\nClassification: Class 0 - Non-Security Bug"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 22564396914, "prompt_eval_count": 435, "eval_count": 616}}, {"id": "198", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 198 : CSS rounded corners don't render properly when combined with box shadows 8 problem? Use -webkit-rounded-corner and -webkit-box-shadow on the same item. For example  div { -webkit-rounded-corner:5px; -webkit-box-shadow:1px 1px 2px #000; }  ? A box with a drop shadow that nicely follows its rounded corners.  ? See screenshot. The round corners are rendered with a black blackground with square corners. Also  the drop shadow seems much bigger than it should be.     Without the box shadow  the round corners display properly; this issue happens only when both properties are used together. chrome-rounded-corners.png 28.4 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download css-test.html 475 bytes  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:12:27.583157088Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze the nature of the issue described.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\n1. **Issue Description**: The report describes CSS rounded corners not rendering properly when combined with box shadows. Specifically, when `-webkit-rounded-corner` and `-webkit-box-shadow` are used together, the corners render with a black background and square corners, and the drop shadow appears larger than expected.\n\n2. **Keywords Present**: \"CSS\", \"render\", \"box-shadow\", \"rounded-corner\", \"background\", \"display\" - these are all visual/UI rendering terms.\n\n3. **Nature of the Bug**: This is purely a visual rendering defect where two CSS properties interact incorrectly, causing the visual output to look wrong. There is no indication of:\n   - Memory corruption or buffer overflow\n   - Information disclosure\n   - Privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n   - Denial of Service through resource exhaustion\n   - Any exploitable security vulnerability\n\n4. **Impact**: The bug affects only the visual presentation of web content. It does not compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability of the system or user data.\n\n**Conclusion**: This is clearly a CSS rendering/UI issue that falls squarely into the Non-Security Bug category, as indicated by the domain knowledge's explicit mention of \"render\", \"CSS\", and \"layout\" as distinctive indicators for Class 0.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- CSS rounded corners don't render properly when combined with box shadows\n- The issue is about visual rendering of CSS properties (-webkit-rounded-corner and -webkit-box-shadow)\n- The round corners are rendered with a black background with square corners\n- The drop shadow appears bigger than it should be\n- This is a visual/UI rendering issue\n\nBased on the domain knowledge provided:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems... Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\")\n\nThis bug report clearly falls under Class 0 because:\n1. It's about CSS rendering - a visual display issue\n2. Keywords like \"render\", \"CSS\", \"box-shadow\", \"rounded-corner\" are all visual/UI related\n3. There's no mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any security vulnerability\n4. It's purely a visual defect where the corners and shadow don't display correctly when combined\n\nThis is definitely a Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 14857914040, "prompt_eval_count": 491, "eval_count": 525}}, {"id": "13097", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 13097 : Set LSMinimumSystemVersion to 10.5 to avoid launching on pre-Leopard systems in a friendly way 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   mark@chromium.org    http://code.google.com/p/chromium/issues/detail?id=13087 Instead of giving users who try to launch on 10.4 messages like this: dyld: Symbol not found: _kLSQuarantineTypeKey Referenced from: /Applications/Google Chrome.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome Expected in: /System/Library/Frameworks/CoreServices.framework/Versions/A/CoreServices we should declare to the OS that we require 10.5 so that it can give a more effective error when someone attempts to launch on an older system. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:12:34.030561439Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to determine if it describes a security vulnerability or a non-security issue.\n\n**Analysis:**\n- The issue requests setting `LSMinimumSystemVersion` to 10.5 in the application's Info.plist\n- The current problem is that users on Mac OS X 10.4 receive a cryptic dyld error message about missing symbols (`_kLSQuarantineTypeKey`)\n- The proposed solution is to declare the minimum system version so the OS can present a more user-friendly error message\n- This is purely a **usability/UX improvement** for error messaging\n- There is no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any exploitable condition\n- The dyld symbol error is a consequence of running on an unsupported OS, not a security vulnerability\n\n**Key observations:**\n- Keywords relate to user experience (\"friendly way\", \"more effective error\")\n- No security-related keywords from the Class 1 criteria appear\n- The underlying functionality (refusing to run on older OS) isn't about security\u2014it's about compatibility and clearer communication to users\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe issue is about setting `LSMinimumSystemVersion` to 10.5 in the application's plist to prevent the app from launching on pre-Leopard (Mac OS X 10.4) systems. Currently, when users on 10.4 try to launch Chrome, they get a cryptic dyld error message about missing symbols. By declaring the minimum system version, the OS would give a friendlier error message.\n\nLet me evaluate this against the criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- This is about improving user experience/error messaging\n- It's about displaying a better error message to users\n- No mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, or DoS\n- No exploitability concerns\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- Looking for: memory corruption, buffer overflow, heap overwrite, cross-context, exhaustion, javascript:, redirect, advisory, privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n- The dyld error mentions symbol not found, but this isn't about exploiting anything\n- No confidentiality, integrity, or availability concerns\n\nThis is clearly a usability/UX improvement. The bug is about providing a better error message when users try to run the application on an unsupported OS version. There's no security vulnerability here - it's not about preventing exploitation, it's about giving users a clearer message about system requirements.\n\nWhile preventing an app from running on unsupported systems could have security implications (unsupported systems don't get security updates), this particular bug is about the user experience of the error message, not about a security vulnerability.\n\nThe classification should be: Non-Security Bug (Class 0)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 10854371871, "prompt_eval_count": 487, "eval_count": 575}}, {"id": "20564", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain k