rce exhaustion\n- No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n- No cross-context issues or malicious URL schemes\n- No evidence of exploitable security impact\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) indicators:** Present\n- This is a functional defect - a plugin fails to load content\n- It's a browser compatibility issue (works in other browsers)\n- The behavior is \"nothing happens\" - a simple failure to render/load, not a crash with security implications\n- No mention of any exploitable condition\n\nThis is clearly a standard functional/compatibility bug where a plugin doesn't work in a specific browser. There is no security impact - no confidentiality, integrity, or availability compromise beyond the normal functionality not working.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about:\n- Issue 643: Shockwave plugin not working\n- The reporter is trying to load a Shockwave game on a website (neopets.com)\n- In Chrome, the shockwave game will not load\n- In Safari 3, Firefox 3, and IE 7, it works fine\n- Expected behavior: The game should start\n- Actual behavior: Nothing happens\n\nThis is clearly a functional compatibility issue - the Shockwave plugin isn't working in Chrome but works in other browsers. This is a feature/plugin compatibility problem, not a security vulnerability.\n\nLet me check against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- Functional defects \u2713 (plugin doesn't work)\n- General application crashes/issues without exploitable security impact \u2713 (game won't load)\n- This is a compatibility/functional issue\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- Memory corruption - No\n- Information disclosure - No\n- Denial of Service (DoS) - No\n- Memory leak, buffer overflow, heap overwrite - No\n- Cross-context, javascript:, redirect, advisory - No\n- Privilege/sandboxing bypasses - No\n\nThere's no indication of any security vulnerability here. This is simply a plugin compatibility issue where the Shockwave plugin doesn't work in Chrome but works in other browsers. It's a functional defect.\n\nThe answer should be (A) Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 15084487464, "prompt_eval_count": 514, "eval_count": 627}}, {"id": "14696", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 14696 : [www.soleg.net]: Text overlap when window side is shrinked. 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   fin