ug - a cosmetic/layout issue with no exploitable security implications.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Issue 14696 on www.soleg.net\n- Problem: Text overlap when the browser window is shrinked/resized\n- Severity: \"Cosmetic problem\"\n- The text becomes overlapped/jumbled when the Chrome window size is reduced\n- Other browsers also have similar issues (Firefox3, Maxthon2 fail; IE6, IE7 work)\n\nKey indicators from the domain knowledge:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) includes: \"UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems\"\n- Keywords like \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\" are indicators of non-security bugs\n- The report explicitly states the severity is \"Cosmetic problem\"\n\nThis is clearly a UI/layout rendering issue - text overlapping when the window is resized. There's no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Any exploitable security vulnerability\n- Any of the security keywords like \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n\nThis is purely a cosmetic/layout issue - text not displaying properly when the window is resized. It falls squarely into Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 14386365683, "prompt_eval_count": 546, "eval_count": 507}}, {"id": "3089", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 3089 : Find is not working properly in Chrome 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   inainsi...@gmail.com    OS: XP and Vista Chrome v-154.0 works fine in v-153.1  ? 1. Any web page  quot;yahoo quot; or  quot; http://news.google.com  quot; 2. Press CTRL+F or find 3. Now type any letter and close it 4. You will observe that after closing the find box it select the text or if it is link it will select the entire link What is the expected output? What do you see instead? Should not select the link or text only happening in Chrome v-154.0  works fine in v-153.1 See Attached video \\\\filer\\home\\indernain\\video\\find_error.avi See Attached Screen Shot find.jpg 103 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:14:32.989834228Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze it for security relevance.\n\n**Bug Description:**\n- The \"Find\" functionality (Ctrl+F) in Chrome has a UI issue\n- After searching for text and closing the find box, the matched text or link remains selected/highlighted\n- This is a regression in Chrome v-154.0 (worked fine in v-153.1)\n- Expected behavior: text/link should not remain selected after closing the find box\n\n**Analysis against classification criteria:**\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) indicators present:**\n- This is a UI/UX rendering issue - text selection state not being properly cleared\n- Keywords match: visual display issue, user experience problem\n- This is a functional defect in the find feature's behavior\n- No exploitable security impact identified\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug) indicators absent:**\n- No memory corruption (no \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\")\n- No information disclosure\n- No Denial of Service or resource exhaustion\n- No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n- No cross-context issues or malicious URL schemes\n- No confidentiality, integrity, or availability compromise\n\nThe bug is simply that the visual selection state persists after closing the find dialog - a cosmetic/UX issue. While text selection could theoretically be used in some attack scenarios (like drag-and-drop exploits), this report doesn't describe any such exploitation path, and the selection state is something the user themselves initiated by searching.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- An issue with the \"Find\" functionality (Ctrl+F) in Chrome\n- When you type a letter in the find box and then close it, the text or link remains selected\n- This happens in Chrome v-154.0 but not in v-153.1\n- The expected behavior is that the text/link should not remain selected after closing the find box\n\nNow let me classify this according to the criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- This is a UI/UX issue - text remaining highlighted after closing the find dialog\n- It's a functional defect in the find feature\n- No mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, or DoS\n- No keywords like \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", privilege escalation, or sandboxing bypasses\n- This is essentially a visual display/UX issue where the selection state isn't properly cleared\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- None present in this report\n\nThis is clearly a UI/UX bug where the find functionality doesn't properly deselect text when the find box is closed. There's no security implication here - no confidentiality, integrity, or availability concerns. It's just a visual/state management issue in the browser's find feature.\n\nThe answer should be (A) Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 14589406424, "prompt_eval_count": 525, "eval_count": 618}}, {"id": "10053", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 10053 : Prioritize css/js/etc higher than image requests 5      nbsp;   willchan@chromium.org    It's more important to load css/js/etc than images in order to improve user perceived page load time. We'll have to break up ResourceType::SUB_RESOURCE into multiple ResourceType::Type enum values  and then adjust priorities accordingly. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:14:37.770067416Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze its content against the classification criteria.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe bug report discusses:\n- Prioritizing CSS/JS requests higher than image requests\n- Goal: Improve user perceived page load time\n- Technical approach: Breaking up `ResourceType::SUB_RESOURCE` into multiple enum values to adjust priorities\n\n**Key observations:**\n1. This is a **performance optimization** request focused on page load time\n2. The mention of \"CSS\" and \"JS\" is about **resource loading priority**, not about visual rendering bugs or security vulnerabilities\n3. The goal is explicitly stated as improving **user experience** (\"user perceived page load time\")\n4. There is no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, sandbox escapes, or any exploitable security impact\n5. No indicators of security relevance such as \"buffer overflow\", \"memory leak\", \"cross-context\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", or bypass mechanisms\n\nThis is a performance/UX enhancement request, not a security vulnerability. Adjusting resource loading priorities may improve page load experience but does not introduce or address any security boundary violations.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about:\n- Prioritizing CSS/JS/etc higher than image requests\n- The goal is to improve user perceived page load time\n- The proposal is to break up ResourceType::SUB_RESOURCE into multiple ResourceType::Type enum values and adjust priorities accordingly\n\nThis is clearly about:\n1. Performance optimization - improving page load time\n2. Resource loading priorities - changing the order in which resources are loaded\n3. Code refactoring - breaking up an enum into multiple values\n\nLet me check against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- Keywords related to visual display: \"CSS\" is mentioned, but in the context of loading priority, not rendering issues\n- User experience: \"improve user perceived page load time\" - this is about UX/performance\n- This is essentially a feature request or performance improvement\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- Memory corruption: No\n- Information disclosure: No\n- Denial of Service: No\n- Memory leak, buffer overflow, heap overwrite: No\n- Cross-context, javascript:, redirect, advisory: No\n- Privilege/sandboxing bypasses: No\n\nThis bug report is about performance optimization - specifically prioritizing which resources load first to improve perceived page load time. There's no security vulnerability being described here. It's a feature request or enhancement to improve user experience (page load time).\n\nThe answer should be Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 7510174832, "prompt_eval_count": 397, "eval_count": 557}}, {"id": "878", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 878 : delete history entries from omni