y Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Issue 19762: \"There aren't any checkboxes\"\n- Chrome Version: 3.0.195.6\n- URL: https://services.google.com/fb/forms/wavesignup/\n- Description: \"just go to the page and there is going to appear check if applied but there is no checkbox\"\n\nThis is clearly a UI/UX rendering issue where checkboxes are not appearing on a web page. The user is reporting that checkboxes that should be visible on a form are not rendering properly.\n\nAccording to the domain knowledge:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): \"Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., 'render', 'CSS', 'layout'), user experience (e.g., 'feature request', 'shortcut'), and generic instability (e.g., 'crash' without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\"\n\nThis bug falls squarely into the UI/UX rendering issue category. There's no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Buffer overflow\n- Any security vulnerability\n- Cross-context issues\n- Redirect issues\n- Privilege bypasses\n\nThis is simply a visual display problem where checkboxes aren't rendering on a form.\n\nThe classification should be: (A) Non-Security Bug"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 6815208064, "prompt_eval_count": 407, "eval_count": 571}}, {"id": "20730", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 20730 : Drag and drop fails with single tab 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   venkataramana@chromium.org    -Open two windows with one tab each -Try drag and drop one tab to the other window. You can't. Basically  user can't drag and drop a tab from a single tab window to other window. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:14:46.236716719Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze the described issue:\n\n**Bug Summary:**\n- Drag and drop functionality fails when trying to move a single tab from one window to another\n- This is a UI interaction issue where tabs cannot be dragged between windows when each window only has one tab\n\n**Analysis:**\n- The issue is about a missing or broken UI/UX feature (drag and drop of tabs)\n- There is no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any exploitable vulnerability\n- No security-related keywords are present (no \"buffer overflow\", \"memory leak\", \"sandbox bypass\", \"cross-context\", etc.)\n- This is purely a functional/usability defect - a user experience problem with tab management\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThis is a functional defect related to UI/UX interaction. A broken drag-and-drop feature for tabs does not compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability of the system. It's simply a usability bug.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Issue 20730: Drag and drop fails with single tab\n- The problem: When you have two windows with one tab each, you can't drag and drop a tab from one window to another window.\n- This is a UI/UX functionality issue related to tab management.\n\nNow let me check against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- Functional defects \u2713 (this is a functional defect - drag and drop not working)\n- UI/UX rendering issues \u2713 (this relates to user experience with tab management)\n- Feature requests\n- General application crashes without exploitable security impact\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service (DoS)\n- Memory leak, buffer overflow, heap overwrite\n- Cross-context issues\n- javascript:, redirect\n- Advisory\n- Privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n\nThis bug is clearly about a UI/UX functionality problem - the drag and drop feature for tabs isn't working correctly. There's no indication of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- DoS\n- Any security vulnerability\n- Any exploitability\n\nThis is purely a usability/functional bug where a feature (drag and drop tabs between windows) doesn't work as expected. It has no security implications.\n\nThe answer should be (A) Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 5950409138, "prompt_eval_count": 406, "eval_count": 502}}, {"id": "2311", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 2311 : Need a URL that shows tree open/close state 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   ben.at.chromium.org    e.g. build.chromium.org/isthetreeopen ... we should then access this URL from gcl commit before checking in to make sure the tree is open before the commit proceeds. a -f/--force option would override for people checking in bustage fixes. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:14:47.339902638Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze its content against the classification criteria.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe bug report is requesting:\n1. A URL endpoint that displays whether the \"tree\" (source code commit tree) is open or closed\n2. Integration with `gcl commit` to automatically check tree status before committing\n3. A `-f/--force` option to bypass this check for bustage fixes\n\n**Evaluating against Class 1 (Security Bug) criteria:**\n- No memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap overwrite\n- No information disclosure vulnerability\n- No denial of service (DoS) concern\n- No cross-context issues, javascript: URLs, or redirect vulnerabilities\n- No privilege/sandboxing bypass - the `--force` flag is simply a command-line option for authorized developers to bypass a workflow check, not a security bypass\n- No exploitation potential or adversarial threat mentioned\n\n**Evaluating against Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) criteria:**\n- This is clearly a **feature request** - asking for new functionality\n- It's a developer workflow/process improvement\n- The keywords and intent relate to \"feature request\" and process automation\n- No exploitable security impact\n\nThe `-f/--force` option is not a security concern - it's a standard development tool feature where the person running the command locally chooses to override a convenience check. There's no privilege escalation or unauthorized access involved.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nIssue 2311: \"Need a URL that shows tree open/close state\"\n\nThe bug report is requesting:\n1. A URL that shows whether the \"tree\" (likely referring to the source code tree/commit queue) is open or closed\n2. They want to access this URL from `gcl commit` before checking in to make sure the tree is open before the commit proceeds\n3. A `-f/--force` option would override for people checking in bustage fixes\n\nThis is essentially a feature request for:\n- A status URL showing tree open/close state\n- Integration with the commit process to check tree status\n- A force flag to bypass the tree status check\n\nLet me evaluate this against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- This is clearly a \"feature request\" - they're asking for new functionality\n- It's about workflow/process improvement for developers\n- No mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, DoS, or other security vulnerabilities\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- No memory corruption, buffer overflow, heap overwrite\n- No information disclosure concerns\n- No DoS vulnerability\n- No cross-context issues, javascript:, redirect vulnerabilities\n- No privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n- The `-f/--force` option is just a convenience for developers who need to bypass the tree status check for bustage fixes - this isn't a security bypass concern\n\nThe request is purely about developer workflow improvement - adding a check to prevent commits when the tree is closed. This is a standard practice in development teams to prevent commits during unstable periods or maintenance.\n\nThe `-f/--force` option is not a security vulnerability - it's a legitimate feature for authorized developers who need to commit fixes for build bustage. This is controlled by the developer running the command locally, not an exploit or bypass.\n\nThis is clearly a **Non-Security Bug** - it's a feature request for process/workflow improvement."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 9133385662, "prompt_eval_count": 417, "eval_count": 716}}, {"id": "9712", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 9712 : Bookmark's status (star) is not shown for long titles in New histor page 2      nbsp;   venkataramana@chromium.org    Build: 2.0.172.2 (Official Build 13060) -Bookmark a page which has long title (eg: http://economictimes.indiatimes.com/ ) -Check the bookmark status (star) for the page in History page. You can't find it  because of it's long tile which is eclipsed. I guess we should show the star button after the eclipses. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:14:50.823034177Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\n**Bug Description Analysis:**\n- The issue is about a bookmark's star icon not being visible in the History page when a page has a long title\n- The long title \"eclipses\" (overlaps/hides) the star button\n- The suggested fix is to show the star button after the ellipsis\n\n**Key Indicators:**\n- This is a **visual display/layout problem** - the star icon is hidden due to spacing\n- Keywords present: \"shown\", \"title\", \"eclipsed\" - all related to UI rendering\n- No mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any exploitable vulnerability\n- The bug affects user experience (visibility of bookmark status), not system security\n\n**Security Assessment:**\n- There is no confidentiality, integrity, or availability impact\n- No malicious exploitation potential\n- This is purely a cosmetic/UI layout issue where a long title overlaps an icon\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThis clearly falls under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) as it involves a UI/UX rendering issue with no security implications.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report describes an issue where:\n- When a page has a long title, the bookmark status (star) icon is not visible in the History page\n- The long title eclipses/hides the star button\n- The suggestion is to show the star button after the ellipsis\n\nThis is clearly a UI/UX display issue. The keywords here are:\n- \"Bookmark's status (star) is not shown\" - visual display issue\n- \"long title\" - layout issue\n- \"eclipsed\" - visual rendering problem\n- The suggestion is about repositioning a UI element\n\nThis falls squarely into Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) because:\n1. It's a visual display/layout problem\n2. There's no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, DoS, or any security vulnerability\n3. It involves UI rendering (star icon visibility)\n4. No exploitable security impact is mentioned\n5. Keywords like \"render\", \"layout\", visual display are indicators of non-security bugs\n\nThis is simply a UI bug where the bookmark star icon gets hidden when the page title is too long. It's a cosmetic/functional issue, not a security vulnerability.\n\nThe answer should be (A) Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 31214660014, "prompt_eval_count": 446, "eval_count": 506}}, {"id": "9472", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 9472 : Inspector not showing data on latest betas (2.0.171.0) 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   sgtipl...@gmail.com    Chrome Version : 2.0.171.0 (Official Build 12323) URLs (if applicable) : All    : Safari 4: Unknown Firefox 3.x: OK IE 7: OK IE 8: OK  ? 1. Right click within the browser window 2. Select  quot;Inspect Element quot; 3. Inspector popups up and shows no Elements  Resources  Styles  metrics or Properties  ? Inspected element details  ? No information shown \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:15:02.20260106Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report step-by-step:\n\n**Issue Analysis:**\n- The bug describes Chrome's Inspector tool not displaying data (Elements, Resources, Styles, metrics, Properties)\n- This occurs when right-clicking and selecting \"Inspect Element\"\n- The Inspector window opens but shows no information\n\n**Classification Evaluation:**\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) indicators:**\n- This is clearly a UI/UX rendering issue - the Inspector is failing to display content visually\n- Keywords align with visual display problems: \"not showing data\", \"no information shown\"\n- This is a functional defect in the developer tools feature\n- The Inspector is a debugging/development utility, and its failure to render data is a usability issue\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug) indicators:**\n- No keywords suggesting memory corruption, information disclosure, DoS, privilege escalation, or sandbox bypass\n- No mention of \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", or \"advisory\"\n- While the Inspector deals with viewing page data, the failure to display is not an information disclosure vulnerability - it's the opposite (data not appearing)\n- No evidence of exploitable security impact\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThis is a straightforward UI rendering bug where the developer tools Inspector is not displaying data properly. There is no security implication - it's a functional defect affecting the visual display of debugging information.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report describes an issue where:\n1. The Inspector tool in Chrome (version 2.0.171.0) is not showing data\n2. When a user right-clicks and selects \"Inspect Element\", the Inspector pops up but shows no Elements, Resources, Styles, metrics, or Properties\n3. The expected behavior is that inspected element details should be shown, but no information is displayed\n\nLet me evaluate this against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- This is a UI/UX rendering issue - the Inspector tool is not displaying data\n- This involves visual display problems (data not showing)\n- This is a functional defect in the developer tools feature\n- There's no indication of memory corruption, information disclosure, DoS, or other security implications\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- None present in this report\n- No keywords like \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", or privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n- No indication of compromised confidentiality, integrity, or availability in a security sense\n\nThis is clearly a UI/display bug where the developer tools (Inspector) is not rendering data properly. The Inspector is a debugging/development tool, and its failure to display information is a functional/visual issue, not a security vulnerability.\n\nThe fact that the Inspector doesn't show data could potentially be related to security (if, for example, the Inspector is blocked from accessing certain contexts due to a security feature), but there's no indication in this report that this is a security mechanism or bypass - it's just a broken feature.\n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, I should classify this as a Non-Security Bug since:\n1. It's a UI/UX rendering issue\n2. It involves visual display problems\n3. There's no clear evidence of security relevance\n4. It's a functional defect without exploitable security impact"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 21919661808, "prompt_eval_count": 473, "eval_count": 724}}, {"id": "12866", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 12866 : Cmd-L should reselect on 2nd try 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   mikesmith@chromium.org    Chrome Version : 3.0.1.182 URLs (if applicable) : OS version : 10.5.7 Behavior in Safari 3.x/4.x (if applicable) Reselects the address bar Behavior in Firefox 3.x (if applicable):  ? 1. from any URL  type cmd-L 2. start typing (don't hit return/enter) 3. cmd-L again Result : nothing happens  ? Reselect the address bar contents  ? \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:15:08.092400632Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report step-by-step:\n\n1. **What is being reported**: The keyboard shortcut Cmd-L (which selects the address bar) doesn't reselect the address bar contents when pressed a second time after the user starts typing.\n\n2. **Key details**:\n   - This is about a keyboard shortcut behavior (\"Cmd-L should reselect on 2nd try\")\n   - The comparison is with Safari's UX behavior\n   - The issue is that pressing Cmd-L again doesn't reselect address bar contents\n   - Keywords: \"shortcut\", \"reselect\", \"address bar\" - all UI/UX related\n\n3. **Security assessment**: There is no mention of:\n   - Memory corruption or leaks\n   - Buffer overflows\n   - Information disclosure\n   - Denial of Service\n   - Privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n   - Any exploitable vulnerability\n\n4. **Classification**: This is purely a usability/functional issue about how a keyboard shortcut should behave to match expected user experience. It's a feature request or UX bug about shortcut behavior consistency.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about Issue 12866 regarding the keyboard shortcut Cmd-L behavior. Let me break down what's being reported:\n\n1. The issue is about the Cmd-L keyboard shortcut\n2. When you press Cmd-L once, it selects the address bar\n3. When you start typing (without pressing enter) and press Cmd-L again, nothing happens\n4. The expected behavior (as seen in Safari) is that pressing Cmd-L again should reselect the address bar contents\n\nThis is clearly a user interface/UX behavior issue related to a keyboard shortcut. It's about how the address bar selection works when using a keyboard shortcut multiple times.\n\nKeywords from the report:\n- \"Cmd-L\" - a keyboard shortcut\n- \"reselect\" - a UI selection behavior\n- \"address bar\" - UI element\n\nThere's no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Privilege escalation\n- Sandbox bypass\n- Any security-related vulnerability\n\nThis is purely a functional/UX bug about how a keyboard shortcut should behave. The user is requesting that a shortcut should work consistently to reselect the address bar contents on subsequent presses.\n\nBased on the classification criteria:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Includes functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, feature requests, and keywords like \"shortcut\"\n- Class 1 (Security Bug): Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability\n\nThis clearly falls under Class 0 as it's a keyboard shortcut behavior issue - a UX/functional concern with no security implications.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 16782146709, "prompt_eval_count": 465, "eval_count": 574}}, {"id": "11016", "ground_truth": "1", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 11016 : Can't tell what URL was downloaded 4      nbsp;   abarth@chromium.org    When I download a file (like an EXE)  I have no idea where it came from. For example  visit: https://www.mozilla.com/en-US/products/download.html?product=firefox- 3.0.9 amp;os=win amp;lang=en-US Did you just download Firefox over HTTPS? It's anyone's guess. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:15:25.570828108Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether the inability to see the source URL of a downloaded file constitutes a security issue.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe bug reports that when downloading a file (specifically executables like .EXE), the user cannot determine the actual URL from which the file was downloaded. The example highlights that it's impossible to tell whether Firefox was downloaded over HTTPS or not.\n\n**Security Implications:**\n\n1. **Information Disclosure/Integrity Verification**: Not showing the download URL prevents users from verifying the source and security properties of downloaded files. This directly impacts a user's ability to assess file trustworthiness.\n\n2. **HTTPS vs HTTP distinction**: The inability to confirm whether a download occurred over an encrypted channel (HTTPS) means users cannot verify that their download wasn't intercepted or tampered with via man-in-the-middle attacks.\n\n3. **Phishing/Malware risk**: If users can't verify the origin of downloaded executables, they cannot make informed security decisions about whether to run them. A malicious site could serve malware without the user being able to trace its origin.\n\n4. **Missing security-relevant metadata**: The download source URL is security-critical information that should be preserved and displayed to users for executable files specifically.\n\nThis is clearly a security vulnerability \u2014 it involves the failure to disclose security-relevant information (the download URL, including protocol) that users need to make trust decisions about executable files. This compromises the user's ability to verify the integrity and authenticity of downloads.\n\n**Answer: (B) Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is titled \"Can't tell what URL was downloaded\" and describes an issue where when downloading a file (like an EXE), the user cannot tell where the file came from. The example given is downloading Firefox from a specific URL, and the user questions whether they downloaded it over HTTPS.\n\nThis is about information disclosure - specifically, the inability to verify the source/URL of a downloaded file. This has clear security implications:\n\n1. If a user can't tell where a downloaded file came from, they can't verify its authenticity\n2. This relates to the integrity and trustworthiness of downloads\n3. Being unable to verify if a download occurred over HTTPS vs HTTP is a security concern - it affects whether the download could have been tampered with (man-in-the-middle attack)\n4. EXE files are executable, and not knowing their source could lead to running malicious software\n\nThis is essentially a security issue about transparency/information disclosure - the user should be able to see the full URL they downloaded a file from to verify its security properties (like whether it was HTTPS, what domain it came from, etc.).\n\nThe keywords and concepts here:\n- URL verification\n- HTTPS vs HTTP (encryption/security)\n- EXE download source verification\n- Information disclosure about download origin\n\nThis falls under Class 1 (Security Bug) because:\n- It involves a vulnerability that compromises integrity (can't verify file authenticity)\n- It's related to information disclosure (not showing the download URL)\n- It has security implications for user safety when downloading executables\n\nThe inability to verify where a downloaded file came from is a security concern, as it prevents users from making informed security decisions about whether to trust/run downloaded files.\n\n**Answer: (B) Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 17315115141, "prompt_eval_count": 421, "eval_count": 678}}, {"id": "7428", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 7428 : Wrong tests being marked as timed out from TestShell 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   kuchhal@chromium.org    Mads noticed this problem and said this in an email: It seems that if a test that uses waitUntilDone fails to call notifyDone  the *next* test that is run will time out. I think this is the reason for the new failures you got after this change to dump on notifyDone. I guess nothing is dumped if you fail to call notifyDone? To reproduce  here is an example: [ager@ager-xp:/cygdrive/c/chrome/src/webkit/tools/layout_tests]$ ./run_webkit_tests.sh LayoutTests/fast/dom/Window/clear-timeout.html LayoutTests/fast/dom/Window/window-property-shadowing.html This will have window-property-shadowing pass. However  if you change clear-timeout.html by deleting the lines that calls notifyDone  then window-property-shadowing will timeout. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:15:38.745934858Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\n**What the bug describes:**\n- In TestShell, if a test using `waitUntilDone` fails to call `notifyDone`, the *next* test run will incorrectly time out\n- This is a state leakage issue between consecutive tests in the testing framework\n- The example shows how deleting the `notifyDone` call from one test causes a completely different, subsequent test to be marked as timed out\n\n**Security Analysis:**\n- This is a **test framework/harness issue** - it affects how tests are executed and their results reported\n- The problem is improper state cleanup between tests in TestShell, not a vulnerability in the application itself\n- No security-relevant keywords or patterns are present (no memory corruption, no privilege escalation, no information disclosure, no sandbox escape, no cross-context issues)\n- The \"timeout\" here refers to a test being incorrectly marked as failing due to leftover state, not a DoS condition affecting users\n- This impacts developer workflow and test reliability, not the security posture of the shipped product\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThis is a functional defect in the test infrastructure. The failure to reset state between tests is a quality/testing issue, not a security vulnerability. There is no evidence that this could be exploited to compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability of the application.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report is about:\n- Wrong tests being marked as timed out from TestShell\n- If a test that uses `waitUntilDone` fails to call `notifyDone`, the *next* test that is run will time out\n- This is a testing framework issue where state from one test bleeds into the next test\n\nLet me evaluate this against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) indicators:**\n- This is a functional defect in the test framework\n- It's about test infrastructure not properly cleaning up state between tests\n- The issue is about incorrect test results (wrong tests being marked as timed out)\n- This affects the reliability of testing but doesn't directly compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability of the actual application\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug) indicators:**\n- Looking at the security-related keywords: \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n- None of these security indicators are present\n- The issue involves `waitUntilDone` and `notifyDone` which are test framework functions, not security-sensitive operations\n\nThe bug is about test state leaking from one test to another in the TestShell. This is a test framework reliability issue, not a security vulnerability. The failure to call `notifyDone` causing the next test to time out is a functional defect in the test harness, not an exploitable security condition.\n\nThis should be classified as a Non-Security Bug (Class 0)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 51150399249, "prompt_eval_count": 531, "eval_count": 618}}, {"id": "5084", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conc