ting purposes. It's about making layout tests (which test UI/rendering) work correctly. This falls squarely into the Non-Security Bug category.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 15639656238, "prompt_eval_count": 391, "eval_count": 411}}, {"id": "14381", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 14381 : [www.youtube.com]: YouTube page does not start loading the movie 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes. Back to list Status: nbsp; WontFix Owner: nbsp; ---- Closed: nbsp; Jul 2009 Cc: nbsp; karen@chromium.org Type- Bug Pri- 2 OS- All Finditbug Area- Compat  nbsp;   findit.role    URL: http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tQRnhG3gSOo amp;feature=featured Browser: Chrome 2.0.172.31 [Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US) AppleWebKit/530.5 (KHTML  like Gecko) Chrome/2.0.172.31 Safari/530.5] Severity: Certain site functionality doesn't work in Google Chrome What is wrong? The movie simply does not load. Does not happen with FF  ? Usually I click on a movie and it loads -- I can wait forever  the movie will not load. Happens with about 1/2 of the youtube movies I try to watch. I simply hit  quot;reload quot; a few times -- and suddenly it works.  ? 1. Not sure -- simply try selecting movies 2. 3. Does it work properly in other browsers? Which did you test? Does not happne in FF -- I have not experienced it. In Chrome it is a commom   youTube.JPG 116 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:17:17.248849978Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let me analyze this bug report step-by-step.\n\n**Issue Summary:**\n- YouTube videos don't load in Chrome (version 2.0.172.31)\n- Affects approximately half of YouTube videos\n- Workaround: reloading the page multiple times eventually works\n- Does not occur in Firefox\n- Categorized as \"Area-Compat\" (compatibility issue)\n\n**Key Indicators Analysis:**\n1. **Nature of the problem**: The video content simply fails to load - this is a functional defect where site functionality doesn't work as expected\n2. **Classification tags**: \"Type-Bug\", \"Area-Compat\" - explicitly marked as a compatibility bug, not a security issue\n3. **Severity description**: \"Certain site functionality doesn't work in Google Chrome\" - purely functional\n4. **No security keywords**: No mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, information disclosure, privilege escalation, DoS, exhaustion, or any exploitable condition\n5. **Impact**: User inconvenience - videos don't play, requiring reloads. This affects usability, not security\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a standard web compatibility issue where Chrome fails to properly load YouTube video content. There is no evidence of:\n- Memory corruption or leaks\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of service (the browser itself doesn't crash or become exhausted)\n- Any exploitable vulnerability\n\nThe bug is purely a functional defect related to site compatibility.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about:\n- YouTube page does not start loading the movie\n- The movie simply does not load\n- Happens with about 1/2 of the YouTube movies\n- Hitting reload a few times makes it work\n- Does not happen in Firefox\n- Chrome 2.0.172.31\n\nThis is clearly a compatibility/functional issue where YouTube videos don't load properly in Chrome. The bug is about:\n1. Site compatibility (Area- Compat)\n2. Functionality not working properly\n3. A rendering/loading issue with video content\n\nLooking at the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact.\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nThis bug report describes:\n- A functional defect (video doesn't load)\n- A compatibility issue between Chrome and YouTube\n- No security implications mentioned\n- No memory corruption, information disclosure, DoS, or other security-related issues\n\nThe bug is categorized as \"Area- Compat\" (compatibility), which is clearly a non-security functional issue. There's no indication of any security vulnerability - it's simply a website functionality problem where videos don't load consistently.\n\nThis is clearly a **Non-Security Bug** - it's a functional/compatibility defect."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 7089704074, "prompt_eval_count": 654, "eval_count": 677}}, {"id": "5467", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 5467 : Strange delay when reusing a tab  lsaquo; Prev 6109 of 14151 Next  rsaquo; 3 problem? 1. Open a new tab 2. Go to any site  it comes up immediately 3. In the same tab  go to a different site. Approximately 30 second delay before any actions take place.  ? - Pages should load immediately  just as they do when I open a new tab and type in a URL.  ? - If I have already visited any other site  typing in a new URL results in a 20-30 second delay before any noticeable activity.     - This began happening a few days ago. It only happens on one PC. I will repair the install and see if that fixes the problem. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:17:17.912126547Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether it describes a security vulnerability or a functional issue.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe report describes:\n- A ~30 second delay when navigating to a new URL in an existing tab\n- New tabs load sites immediately; the delay only occurs when reusing a tab\n- The issue started recently on one PC\n- The user suggests repairing the installation\n\n**Key considerations:**\n- This is a **performance/latency issue** - the browser is slow to respond, not crashed or compromised\n- There is **no evidence of memory corruption** (no buffer overflow, heap overwrite, use-after-free)\n- There is **no information disclosure** risk mentioned\n- There is **no exploitable Denial of Service** - this is a UI responsiveness delay during normal user navigation, not an attacker-triggered resource exhaustion or crash\n- The symptoms point to a **local configuration or installation issue** (happens on one PC, recent onset, user considers repair)\n- No security-related keywords or patterns are present (no sandbox bypass, privilege escalation, cross-context issue, etc.)\n\nWhile \"delay\" could theoretically relate to availability, this does not constitute a security DoS vulnerability - it's a general performance/functional defect without an exploitable attack vector.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n1. Opening a new tab and going to a site works immediately\n2. Going to a different site in the same tab causes a ~30 second delay\n3. This is a recent problem on one PC\n4. The user plans to repair the install to see if it fixes the problem\n\nKey observations:\n- This is about a delay/slowness when navigating to a new URL in an existing tab\n- There's no mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, or any memory-related issues\n- There's no mention of information disclosure or confidentiality breach\n- There's no mention of privilege escalation or sandbox escape\n- There's no mention of cross-context issues or javascript: URLs\n- The delay could be caused by many things - browser extensions, network configuration, DNS issues, corrupted browser profile, etc.\n\nIs this a Denial of Service (DoS)?\n- DoS in security context typically means an attacker can cause the application to become unavailable through exploitation\n- This appears to be a performance/functional issue, not an exploitable DoS condition\n- The delay happens during normal user navigation, not through a malicious attack vector\n- There's no indication that this is caused by resource exhaustion that an attacker could trigger\n\nThis seems like a functional/performance bug - a UI responsiveness issue where navigating in an existing tab is slow. This is a general application performance issue, not a security vulnerability.\n\nBased on the classification criteria:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): \"general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact\" and \"generic instability\"\n- Class 1 (Security Bug): requires evidence of \"memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS)\"\n\nThis bug doesn't fit the security criteria. It's a performance/delay issue that lacks exploitable security impact. The delay could be due to many non-security reasons (corrupted cache, extension interference, DNS resolution issues, etc.).\n\nI'll classify this as a Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 18917454475, "prompt_eval_count": 489, "eval_count": 690}}, {"id": "6642", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 6642 : Marquee not working 2 problem? 1. Just run the above mentioned URL in Google Chrome. 2. 3.  ? In the  quot;Featured Companies quot; section  the white box is supposed to have company logos running across as marquee. But none of them come up.  ? Nothing happens.     \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:17:23.037222233Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let me analyze this bug report step-by-step.\n\n**Bug Description:**\n- The issue is about a \"marquee\" not working in the \"Featured Companies\" section\n- Company logos are supposed to scroll across as a marquee, but they don't appear\n- The observation is simply \"Nothing happens\" - a functional failure\n\n**Analysis:**\n1. This is a **visual display/rendering issue** - the marquee animation isn't displaying logos as expected\n2. There are no indicators of security relevance:\n   - No memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap issues\n   - No information disclosure or confidentiality breach\n   - No denial of service or availability concern beyond a visual feature not working\n   - No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n   - No injection, redirect, or cross-context issues\n3. The keywords here relate to UI/UX functionality: \"marquee,\" \"logos running across,\" visual display of content\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThis is clearly a UI/UX rendering p