ars\" relate to user interface functionality\n- No security impact is mentioned\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug) indicators:**\n- No memory corruption, buffer overflow, heap overwrite mentioned\n- No information disclosure vulnerability\n- No denial of service (DoS) - the browser doesn't crash or become exhausted\n- No cross-context issues, javascript: exploits, redirects\n- No privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n- No confidentiality, integrity, or availability compromise\n\nThis is purely a functional/UX defect where scrollbars don't appear for large SVG documents. The document is \"obscured\" not \"exposed\" - there's no information disclosure, just inability to view content. There's no crash, no memory issue, no exploitable condition.\n\nThis falls clearly into Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) as it's a UI/UX rendering/layout issue where scrollbars fail to appear for large SVG documents.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 26975368501, "prompt_eval_count": 456, "eval_count": 607}}, {"id": "17858", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 17858 : [http://www.ouchpost.com/ouchpost/classified_work/index_Ouchpost.asp]: CSS rendering is incorrect only in Chrome because pseudo-element  quot;:after quot; 6 problem? 1. Merely browsing to the page and observing the broken layout in Chrome as compared with viewing in Firefox or any other browser.  ? The expected result is precisely how the same page appears in all other browsers (which is CORRECT).  ? Instead  Chrome shows the central div wider than it is supposed to be.     Screen Caps attached. Notice in the Firefox screen cap there is nothing wrong with the layout. That is how it looks in ALL browsers except for Chrome. Now look at the Chrome screen cap. Look at my red arrows pointing to the error in Chrome's rendering of the div. screenCap_Chrome.jpg 447 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download screenCap_Firefox.jpg 486 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:19:20.079065721Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "**Reasoning:**\n\nThis bug report describes a CSS rendering discrepancy between Chrome and other browsers. The key indicators are:\n\n1. **\"CSS rendering is incorrect\"** - explicitly about CSS, a styling/layout technology\n2. **\"pseudo-element ':after'\"** - a CSS feature for visual styling\n3. **\"broken layout\"** and **\"central div wider than it is supposed to be\"** - this is a layout/visual display issue\n4. **Comparison with Firefox** showing correct rendering - standard cross-browser rendering difference\n\nThe domain knowledge clearly states that Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) encompasses \"UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems\" with distinctive indicators including keywords like \"render\", \"CSS\", and \"layout.\"\n\nThere is no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, DoS, or any other security-relevant impact. The issue is purely about visual appearance - a div being wider than expected in one browser compared to others.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about:\n- A CSS rendering issue on a specific website\n- The `:after` pseudo-element causing layout problems\n- Chrome rendering a div wider than it should be\n- The layout appears correct in other browsers (Firefox, etc.)\n\nKey indicators from the report:\n- \"CSS rendering is incorrect\"\n- \"pseudo-element\"\n- \"broken layout\"\n- \"Chrome shows the central div wider than it is supposed to be\"\n- This is purely a visual/layout rendering difference between browsers\n\nAccording to my domain knowledge:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) includes \"UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems\" with distinctive indicators including keywords like \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"\n- Class 1 (Security Bug) involves vulnerabilities compromising confidentiality, integrity, or availability\n\nThis report is clearly about a CSS rendering difference between browsers - a layout/visual display issue. There is no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, DoS, privilege escalation, or any other security-relevant impact.\n\nThis is definitely a Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 22438907028, "prompt_eval_count": 531, "eval_count": 434}}, {"id": "19727", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 19727 : Carpet-bombing dialog needs informative text  per Apple HIG 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   pinkerton@chromium.org    - go to http://skypher.com/SkyLined/Repro/Chrome/carpet bombing/repro.html - see carpet-bombing dialog expected: Dialog should have multiple lines of explanation  including a message text and supplemental informative text  per the Apple HIG. Actual: Dialog is missing the informative text. See http://developer.apple.com/documentation/userexperience/Conceptual/A ppleHIGuidelines/XHIGWindows/XHIGWindows.html#//apple_ref/doc/uid/2 0000961-BACFBACB The HIG stresses  quot;Important: Do not leave out the informative text. What you think of as an intuitive alert message might be far from intuitive to your users. Use informative text to reword and expand on the alert message text. quot; \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:19:24.578175048Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze what the actual issue is requesting.\n\nThe report describes:\n1. A \"carpet-bombing dialog\" that appears when visiting a specific URL\n2. The dialog is missing \"informative text\" per Apple's Human Interface Guidelines (HIG)\n3. The request is for the dialog to include \"multiple lines of explanation\" and \"supplemental informative text\"\n\nWhile \"carpet-bombing\" is a security-related concept (automatic downloads that can overwhelm a user), the actual bug being reported is **not about the vulnerability itself**. The security dialog already exists and functions - it warns users about carpet-bombing attempts.\n\nThe specific issue is a **UI/UX deficiency**: the dialog lacks explanatory text that Apple's design guidelines recommend. The request is to improve user communication through better text in the dialog, following Apple HIG standards.\n\nKey indicators this is a Non-Security Bug:\n- The focus is on missing \"informative text\" - a UX/content issue\n- References Apple HIG (Human Interface Guidelines) - design standards\n- The underlying security mechanism (warning dialog) already works\n- Keywords like \"informative text\", \"explanation\", and \"intuitive alert message\" are UX-focused\n\nThis is essentially a feature request to improve dialog clarity, not a report of a security vulnerability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about a \"Carpet-bombing dialog\" needing informative text per Apple HIG (Human Interface Guidelines).\n\nThe issue describes:\n1. Going to a specific URL that triggers a carpet-bombing dialog\n2. The dialog is missing informative text that should explain what's happening\n3. The reporter references Apple's Human Interface Guidelines which stresses the importance of informative text in alert dialogs\n\nNow, let me consider the context:\n\n\"Carpet-bombing\" in the context of browsers refers to a type of attack where a malicious website automatically downloads multiple files without user consent, potentially filling up the user's desktop or downloads folder. This is related to a security vulnerability - specifically, it's a form of attack that could lead to denial of service or user annoyance/manipulation.\n\nHowever, the specific issue being reported here is NOT about the carpet-bombing vulnerability itself. The issue is about the UI/UX of the warning dialog - specifically that it's missing \"informative text\" as per Apple's Human Interface Guidelines. The reporter wants the dialog to be more informative to users.\n\nThis is essentially a UX/UI improvement request - making a dialog more user-friendly by adding explanatory text. The underlying security mechanism (the dialog that warns users) already exists and functions. The bug is about improving the communication to users through better text in the dialog.\n\nKeywords in this report:\n- \"dialog needs informative text\" - UI/UX concern\n- \"per Apple HIG\" - Human Interface Guidelines reference, UI standard\n- \"multiple lines of explanation\" - UX improvement\n- \"intuitive alert message\" - UX terminology\n\nThis is a Non-Security Bug. While it relates to a security feature (a dialog that warns about carpet-bombing), the actual bug being reported is about the UX of the dialog - specifically that it needs better explanatory text according to Apple's design guidelines. The security functionality (warning users) exists; the improvement requested is about user experience and compliance with design standards.\n\nThis falls under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) because:\n- It's about UI/UX improvement (missing informative text in a dialog)\n- It's about compliance with design guidelines (Apple HIG)\n- It's not about a vulnerability being exploited or security mechanisms being bypassed\n- The underlying security feature (the warning dialog) already works\n\nI should classify this as a Non-Security Bug (A)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 39397988624, "prompt_eval_count": 530, "eval_count": 788}}, {"id": "1018", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 1018 : Unable to play parking war at facebook 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   lixasafa      : 0.2.149.27 URLs (if applicable) : http://apps.new.facebook.com/parkingwars/profile.php?id=438775    : Safari 3: Firefox 3: OK IE 7: OK  ? 1. when try to click at any of empty parking lot 2. 3.  ? will get prompt to choose which car to be moved  ? nothing happen     \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:19:34.085388994Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze it for security relevance.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe report describes:\n- A Facebook game (\"Parking War\") that doesn't function properly\n- When clicking an empty parking lot, the user is prompted to choose a car, but then \"nothing happens\"\n- The issue appears browser-specific (Safari 3 has the problem, Firefox 3 and IE 7 work fine)\n\n**Checking against classification criteria:**\n\n- **No memory corruption indicators**: No mention of buffer overflow, heap overwrite, or memory leaks\n- **No confidentiality/integrity/availability compromise**: This is a game feature not working, not an exploitable vulnerability\n- **No DoS context**: The \"nothing happens\" is about a UI interaction failing, not resource exhaustion\n- **No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass**: No indication of cross-context issues\n- **No security-related keywords**: No \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory,\" or similar terms\n- **Functional defect**: This is a classic browser compatibility issue where a game mechanic fails to execute\n\nThis is a straightforward functional bug - a game interaction doesn't produce the expected result in a specific browser. There is no evidence of any security vulnerability or exploitable condition.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Issue 1018: Unable to play parking war at Facebook\n- URL: http://apps.new.facebook.com/parkingwars/profile.php?id=438775\n- Browser: Safari 3 (Firefox 3: OK, IE 7: OK)\n- Steps to reproduce: Click on an empty parking lot, get prompted to choose which car to be moved\n- Expected behavior: (not clearly stated)\n- Actual behavior: Nothing happens\n\nThis is clearly a functional bug where a Facebook game (Parking Wars) is not working properly in Safari 3 browser. The user clicks on an empty parking lot, gets prompted to choose a car, but then nothing happens.\n\nLet me check against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- This is a functional defect - the game feature doesn't work\n- No security impact - just a game not functioning properly\n- Browser compatibility issue (works in Firefox and IE, not in Safari)\n- No keywords related to security vulnerabilities\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- No memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap overwrite mentioned\n- No information disclosure\n- No DoS attack vector\n- No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n- No \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", or other security keywords\n- No cross-context issues\n\nThis is clearly a non-security bug. It's a functional issue with a Facebook game where a click action doesn't produce the expected result in a specific browser. There's no indication of any security vulnerability - it's simply a game feature that doesn't work properly in Safari 3.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 31786284393, "prompt_eval_count": 445, "eval_count": 622}}, {"id": "4653", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 4653 : AJAX page not working correctly  lsaquo; Prev 384 of 14150 Next  rsaquo; 3 problem? 1. Go to http://myhouseweb.net/fsbo/listingDisplay.php?listingid=3 2. Click on  quot;Next  gt; gt; quot; a few times What is the expected output? There are 3 pictures on this page. The AJAX/Javascript code should cycle through all of them when  quot;Next  gt; gt; quot; is clicked multiple times. What do you see instead? It's not cycling past the 2nd picture. Compare to the way IE and FF cycle correctly through the pictures. = gt;Safari has same problem as Chrome. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:19:38.840079705Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze the nature of the issue described.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- An AJAX/JavaScript photo cycling feature not working correctly\n- Clicking \"Next >>\" doesn't cycle past the 2nd picture out of 3\n- This is a browser compatibility issue - works in IE and FF, but fails in Chrome and Safari\n\n**Key observations:**\n1. This is a **functional defect** - a feature (photo cycling) doesn't work as expected\n2. The issue is **browser compatibility** - JavaScript/AJAX behaves differently across browsers\n3. There is **no mention** of any security-relevant keywords or patterns (no memory corruption, no information disclosure, no privilege escalation, no DoS, no buffer overflow, no sandbox bypass)\n4. This is purely a **UI/UX rendering issue** where a navigation feature fails to cycle through all available content\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nThis is a standard functional bug related to JavaScript/AJAX browser compatibility. There is no evidence whatsoever of a security vulnerability - no confidentiality, integrity, or availability compromise is indicated. The bug simply prevents users from viewing the third picture, which is a usability issue, not a security issue.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- An AJAX page not working correctly\n- A photo gallery/cycling feature where clicking \"Next >>\" should cycle through 3 pictures\n- The issue is that it's not cycling past the 2nd picture\n- This is a browser compatibility issue (works in IE and FF, but not in Chrome and Safari)\n\nThis is clearly a functional/UI bug related to JavaScript/AJAX not working correctly in certain browsers (Chrome and Safari). There's no indication of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Privilege escalation\n- Sandbox bypass\n- Any security vulnerability\n\nThis is a browser compatibility issue with AJAX/JavaScript that causes a feature (picture cycling) to not work as expected. This falls squarely into the \"Non-Security Bug\" category as it's a functional defect without any exploitable security impact.\n\nThe classification should be Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 28061395137, "prompt_eval_count": 482, "eval_count": 469}}, {"id": "2194", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 2194 : Incompatible with Gameguard (gaming protect system) 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   ntcong.it      : 0.2.149.27 URLs (if applicable) : n/a    : Safari 3: OK Firefox 3: OK IE 7: OK  ? 1. Run and online game that have Gameguard (like Mapleglobal.com  gunbound.ijji.com or trickster.ntreev.net) 2. Open a New tab or change the URL of the current tab. 3.  ? The page load normally.  ? The Aw  Snap! page display says  quot;Something went wrong went display this page... quot; If you try change the URL  a blank page will load with no loading. Click on a link in current page (load before the game start) that open a new tab or change the url of the current tab  it works fine. Maybe the problem only because of the Adress bar. GG_bugs.jpg 56.0 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:19:53.439914874Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether it describes a security vulnerability or a functional/compatibility issue.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe report describes an incompatibility between the browser and Gameguard (a gaming anti-cheat/protection system). When running a game with Gameguard and then trying to navigate via the address bar or open a new tab, the browser displays an \"Aw Snap!\" error page or a blank page.\n\n**Key observations:**\n1. The \"Aw Snap!\" error is Chrome's generic renderer crash page - this indicates a stability issue, not necessarily a security vulnerability\n2. The report describes functional failure (pages not loading) rather than security compromise\n3. No mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or sandbox bypass\n4. While Gameguard is a security-related software, the bug is about *compatibility* with it, not about a vulnerability being exploited\n5. The crash appears to be a generic instability issue without context of memory corruption or exhaustion\n\n**Classification reasoning:**\n- This is fundamentally a compatibility/crash issue between the browser and third-party software\n- The crash described falls under \"generic instability (e.g., 'crash' without memory corruption or exhaustion context)\" which maps to Class 0\n- There is no clear evidence of security impact (confidentiality, integrity, or availability compromise through exploitable means)\n- The guidance states to heavily penalize classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe issue is about incompatibility with \"Gameguard\" (a gaming protection system). Here's what the report describes:\n\n1. Run an online game that has Gameguard (like MapleGlobal, Gunbound, or Trickster)\n2. Open a new tab or change the URL of the current tab\n3. Result: \"Aw Snap!\" page displays (Chrome's crash/error page) saying \"Something went wrong when displaying this page\"\n4. If you try to change the URL, a blank page will load with no loading\n5. Clicking on a link in the current page that opens a new tab works fine\n6. The problem might only be with the Address bar\n\nThis appears to be a compatibility issue between Chrome and Gameguard (a gaming anti-cheat/protection system). Gameguard is known to hook into various system processes and can conflict with browsers.\n\nLet me consider whether this is a security bug or non-security bug:\n\nArguments for Security Bug:\n- Gameguard is a security/protection system\n- The incompatibility could indicate that Chrome's security boundaries are being affected\n- Gameguard's hooks could be bypassing or interfering with browser security mechanisms\n\nArguments for Non-Security Bug:\n- This is fundamentally a compatibility issue\n- The \"Aw Snap!\" error is a generic renderer crash\n- The bug description is about functionality not working (pages not loading)\n- There's no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or other security vulnerabilities\n- The user is just experiencing a crash/incompatibility, not an exploitable condition\n\nLooking at the classification guidelines:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Includes \"generic instability (e.g., 'crash' without memory corruption or exhaustion context)\"\n- Class 1 (Security Bug): Requires evidence of \"vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability\"\n\nWhile Gameguard is a security system, the bug report is about a compatibility/crash issue, not about a security vulnerability being exploited. The \"Aw Snap!\" page is Chrome's standard error page for renderer crashes, which is a stability issue, not necessarily a security one.\n\nThe report doesn't describe:\n- Memory corruption exploits\n- Information disclosure\n- Privilege escalation\n- Sandbox bypass\n- Any exploitable condition\n\nIt's describing a crash/incompatibility that prevents normal page loading. This is a functional defect/compatibility issue.\n\nI should classify this as a Non-Security Bug (Class 0) because:\n1. It's primarily a compatibility issue with third-party software\n2. The crash is generic (no memory corruption or exhaustion context mentioned)\n3. There's no evidence of security exploitation or vulnerability\n4. The impact is on functionality (pages not loading), not on security properties\n\nFollowing the instruction to \"heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance,\" I should classify this as Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 19148016137, "prompt_eval_count": 559, "eval_count": 913}}, {"id": "11757", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 11757 : Crash in BrowserThemeProvider if browser is torn down after tab is dragged out 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   glen@chromium.org    http://build.chromium.org/buildbot/waterfall/builders/Chromium%20Reliability/ builds/2725/steps/reliability:%20complete%20result%20of%20previous%20build/lo gs/stdio \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:20:07.402378118Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether the crash has security implications.\n\n**Key observations:**\n1. The bug is described as a \"Crash in BrowserThemeProvider if browser is torn down after tab is dragged out\"\n2. This is a crash during browser teardown/cleanup after a specific user action (dragging a tab out)\n3. The component involved is \"BrowserThemeProvider\" - a UI/theming component\n4. The description only mentions \"Crash\" without any specific memory corruption terminology\n\n**Analysis:**\n- The crash occurs during a teardown sequence, which is typically a lifecycle/ordering issue rather than an exploitable memory corruption vulnerability\n- There's no mention of keywords indicating security relevance such as \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"use-after-free\", \"memory corruption\", or any exploitation potential\n- BrowserThemeProvider is a UI component dealing with theming/styling, not a security-sensitive component\n- A generic crash during shutdown sequences is commonly a null pointer dereference or similar stability issue, not necessarily exploitable\n\nWithout clear evidence of memory corruption, privilege escalation, information disclosure, or other security impact, this appears to be a general stability/functional bug rather than a security vulnerability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is:\n\"Issue 11757 : Crash in BrowserThemeProvider if browser is torn down after tab is dragged out\"\n\nKey elements:\n1. \"Crash\" - This is a crash, but I need to determine if it has security implications\n2. \"BrowserThemeProvider\" - This is related to the browser theme\n3. \"browser is torn down after tab is dragged out\" - This describes a teardown/shutdown scenario\n\nLet me consider the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context - This seems like a generic crash\n- No mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, heap overwrite, etc.\n- This appears to be a teardown order issue - when a tab is dragged out and the browser is torn down, there's a crash\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- Could a crash during teardown be exploitable? Potentially, but there's no evidence of memory corruption mentioned\n- No keywords like \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", or privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n\nThe bug report describes a crash that happens during browser teardown after a tab is dragged out. This sounds like a use-after-free or null pointer dereference during cleanup, which could potentially have security implications if it involves memory corruption.\n\nHowever, the expert knowledge states I should heavily penalize classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. The report just says \"Crash\" without specifying memory corruption or any exploitable condition.\n\nOn the other hand, crashes during teardown can sometimes be use-after-free bugs, which are security vulnerabilities. But without more detail about memory corruption, I should lean toward the safer classification as per the instructions.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. The instructions say \"When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\" In cybersecurity context, the \"safer\" option would be to classify something as a security bug if there's any doubt, because missing a security vulnerability is more dangerous than false positives.\n\nBut the instructions also say to \"heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance.\"\n\nThis is a crash during browser teardown. It could be:\n1. A null pointer dereference (often not security-relevant)\n2. A use-after-free (potentially security-relevant)\n\nWithout clear evidence of memory corruption or security impact, this seems like a general stability bug. The crash is in BrowserThemeProvider which is related to UI/theming, not a security-sensitive component.\n\nI'll classify this as a Non-Security Bug because:\n- It's described as just a \"Crash\" without mention of memory corruption\n- It involves BrowserThemeProvider (UI/theme component)\n- It's a teardown scenario that's more likely a race condition or null deref\n- No security keywords or indicators present\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 42529283696, "prompt_eval_count": 419, "eval_count": 870}}, {"id": "20788", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Secur