n crashes when performing a routine operation\n6. The crash doesn't appear to be exploitable or involve memory corruption based on the description\n7. The criteria specifically mentions \"crash without memory corruption or exhaustion context\" as an indicator of Class 0\n\nWhile a crash could theoretically be related to a security vulnerability (like a DoS), the description here is very generic - just \"Google chrome crashes\" with no details about memory corruption, buffer overflows, or any exploitable condition. The user is performing a normal UI action and the browser crashes.\n\nAccording to the expert knowledge, \"crash without memory corruption or exhaustion context\" is classified as Class 0 (Non-Security Bug).\n\nThis is a generic application crash without any clear evidence of security relevance. There's no indication of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any exploitable vulnerability.\n\nTherefore, this should be classified as a Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 12168086184, "prompt_eval_count": 442, "eval_count": 765}}, {"id": "14300", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromi