ograms/plugins and use it on Chrome Linux browser. So advanced users could choose use for example flash from Wine or the one installed on Linux. But I requested this feature thinking in one thing: Recently was released a program called Bhuvan: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bhuvan I really would like to use it  but to run the program is necessary Windows/Directx.. As I can see the problem doesn't work with Wine yet: http://bugs.winehq.org/show_bug.cgi?id=19754 . But I would like to know if the program worked with Wine  could be possible to run this on Chrome changing Chrome user agent to IE? \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:24:11.74173625Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\n**Key observations from the report:**\n\n1. The title explicitly states \"Feature request\" - this is a user requesting new functionality\n2. The user is asking whether Chrome on Linux could integrate with Wine to scan for and run Windows programs/plugins\n3. The specific use case is running a program called Bhuvan that requires Windows/DirectX\n4. The user also asks about changing Chrome's user agent to IE for compatibility\n\n**Security analysis:**\n\n- No mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap issues\n- No information disclosure vulnerability described\n- No denial of service or resource exhaustion concerns\n- No sandbox bypass or privilege escalation mentioned\n- The user agent change suggestion is for compatibility, not for security bypass\n\n**Classification reasoning:**\n\nThis report is explicitly a **feature request** - one of the clear indicators listed under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug). The user is suggesting new functionality (Wine integration for running Windows programs/plugins in Chrome), not reporting a security vulnerability. While running Windows programs through Wine in a browser could theoretically raise security concerns, the report itself doesn't identify or describe any vulnerability - it's simply asking for a new feature.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is titled \"Feature request: Chrome could look and run programs or plugins installed with Wine\"\n\nKey points from the report:\n1. This is explicitly labeled as a \"Feature request\"\n2. The user is asking if Chrome on Linux could look at Wine directories and scan for programs/plugins to use in the Chrome browser\n3. The specific use case mentioned is running programs like Bhuvan that require Windows/DirectX through Wine\n4. The user also asks about changing the Chrome user agent to IE\n\nThis is clearly a feature request, not a bug report about a security vulnerability. The user is suggesting:\n- A new feature to integrate Wine programs/plugins with Chrome\n- The ability to change the user agent\n\nThere's no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Any exploitable vulnerability\n\nThe keywords and content align with Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) which includes \"feature requests\" as a distinctive indicator.\n\nWhile changing user agents could have privacy implications in some contexts, this is presented as a feature request for compatibility purposes, not as a security vulnerability.\n\nThis is clearly a non-security bug (specifically a feature request)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 27086589784, "prompt_eval_count": 595, "eval_count": 513}}, {"id": "2812", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for sec