Class 0 indicator)\n2. It involves CSS rendering (white-space, pre-wrap are CSS properties)\n3. There's no indication of memory corruption, information disclosure, DoS, or any security vulnerability\n4. It's a visual display issue with RTL text rendering\n\nThere is no security relevance here - this is purely a rendering/layout test failure.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 8535987185, "prompt_eval_count": 446, "eval_count": 631}}, {"id": "8097", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 8097 : Chrome does not support FIOS settings  suffers 60% performance penalty  lsaquo; Prev 6262 of 14148 Next  rsaquo; 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   manfmnantucket    Chrome Version : 1.0.154.48 (ALL) URLs (if applicable) :    : Firefox 3: OK IE 7: OK  ? 1. From a FIOS 20/5 MB/s connection  invoke speedtest.net in IE  FF  and Chrome 2. Do speedtest on all 3. Chrome will be 60% slower than IE and FF which yield rated bandwidth. IE and FF are supported by FIOS Optimizer but Chrome tweaks not yet given/known. 3. To fix  go to quickfios.com and run optimizer on IE  see what tweaks it makes.  ?  ?     \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:25:09.6799479Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to assess whether it describes a security vulnerability or a non-security issue.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe report describes a performance issue where Chrome runs 60% slower than IE and Firefox on FIOS (Fiber Optic) internet connections. The root cause identified is that the FIOS Optimizer tool applies network tweaks to IE and Firefox but not to Chrome, since Chrome-specific tweaks are not yet known/supported.\n\n**Key observations:**\n- This is a **network performance/compatibility issue** - Chrome lacks specific TCP/network optimizations that FIOS provides for other browsers\n- The suggestion is essentially a **feature request** to understand and implement FIOS optimizer tweaks for Chrome\n- There is **no mention** of memory corruption, buffer overflow, information disclosure, privilege escalation, sandbox bypass, or any exploitable condition\n- The \"60% performance penalty\" is about network throughput speed, not a Denial of Service vulnerability\n- No security keywords or patterns are present (no \"memory leak\", \"overflow\", \"disclosure\", \"bypass\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", etc.)\n\nThis is a straightforward performance and compatibility issue - Chrome needs to support FIOS-specific network settings. While performance is impacted, this is a functional/optimization concern rather than a security vulnerability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe issue is about Chrome not supporting FIOS settings, resulting in a 60% performance penalty compared to IE and Firefox when running speed tests on a FIOS connection.\n\nKey points from the report:\n1. Chrome is slower (60%) than IE and Firefox on FIOS connections\n2. The issue is related to FIOS Optimizer tweaks that are applied to IE but not Chrome\n3. The suggestion is to look at what tweaks the FIOS optimizer makes on IE\n\nThis is clearly a performance/network configuration issue - Chrome doesn't have the same network optimization settings that FIOS provides for IE and Firefox. This is a feature request or compatibility issue, not a security vulnerability.\n\nThere's no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service (in the security sense)\n- Privilege escalation\n- Sandbox bypass\n- Any exploitable condition\n\nThis is simply a network performance issue where Chrome doesn't benefit from FIOS-specific optimizations. It's a functional/compatibility issue, not a security bug.\n\nClassification: Non-Security Bug (Class 0)"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 6090101120, "prompt_eval_count": 520, "eval_count": 510}}, {"id": "15801", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnera