esn't work well 3   Back to list Signature in Gmail Doesn #39;t Behave the same as other browsers when changing directionality\" >issue 4443 Owner: nbsp; all-bugs-test@chromium.org Closed: nbsp; Dec 2008 Cc: nbsp; jshin@chromium.org   idana@chromium.org   karen@chromium.org   xji@chromium.org   c...@chromium.org   vivi...@chromium.org Type- Bug Pri- 2 OS- All Area- Compat I18N RTL Sign in to add a comment  nbsp;   xlyuan@chromium.org    Spun off issue 3830 Build: 0.3.155.0 (Developer Build 5572) OS: Any Other Browsers: Safari: Fail Firefox3: OK IE7: OK Steps: 1. Launch Chrome 2. Open Gmail 3. Switch Gmail UI language to Hebrew 4. Add some Hebrew Signature from  quot;Settings quot; page 5. Compose a new email 6. Type some Hebrew content 7. Insert the cursor into the middle of the text 8. Kit  quot;Home quot; key 9. Observe Result: It goes to the end of the line (=Left) Expected: Should go to the beginning of the line (=Right) Notes: Note reproduce if remove the signature \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:32:09.432264688Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\n**Bug Summary:**\nThe issue describes a problem where the \"Home\" key doesn't work correctly when Gmail has a signature in an RTL (Right-to-Left) language like Hebrew. When the cursor is in the middle of Hebrew text and the user presses \"Home,\" the cursor goes to the left (which is the end of the line in RTL context) instead of the right (which is the beginning of the line in RTL context).\n\n**Key Observations:**\n1. This is a keyboard navigation behavior issue in the context of RTL (internationalization) support\n2. The bug is categorized as \"Compat I18N RTL\" in the report itself\n3. It's about cursor movement behavior (user experience/shortcut functionality)\n4. There is no mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any exploitable condition\n5. No security-related keywords are present (no \"memory leak\", \"overflow\", \"disclosure\", \"sandbox bypass\", etc.)\n\n**Classification Analysis:**\n- This is a **functional defect** related to how the \"Home\" key behaves in RTL language contexts\n- It falls under UI/UX behavior (keyboard navigation/shortcut behavior)\n- It's an internationalization compatibility issue between browsers\n- There is absolutely no security impact - this doesn't compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability\n\nThis clearly falls under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) as a functional/UI defect related to user experience and internationalization.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug is about the \"Home\" key not working correctly when Gmail has a signature in RTL (Right-to-Left) language. Specifically:\n- When typing Hebrew content (RTL language) in Gmail with a Hebrew signature\n- Pressing the \"Home\" key moves the cursor to the wrong end of the line\n- It goes to the left (end of line in RTL context) instead of the right (beginning of line in RTL context)\n\nThis is clearly a UI/UX behavior issue related to:\n1. Keyboard navigation behavior\n2. RTL (Right-to-Left) language support\n3. Compatibility with other browsers\n\nThere is no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Information disclosure\n- Privilege escalation\n- Denial of Service\n- Any security vulnerability\n\nThis is a functional bug related to keyboard behavior in RTL language contexts. It's about user experience and internationalization (i18n) compatibility.\n\nBased on the domain knowledge provided:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability.\n\nThe \"Home\" key behavior is a user experience/shortcut issue, which falls under Class 0.\n\nThis is clearly a Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 7544410092, "prompt_eval_count": 640, "eval_count": 637}}, {"id": "14378", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 14378 : [www.bayernfanclub.ch]: Top banner not showing 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes. Back to list Status: nbsp; WontFix Owner: nbsp; ---- Closed: nbsp; Jul 2009 Cc: nbsp; karen@chromium.org Type- Bug Pri- 2 OS- All Finditbug Area- Compat  nbsp;   findit.role    URL: http://www.bayernfanclub.ch/ Browser: Chrome 2.0.172.31 [Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US) AppleWebKit/530.5 (KHTML  like Gecko) Chrome/2.0.172.31 Safari/530.5] Severity: Cosmetic problem What is wrong? the top banner is not showing up. it is supposed that it is showing various pictures and the site title  ? banner showing up  ? 1. go to http://www.bayernfanclub.ch/ 2. 3. Does it work properly in other browsers? Which did you test? FF3: OK Safari4: OK IE6: OK Chrome: NOK   bm-ff.zip 358 KB  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:32:12.437205282Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step:\n\n**Key Details from the Bug Report:**\n- Issue: Top banner not showing on www.bayernfanclub.ch\n- Severity: **\"Cosmetic problem\"** (explicitly stated)\n- Area: **Compat** (browser compatibility)\n- Works correctly in: FF3, Safari4, IE6\n- Does NOT work in: Chrome\n- Type: Bug (Pri-2)\n\n**Analysis against Classification Criteria:**\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) Indicators:**\n- This is a **visual display/rendering issue** - the banner is not rendering correctly\n- The severity is explicitly labeled as a \"Cosmetic problem\"\n- Keywords present: visual display issue, rendering difference across browsers\n- This is a browser compatibility issue affecting how content is displayed\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug) Indicators:**\n- No memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap overwrite mentioned\n- No information disclosure or data leakage\n- No Denial of Service (DoS) or resource exhaustion\n- No privilege escalation, sandbox bypass, or cross-context issues\n- No \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", or other security-relevant patterns\n- No exploitable security impact identified\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThis is clearly a cosmetic/visual rendering bug where a banner doesn't display properly in Chrome. It's explicitly marked as a \"Cosmetic problem\" and is a browser compatibility issue. There is zero evidence of any security vulnerability - no confidentiality, integrity, or availability concerns are present.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully to determine if it's a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug.\n\nThe bug report is about:\n- A website (www.bayernfanclub.ch) where the top banner is not showing up\n- The banner is supposed to display various pictures and the site title\n- It's classified as \"Severity: Cosmetic problem\"\n- It works in other browsers (FF3, Safari4, IE6) but not in Chrome\n- The area is \"Compat\" (compatibility)\n- It's marked as \"WontFix\"\n\nNow let me check against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- \"visual display\" issues - YES, this is about a banner not rendering\n- \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\" keywords - This is a rendering/display issue\n- \"Cosmetic problem\" - explicitly stated in the bug report\n- UI/UX rendering issues - this is exactly what this is about\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- Memory corruption, information disclosure, DoS - NO\n- \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\" - NO\n- \"cross-context\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\" - NO\n- Privilege/sandboxing bypasses - NO\n\nThis is clearly a visual display/rendering issue - a cosmetic problem where a banner doesn't show up. There's no security impact whatsoever. It's a browser compatibility issue affecting how content is rendered visually.\n\nThis falls squarely into Class 0 (Non-Security Bug).\n\nThe answer is (A) Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 8455997562, "prompt_eval_count": 591, "eval_count": 656}}, {"id": "21582", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 21582 : Use --ignore-externals for svn co/up in gclient.py  lsaquo; Prev 1516 of 14163 Next  rsaquo; 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   maruelatchromium    Issue: http://protobuf.googlecode.com/svn/trunk/src fetches gtest as an svn:external reference. gclient status gives: ________ running 'svn status' in 'maruel/chrome/src/third_party/protobuf2/src' X gtest which triggers during gclient revert and slows down gclient sync. Solution: Add --ignore-externals to some svn calls. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:32:15.809792256Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to assess whether it describes a security vulnerability or a functional issue.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\n1. **The Issue**: When `gclient` runs SVN operations (status, revert, sync), it processes `svn:external` references (like gtest in protobuf), which causes the \"X\" marker to appear and slows down operations.\n\n2. **The Impact**: The report states this \"slows down gclient sync\" - this is a performance/convenience issue, not a security exploit.\n\n3. **The Proposed Solution**: Add `--ignore-externals` flag to SVN calls - this is a simple functional fix to change behavior, not a security patch.\n\n4. **Security Relevance Check**:\n   - No memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap issues\n   - No information disclosure or confidentiality breach\n   - No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n   - The \"slows down\" mention is about operational inconvenience, not a Denial of Service vulnerability (there's no indication of intentional exhaustion or exploitation)\n   - No cross-context issues, redirects, or injection vectors\n\nThis is clearly a **functional defect** related to how the tool handles SVN externals - it causes unwanted behavior (fetching externals) and performance degradation during normal operations. The fix is a standard feature/behavior adjustment.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe issue is about `gclient.py` and its interaction with SVN (Subversion) externals. \n\nKey points from the report:\n1. When fetching protobuf from SVN, it also fetches gtest as an svn:external reference\n2. `gclient status` shows an \"X\" marker for gtest (indicating it's an external)\n3. This triggers during `gclient revert` and slows down `gclient sync`\n4. The proposed solution is to add `--ignore-externals` to some SVN calls\n\nThis is clearly a functional/behavioral issue:\n- It's about performance (slowing down operations)\n- It's about how the tool handles SVN externals\n- The fix is simply to add a flag (`--ignore-externals`) to SVN commands\n- There's no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any security vulnerability\n- No confidentiality, integrity, or availability compromise is indicated\n- It's essentially a feature request or functional improvement to make gclient faster by ignoring externals\n\nThis is a Non-Security Bug. It's about improving the behavior/performance of a development tool, not about any security vulnerability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 7513161308, "prompt_eval_count": 462, "eval_count": 547}}, {"id": "5869", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 5869 : Middle-Click Close Tab detected on press rather than release 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   bluknight    Chrome Version :  lt;REPLACE this with your version. Ex: 0.3.154.3 gt; URLs (if applicable) :    : Safari 3: ? Firefox 3: OK IE 7: FAIL  ? 1. Press down middle button while cursor is on a tab 2. Drag mouse off 3. Let go of button (or click a different button)  ? The tab should not close. This works with links  if you click a link and drag off it before letting go it does not register the click.  ? The tab closes.     The problem is just that the close event is being triggered by mouse being pressed when it should really be triggered by the mouse button being released. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:32:16.795063412Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether it has security implications.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe bug describes a UI behavior issue where middle-clicking to close a tab triggers on mouse press (mousedown) rather than mouse release (mouseup). The expected behavior is that users should be able to cancel the close action by dragging off the tab before releasing the button - similar to how link clicks work.\n\n**Key observations:**\n- This is a UI/UX behavioral inconsistency issue\n- No memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap-related vulnerabilities mentioned\n- No information disclosure or data exposure risk\n- No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n- No denial of service or resource exhaustion\n- No cross-context attacks, injection points, or redirect issues\n- The bug is purely about when a click event should be registered (press vs. release)\n- This falls under functional defect / UI behavior - inconsistent with standard click conventions\n\nWhile accidentally closing a tab could be inconvenient, this doesn't compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability in a security sense. It's a usability issue about event handling consistency.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Issue: Middle-click close tab is detected on press rather than release\n- Steps to reproduce: \n  1. Press down middle button on a tab\n  2. Drag mouse off the tab\n  3. Release the button\n- Expected behavior: The tab should not close (similar to how links work - if you click a link and drag off before releasing, it doesn't register)\n- Actual behavior: The tab closes\n- Root cause: The close event is triggered by mouse press instead of mouse release\n\nThis is essentially a UI/UX behavior issue. The middle-click action to close a tab fires on the mousedown event instead of the mouseup event. This is inconsistent with standard UI conventions where clicks are typically registered on release (which allows users to cancel an action by moving the mouse away before releasing).\n\nIs this a security bug? Let me consider:\n- It doesn't involve memory corruption, buffer overflow, or any memory-related vulnerabilities\n- It doesn't involve information disclosure\n- It doesn't involve privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n- It doesn't involve cross-context attacks or injection\n- It's not a DoS vulnerability\n- There's no mention of \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", or other security keywords\n\nThis is purely a UI/UX behavioral issue about when a click event should be registered. While it could be annoying (accidentally closing a tab when you didn't mean to), it doesn't compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability in a security sense.\n\nThe bug is about user experience and consistency with standard UI patterns. It's a functional defect in how the click event is handled, not a security vulnerability.\n\nClassification: Non-Security Bug"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 8897722241, "prompt_eval_count": 513, "eval_count": 591}}, {"id": "10102", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 10102 : 1 Failing Layout Tests 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   gwilson@chromium.org    The following layout tests/directories are failing  but are not marked as WONTFIX in test_expectations.txt: This fails because of differences between how CG and Skia tests for a point in a path. See http://code.google.com/p/chromium/issues/detail?id=7465 for details. DEFER WIN LINUX : LayoutTests/fast/canvas/pointInPath.html = FAIL (Automatically generated from line 930 to line 935 of test_expectations.txt) \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:32:17.785855494Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze the key details:\n\n1. **Issue Type**: A failing layout test\n2. **Root Cause**: Differences between CG (Core Graphics) and Skia when testing if a point is in a path\n3. **Affected Test**: `LayoutTests/fast/canvas/pointInPath.html`\n4. **Nature**: A rendering/graphics behavior inconsistency between two graphics backends\n\n**Analysis:**\n- This is about a **layout test failure** - a common type of non-security issue related to rendering consistency\n- The issue involves differences in how two graphics libraries (CG and Skia) implement `pointInPath` - this is a **functional/compatibility defect**\n- There are no indicators of security impact:\n  - No memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap issues\n  - No information disclosure or privilege escalation\n  - No denial of service or sandbox bypass\n  - No exploitable vulnerability pattern\n- The keywords (\"layout tests\", \"canvas\", rendering differences between graphics engines) align with Class 0 indicators like \"render\", \"layout\", and visual display issues\n\nThis is clearly a rendering consistency issue between different graphics backends - a functional defect, not a security vulnerability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report is about:\n1. A failing layout test\n2. It's about differences between how CG (Core Graphics) and Skia test for a point in a path\n3. The specific test is `LayoutTests/fast/canvas/pointInPath.html`\n4. It's a test expectation issue - the test fails on certain platforms (WIN LINUX) but is not marked as WONTFIX\n\nKey indicators:\n- This is a \"layout test\" failure\n- The issue is about rendering/drawing differences between two graphics libraries (CG and Skia)\n- It involves canvas operations - specifically `pointInPath`\n- This is a functional/compatibility issue between different graphics backends\n\nIs this a security bug?\n- No memory corruption mentioned\n- No buffer overflow\n- No information disclosure\n- No privilege escalation\n- No DoS\n- No sandbox bypass\n- It's a rendering consistency issue between different graphics engines\n\nThis is clearly a non-security bug. It's about the difference in behavior between CoreGraphics and Skia when testing if a point is in a path - this is a functional/compatibility issue, not a security vulnerability.\n\nThe keywords here - \"layout tests\", \"canvas\", \"pointInPath\", \"FAIL\" - all point to a functional defect in rendering behavior consistency, which falls under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 8063041726, "prompt_eval_count": 460, "eval_count": 553}}, {"id": "2413", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 2413 : Add option to white-list domains that are allowed to get/set cookies 28      nbsp;   abarth@chromium.org    We should add an option to let the user white-list which domains are allowed to set or read cookies. This privacy feature is useful in preventing lots of random web sites from tracking the user and doesn't have the computability hit of turning cookies off entirely. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:32:24.714491708Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze its nature carefully.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe report is requesting to \"Add option to white-list domains that are allowed to get/set cookies.\" This is clearly a feature request - it's asking for new functionality to be implemented, not reporting an existing vulnerability or exploitable flaw.\n\nKey observations:\n1. **Language indicates feature request**: \"Add option\", \"We should add an option\" - these are proposals for new functionality\n2. **Privacy vs. Security**: While the request mentions preventing tracking (a privacy concern), it's not reporting a vulnerability where cookies are being improperly accessed or leaked. It's requesting a new control mechanism.\n3. **No vulnerability described**: There's no indication of an exploit, bypass, memory corruption, information disclosure, or any active compromise of confidentiality, integrity, or availability.\n4. **Compatibility focus**: The report mentions avoiding \"compatibility hit of turning cookies off entirely\" - this is about usability design, not security remediation.\n\nWhile cookie controls relate to privacy (which overlaps with security), the report itself is a feature request for new functionality, not a report of an existing security vulnerability that needs fixing.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report is about adding an option to white-list domains that are allowed to get/set cookies. This is essentially a feature request for privacy control.\n\nKey points from the report:\n1. It's a feature request (\"Add option\", \"We should add an option\")\n2. The purpose is privacy - preventing random websites from tracking users\n3. It's described as a \"privacy feature\"\n4. It doesn't describe a vulnerability or exploit - it's requesting new functionality\n\nLet me check against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display, user experience (e.g., \"feature request\"), and generic instability.\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nThe report:\n- Is explicitly a feature request (\"Add option to white-list domains\")\n- Mentions \"privacy feature\" - this relates to confidentiality/tracking prevention\n- While privacy is related to security, this is requesting new functionality rather than reporting a vulnerability\n- There's no exploit, no vulnerability being exposed, no memory corruption, no DoS\n\nThe distinction here is:\n- A security vulnerability would be: \"Website X can bypass cookie restrictions and track users\"\n- A feature request is: \"We should add the ability to restrict cookies to certain domains\"\n\nThis is clearly a feature request. Even though it relates to privacy (which has security implications), the bug itself is asking for new functionality to be added, not reporting that existing functionality has a security flaw.\n\nHowever, I should consider: Does this relate to \"confidentiality\" as mentioned in Class 1? Cookie tracking does relate to confidentiality of user data/browsing habits. But the report is not describing a vulnerability - it's requesting a new feature that would enhance privacy.\n\nThe key phrase in the classification is \"lack exploitable security impact.\" This feature request is about adding new privacy controls, not about fixing a vulnerability where cookies are being leaked or where there's an exploit.\n\nI'll classify this as Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) because:\n1. It's explicitly a feature request\n2. It doesn't describe a vulnerability or exploit\n3. It's about adding new functionality, not fixing a security flaw\n4. While related to privacy, it's not reporting a breach of confidentiality but rather requesting new controls"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 11971869889, "prompt_eval_count": 408, "eval_count": 838}}, {"id": "7893", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 7893 : incognito feature makes chrome forget what tabs were open last time 2      nbsp;   vishalchandra    Chrome Version :  lt;REPLACE this with your version. Ex: 0.3.154.3 gt; chrome has two features..... one is the incognito window the other feature is to remember the tabs which were open last time when u restart the browser these two features work independently but if I open an incognito window and close my regular window with all the tabs.... and after that I close the incognito window... next time when I restart chrome it forgets what tabs were open in the regular window so basically whenever the regular window is closed chrome should make a record of the tabs that are being closed... rather than making a record only when chrome is being shut down.... \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:32:25.400720533Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "## Analysis\n\nThis bug report describes a functional interaction issue between two Chrome features:\n\n1. **Incognito mode** - private browsing window\n2. **Session restore** - remembering tabs that were open when the browser was last closed\n\n**The Problem:** When a user has both regular and incognito windows open, if they close the regular window first and then the incognito window, Chrome forgets the tabs from the regular window. The user suggests Chrome should save the tab state when the regular window is closed, rather than only when Chrome shuts down.\n\n**Security Assessment:**\n- This is a **usability/functional defect** - the session restore feature doesn't work correctly in a specific window-closing sequence\n- No memory corruption, buffer overflow, or code execution vulnerabilities are mentioned\n- No information disclosure is occurring - in fact, the bug causes data (tab state) to be **lost**, not leaked\n- No privilege escalation or sandbox escape is involved\n- No denial of service (the browser continues to function)\n- The incognito feature is mentioned, but the issue is that regular tabs are forgotten (not that incognito data is improperly persisted, which would be a security concern)\n\nThe issue is simply that two features don't interact as the user expects - a classic functional bug related to session state management.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe issue describes a problem with Chrome's incognito feature and the \"remember tabs\" feature:\n\n1. Chrome has a feature to remember what tabs were open last time when you restart the browser\n2. Chrome also has an incognito window feature\n3. The problem: If a user opens an incognito window, closes their regular window (with all its tabs), and then closes the incognito window, Chrome forgets what tabs were open in the regular window\n4. The suggested fix: Chrome should record the tabs being closed when the regular window is closed, rather than only recording tabs when Chrome is being shut down\n\nThis is essentially a functional bug - the interaction between two features (incognito and session restore) doesn't work correctly. The user expects their regular tabs to be remembered even if they close the regular window before the incognito window.\n\nLet me check if this has any security implications:\n- This is about session/tab persistence behavior\n- It's about the \"remember tabs\" feature not working correctly in a specific scenario\n- There's no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any exploitable vulnerability\n- The incognito feature is involved, but the bug is about regular tabs being forgotten, not about incognito data being leaked or persisted (which would be a security issue)\n- This is essentially a UX/functional issue where the user's expected behavior (remembering regular tabs) isn't happening\n\nThis doesn't appear to be a security bug. It's a functional defect where the session restore feature doesn't work correctly when incognito windows are involved. The issue is about convenience (remembering tabs) rather than security (no data is being exposed or corrupted).\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 9421337211, "prompt_eval_count": 493, "eval_count": 641}}, {"id": "5088", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 5088 : shockwave plug in crash 5 problem? 1. when ever i try to watch videos on youtube 2. more than one video at sametime 3.  ?  ?     \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:32:25.668047913Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze it carefully:\n\n**Bug Report Analysis:**\n- The issue describes a \"shockwave plug in crash 5 problem\"\n- Crashes occur when watching YouTube videos\n- Crashes occur when watching multiple videos simultaneously\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n1. The report mentions \"crash\" but provides no context of memory corruption, buffer overflow, heap overwrite, or other security-relevant memory issues\n2. There's no mention of exploitation potential, privilege escalation, or information disclosure\n3. No indicators of DoS in a security sense (resource exhaustion that an attacker could trigger)\n4. The crash appears to be a general stability/compatibility issue with the Shockwave plugin under normal usage conditions\n\n**Classification Logic:**\nPer the expert knowledge, \"generic instability (e.g., 'crash' without memory corruption or exhaustion context)\" falls under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug). This crash report lacks any security-specific indicators - it's describing a functional failure of the plugin during standard video playback, not an exploitable vulnerability.\n\nWhile plugin crashes *can* sometimes be security-relevant (if they involve memory corruption), this report provides no evidence of such. It's a straightforward stability complaint.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Issue 5088: shockwave plug in crash 5 problem\n- The user is experiencing crashes when:\n  1. Trying to watch videos on YouTube\n  2. Watching more than one video at the same time\n\nThis appears to be a plugin crash related to Shockwave/Flash player. Let me consider the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context\n- Generic instability\n- Functional defect\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Heap overwrite\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service (DoS)\n- Memory leak\n\nThe report mentions a \"crash\" but doesn't provide any context of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Heap overwrite\n- Exploitable conditions\n- Memory exhaustion/leak\n- Any security impact\n\nThis seems like a general application crash - the plugin crashes when watching videos or multiple videos. There's no indication of:\n- Exploitable memory corruption\n- Security vulnerability\n- Privilege escalation\n- Information disclosure\n- DoS (in the sense of an attacker being able to trigger it maliciously)\n\nThe crash described is a functional issue where the plugin fails under normal usage conditions (watching videos). This is a stability/compatibility issue rather than a security vulnerability.\n\nAccording to the guidelines: \"generic instability (e.g., 'crash' without memory corruption or exhaustion context)\" falls under Class 0.\n\nTherefore, this should be classified as a Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 8578566232, "prompt_eval_count": 368, "eval_count": 607}}, {"id": "8448", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 8448 : All 