sms (buttons, Esc key) are not working properly.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Issue: Buttons like \"OK/Close/Cancel\" and the Escape key fail to dismiss a dialog\n- Steps to reproduce: Open the About window, click OK button\n- Expected: The window closes\n- Actual: Nothing happens - the window has to be closed by clicking the X button\n\nThis is clearly a functional/UI defect. The buttons and keyboard shortcut (Esc) are not working to dismiss a dialog. This is a user interface functionality issue - buttons don't perform their intended action.\n\nLooking at the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nThis bug report is about:\n- A UI button not functioning properly\n- A keyboard shortcut (Esc) not working\n- A functional defect in dialog dismissal\n\nThere is no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Buffer overflow\n- Any security vulnerability\n- Any exploitable condition\n\nThis is clearly a non-security bug - it's a functional UI defect where buttons don't work as expected. The user can still close the dialog using the X button, so it's not even a blocking issue from a security perspective.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 9204424758, "prompt_eval_count": 437, "eval_count": 699}}, {"id": "8403", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 8403 : Chromium build downloads get cut off on slow internet connections 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   theodorejb    Chrome Version : N/A URLs (if applicable) http://build.chromium.org/buildbot/snapshots/chromium-rel-xp/   Firefox 3.0  IE8  : Safari 3: Firefox 3: Fail IE 7: Fail  ? 1. Try to download any of the Chromium builds (either the mini installer or the zip file) from a slow internet connection (say 40 KB/s)  ? The whole file downloads  ? The download stops before it's finished     It seems to be a timeout issue. The only way I can get any of the builds is to use Firefox 3.0. With Firefox you can re-download the file whenever it stops and it will continue downloading where it left off - I keep doing this until the whole file is downloaded. Chromium download issue.PNG 64.3 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:32:33.815232458Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report step-by-step:\n\n**Issue Summary**: Chromium build downloads get cut off/stop prematurely when using slow internet connections (~40 KB/s). This appears to be a timeout issue. The workaround is using Firefox, which supports resume functionality.\n\n**Analysis**:\n- The problem is a **functional defect** - downloads don't complete on slow connections due to apparent timeout behavior\n- There is **no memory corruption, buffer overflow, or code execution vulnerability** mentioned\n- There is **no information disclosure or privilege escalation** involved\n- While this affects \"availability\" of the download, it's a **general infrastructure/usability issue**, not a Denial of Service vulnerability that an attacker could exploit\n- The issue stems from server-side timeout configuration or client download implementation, not a security boundary bypass\n- No security-related keywords or patterns from the Class 1 criteria are present (no \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", or sandboxing bypasses)\n\n**Conclusion**: This is a straightforward functional bug related to download reliability on slow connections - a quality/UX issue, not a security vulnerability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe issue is about Chromium build downloads getting cut off on slow internet connections. Let me identify the key aspects:\n\n1. **Problem**: Downloads of Chromium builds stop/cut off before finishing when using slow internet connections (around 40 KB/s)\n2. **Cause**: It seems to be a timeout issue\n3. **Workaround**: Using Firefox 3.0, which supports resuming downloads, allows the user to eventually get the complete file\n4. **Affected browsers**: IE7 and IE8 fail, Firefox 3 works with the resume workaround\n\nNow let me evaluate this against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes without exploitable security impact.\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or DoS.\n\nAnalysis:\n- This is a functional defect - downloads don't complete on slow connections\n- It's a timeout/network issue, not a security vulnerability\n- There's no memory corruption, buffer overflow, information disclosure, or other security implications\n- While it could be seen as an availability issue (can't download files), this is a general functional problem, not a security vulnerability or DoS attack\n- The issue is about a server-side timeout affecting legitimate users on slow connections - not about an attacker causing denial of service\n- There's no indication of any exploitable security impact\n\nThis is clearly a functional/infrastructure issue, not a security vulnerability. The downloads cutting off due to slow internet is a server configuration or client implementation issue, not something that could be exploited by an attacker to compromise security.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 7842194652, "prompt_eval_count": 543, "eval_count": 628}}, {"id": "19788", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 19788 : Mono audio/video files crash Linux 1 p