\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", etc.\n\nThis crash:\n- Is a generic crash without specific memory corruption indicators\n- Doesn't mention any security-relevant keywords\n- Is a UI interaction issue with select elements\n- No evidence of exploitable security impact\n\nWhile a crash could potentially be a security issue (if it involves memory corruption that could be exploited), this report doesn't provide any such evidence. It's simply describing that Chrome crashes during normal UI interaction.\n\nThe guidelines say \"You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\" - Wait, \"prefer the safer option\" could mean classifying as security to be safe, but the context suggests preferring non-security unless there's clear evidence.\n\nActually, re-reading: \"When in doubt, prefer the safer option\" - in cybersecurity, the safer option would be to classify something as a security bug to ensure it gets proper attention. But the instruction also says to \"heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance.\"\n\nGiven:\n1. No clear evidence of memory corruption or other security implications\n2. Generic crash during UI interaction\n3. No security-related keywords in the report\n\nI should classify this as a Non-Security Bug (Class 0)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 10846857092, "prompt_eval_count": 490, "eval_count": 812}}, {"id": "11364", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general 