erations:\n1. This is a memory leak - memory is being allocated but not freed\n2. According to the domain knowledge, \"memory leak\" is explicitly listed as an indicator for Class 1 (Security Bug)\n3. Memory leaks can lead to Denial of Service (DoS) by exhausting available memory\n4. The domain knowledge states: \"Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS)\"\n5. The keyword \"memory leak\" and \"exhaustion\" are explicitly mentioned as distinctive patterns/keywords for Class 1\n\nMemory leaks are security-relevant because:\n- They can be exploited to cause resource exhaustion\n- They can lead to Denial of Service conditions\n- Attackers could potentially trigger the leak repeatedly to exhaust memory\n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, which explicitly lists \"memory leak\" as a distinctive keyword/pattern for Class 1 (Security Bug), and the fact that memory leaks can compromise availability (DoS), this should be classified as a Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 17364174959, "prompt_eval_count": 695, "eval_count": 587}}, {"id": "19796", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion