ooking at this bug report step-by-step:\n\n1. **Issue Description**: The Inspector (developer tools) is broken - it shows empty elements unless the user double-clicks on \"html\" or \"body\" tabs.\n\n2. **Reproduction Steps**: Navigate to a page, press Ctrl+Shift+J to open inspector, and the elements panel appears empty.\n\n3. **Analysis**:\n   - This is a UI/display rendering issue within the developer tools\n   - The keywords \"shows empty elements\", \"broken\", and the focus on visual display of the inspector all point to a functional defect\n   - There is no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, sandbox bypass, or any exploitable security vulnerability\n   - The bug affects the developer experience of using the inspector, not the security of the browser or user data\n\n4. **Classification**: This clearly falls under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) as it is a UI/UX rendering issue - specifically a visual display problem in the developer tools that lacks any exploitable security impact.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Issue 8821: [Regression] Inspector is broken\n- Shows empty elements unless you double click on \"html\" or \"body\" tabs\n- Steps to reproduce: Navigate a page, Ctrl+Shift+J to open inspector, inspector shows empty elements\n\nThis is clearly a UI/UX rendering issue with the developer tools inspector. The inspector is not displaying elements correctly - it shows empty elements until you double click on certain tabs.\n\nLet me check against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability.\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nThis bug report:\n1. Is about the inspector showing empty elements - this is a UI/display issue\n2. The keywords \"shows empty elements\", \"broken\", \"regression\" relate to visual display problems\n3. There's no indication of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any security impact\n4. This is a functional defect in the developer tools UI\n\nThis clearly falls under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) as it's a UI/UX rendering issue with no security implications.\n\nFinal answer: (A) Non-Security Bug"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 12512385384, "prompt_eval_count": 401, "eval_count": 621}}, {"id": "6041", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 6041 : Chrome bypass the form data validation because the user-defined propery of text field is not recognized by Chrome 2      nbsp;   acccf.acccf    Chrome Version : 1.0.154.36    : IE 7: OK The code bellow works whith IE7 but it doesn t work with Chrome  lt;script language= quot;JavaScript quot; gt; function ValidaSemPreenchimento(form){ for (i=0;i lt;form.length;i++){ var obg = form[i].obrigatorio; if (obg==1){ if (form[i].value ==  quot; quot;){ var nome = form[i].descricao alert( quot;O campo  quot; + nome +  quot; \u8305 obrigat rio. quot;) form[i].focus(); return false } } } return true }  lt;/script gt;  lt;form method= quot;post quot; action= quot; quot; onSubmit= quot;return validaSemPreenchimento(this) quot; gt;  lt;input type= quot;text quot; obrigatorio= quot;1 quot; descricao= quot;Texto quot; name= quot;texto quot; gt;  lt;input type= quot;submit quot; value= quot;OK quot; gt;  lt;/form gt;  ? A Validation of the form using the propertie  quot;descricao quot; to show the message  ? It submits the empty fields. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:46:50.822644187Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether this represents a security vulnerability or a functional/cross-browser compatibility issue.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe report describes Chrome not recognizing user-defined properties (`obrigatorio` and `descricao`) on form input elements. The JavaScript validation code uses `form[i].obrigatorio` to access these custom HTML attributes, which works in IE7 but not in Chrome.\n\nThis is a **cross-browser compatibility issue**, not a security vulnerability:\n\n1. **Root cause**: The code uses non-standard DOM property access (`form[i].obrigatorio`) instead of the standards-compliant `getAttribute('obrigatorio')` method. IE7 exposes custom attributes as expando properties, while Chrome follows web standards and does not.\n\n2. **Nature of the bypass**: The \"bypass\" is simply the validation code failing silently because `form[i].obrigatorio` returns `undefined` in Chrome, making `obg == 1` false, and thus skipping validation. This is a code logic/compatibility issue.\n\n3. **No security impact**: There's no memory corruption, privilege escalation, information disclosure, or exploitable vulnerability. The validation is client-side only and should never be relied upon for security anyway (server-side validation is always required).\n\n4. **Keywords check**: No security-relevant keywords like \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"sandbox bypass\", etc.\n\nThe issue is a functional defect - a web developer using non-standard JavaScript that doesn't work across browsers.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe issue describes that Chrome bypasses form data validation because user-defined properties of text fields are not recognized by Chrome. \n\nLooking at the code:\n- The form has a text input with custom attributes: `obrigatorio=\"1\"` and `descricao=\"Texto\"`\n- The JavaScript validation function tries to access these custom properties using `form[i].obrigatorio` and `form[i].descricao`\n- In IE7, this works because IE allows accessing custom HTML attributes as properties on DOM elements\n- In Chrome (and other standards-compliant browsers), custom attributes are not directly accessible as properties on DOM elements - you need to use `getAttribute('obrigatorio')` instead\n\nThis is a cross-browser compatibility issue, not a security vulnerability. The form validation is failing because of how the JavaScript code is written - it's using a non-standard way to access custom attributes that only works in IE.\n\nThis is a functional bug related to form validation not working in Chrome due to non-standard JavaScript coding practices. The user-defined properties (custom attributes) aren't being recognized, which causes the validation to fail and allows empty form submission.\n\nThis is NOT a security bug because:\n1. It's a client-side validation issue due to non-standard JavaScript code\n2. There's no memory corruption, buffer overflow, or any typical security vulnerability\n3. It's a cross-browser compatibility issue\n4. The \"bypass\" mentioned is not an intentional security bypass - it's just the validation code not working properly in Chrome\n5. Client-side validation should never be the sole means of validation anyway (server-side validation is required for security)\n\nThe classification should be Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) - it's a functional defect related to browser compatibility."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 45098945267, "prompt_eval_count": 637, "eval_count": 694}}, {"id": "21687", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 21687 : Most visited thumbnail titles don't respond to cleared browsing history 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   mirandac@chromium.org     ? 1. Have filled some of the most visited thumbnails. 2. Clear browsing history. What is the expected output? What do you see instead? One thumbnail is still present (Chrome themes gallery)  and pinned titles are still there. Thumbnails and titles in between have been cleared. The titles remain even after browser restart. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:46:54.960438541Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether it represents a security vulnerability or a functional defect.\n\n**Analysis:**\n- The issue describes thumbnails and pinned titles persisting in the \"most visited\" section after clearing browsing history\n- This is primarily a UI/data synchronization issue where the visual display doesn't properly update after the user clears their browsing data\n- The mention of \"pinned titles\" remaining is expected behavior for pinned items\n- The Chrome themes gallery thumbnail persisting may be a default/pinned item\n- While one could argue that traces of browsing history remaining might have privacy implications, this appears to be a UI refresh/data persistence issue in the new tab page, not an exploitable vulnerability\n\n**Key indicators pointing to Non-Security Bug:**\n- This is about UI rendering and display not updating properly\n- The \"pinned\" items behavior is likely by design\n- No memory corruption, buffer overflow, sandbox escape, or other security-relevant keywords\n- This is a functional defect in how the most visited thumbnails respond to history clearing\n\nThe bug describes a functional/UX defect where the UI doesn't properly refresh after clearing browsing history, rather than an exploitable security vulnerability compromising confidentiality, integrity, or availability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug is about:\n- Most visited thumbnail titles don't respond to cleared browsing history\n- When browsing history is cleared, some thumbnails and pinned titles still remain\n- This is a display/data persistence issue in the \"most visited\" section of Chrome's new tab page\n\nThis is clearly a functional defect - a UI/data synchronization issue where clearing browsing history doesn't properly clear all the thumbnail titles. It's about visual display and data persistence in the UI.\n\nLooking at the classification criteria:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems\n- Class 1 (Security Bug): vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability\n\nWhile one could argue that failing to clear browsing data could be a privacy concern (information disclosure - browsing history not being properly deleted), this bug report is specifically about:\n1. Pinned titles remaining (which is actually a feature - pinned items stay)\n2. One thumbnail remaining (Chrome themes gallery - likely a default/pinned item)\n\nThe bug seems to be about the UI not updating properly when history is cleared, particularly for pinned items. This appears to be a functional/UI defect rather than a security vulnerability.\n\nHowever, I should consider: if browsing history is supposed to be cleared but some traces remain, this could be seen as a privacy/confidentiality issue. The titles of visited sites remaining after clearing history could expose user browsing behavior.\n\nBut looking at the report more carefully:\n- It mentions \"pinned titles are still there\" - pinned items are designed to stay\n- The Chrome themes gallery thumbnail staying could be a default/pinned item\n- This seems more like a UI synchronization issue where the display doesn't update properly after clearing history\n\nThe key distinction is: is this a failure to actually delete the data (which could be security/privacy), or is this a UI refresh issue where the data is deleted but the display isn't updated?\n\nThe report says \"titles remain even after browser restart\" - this suggests the data itself might not be properly cleared, which could be a privacy concern. However, the context suggests this is about pinned items and default items not being cleared, which is likely by design.\n\nGiven the overall context and the fact that this appears to be a UI/data synchronization issue rather than an exploitable vulnerability, I'll classify this as a Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 20896702658, "prompt_eval_count": 433, "eval_count": 746}}, {"id": "5840", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 5840 : Recently closed tabs: default favicons don't help identify History  Downloads  about: pages 4      nbsp;   mberkow...@chromium.org    Chromium 1.0.156.0 ( r7453 ) 1. Open History and Downloads tabs in a browser wi