ion disclosure, cross-context, javascript:, redirect\n- No mentions of DoS, exhaustion, privilege bypass, sandboxing bypass\n- No advisory language\n- No exploitable security impact indicators\n\nWhat we have:\n- A login error that appears when trying to log in\n- This seems to be a functional defect - an error message appearing during login\n- There's no indication that this error leads to authentication bypass, credential exposure, or any security vulnerability\n- It's just a user experiencing an error when trying to log in - this is a functional/UI issue\n\nThis appears to be a non-security bug - a functional defect where a user encounters an error when trying to login. There's no evidence of security implications like authentication bypass, credential leakage, or any exploitable vulnerability.\n\nThe classification should be Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 15007215454, "prompt_eval_count": 388, "eval_count": 458}}, {"id": "557", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 557 : ActiveX control not auto-installing 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   r...@jdmlife.co.uk      :  lt;see about:version gt; URLs (if applicable) : Internal    : Safari 3: Firefox 3: FAIL IE 7: OK  ? 1. 2. 3.  ? When you click onto a machine (to remote control) on Kaseya MSP software  a lightweight VNC viewer ActiveX control automatically installs and connects you to the chosen desktop using VNC  in a VNC window.  ? Prompted to install an EXE file manually. This has to be done with every PC we remote control  every time. Please see screenshot attached.     activexerror.JPG 53.2 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:50:25.93699823Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether it describes a security vulnerability or a functional issue.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe report describes an issue where:\n- An ActiveX control (VNC viewer for Kaseya MSP remote control software) fails to auto-install\n- The user is instead prompted to manually install an EXE file every time\n- The feature works in IE7 but fails in Safari 3 and Firefox 3\n\n**Key observations:**\n1. This is fundamentally a **browser compatibility/functional issue** - the auto-installation mechanism for an ActiveX control isn't working as expected in certain browsers\n2. There's no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any exploitable condition\n3. The \"failure\" here is that automation doesn't work - the user has to manually perform an extra step, not that a security boundary is being violated\n4. No security-related keywords or patterns (buffer overflow, heap corruption, sandbox escape, etc.) are present\n5. The fact that ActiveX is involved doesn't automatically make it a security bug - the issue is about installation convenience, not a vulnerability being exploited\n\nWhile ActiveX controls have historically been associated with security concerns, this particular report is about a functional defect where the auto-install feature doesn't work in non-IE browsers. There is no evidence of an exploitable vulnerability compromising confidentiality, integrity, or availability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe issue is about an ActiveX control not auto-installing. Specifically:\n- When using Kaseya MSP software to remote control a machine\n- A lightweight VNC viewer ActiveX control should automatically install and connect\n- Instead, the user is prompted to install an EXE file manually every time\n- This works in IE7 but fails in other browsers (Safari 3, Firefox 3)\n\nThis is essentially a functionality issue - the ActiveX control isn't auto-installing as expected. The user has to manually download and install the EXE every time they want to remote control a PC.\n\nLet me check against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- Functional defect - the auto-install feature isn't working\n- Browser compatibility issue (works in IE7, not in Safari/Firefox)\n- This is a feature/functional expectation not being met\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- Memory corruption, information disclosure, DoS - none present\n- No \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", or privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n\nWhile ActiveX controls can have security implications, the bug reported here is specifically about the auto-installation mechanism not working - it's a functional issue, not a security vulnerability. The user is being prompted to install manually rather than having it happen automatically.\n\nThere's no indication of:\n- Exploitable vulnerability\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of service\n- Any attack vector being opened up\n\nThis is simply a browser compatibility/functional issue where the ActiveX auto-install feature doesn't work in certain browsers.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 33435534273, "prompt_eval_count": 506, "eval_count": 674}}, {"id": "820", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 820 : Editing Squidoo page puts side bar at the bottom of the page 2 problem? 1. Create a lens 2. Edit a subsection 3. The sidebar is at the bottom of the page during the edit session.  ?  ?     \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:50:26.121993682Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze the described issue:\n\n**Issue Description:**\n- When editing a Squidoo page (lens), the sidebar appears at the bottom of the page instead of its proper position\n- This occurs during the edit session after modifying a subsection\n\n**Analysis:**\n1. The problem is purely a **layout/rendering issue** - the sidebar is visually misplaced (appearing at the bottom instead of the side)\n2. There are no indicators of security impact:\n   - No memory corruption or buffer overflow\n   - No information disclosure\n   - No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n   - No denial of service\n   - No cross-context or redirect vulnerabilities\n3. The keywords and nature of this bug clearly align with UI/UX rendering problems (\"sidebar\", \"bottom of the page\", visual positioning)\n4. This