 correctly\n4. This is on Windows XP Professional SP3\n5. The problem started with a recent Chrome update\n6. Other programs/windows don't have this issue\n7. Previous versions of Chrome were fine\n8. Reinstalling Chrome and testing without Windows theme didn't help\n\nThis is clearly a UI/rendering issue related to window management - specifically a problem with how the maximized window displays on screen. The window isn't properly fitting the desktop size when maximized.\n\nAccording to the domain knowledge:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) includes \"UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems\" and keywords like \"render\", \"layout\"\n- Class 1 (Security Bug) involves vulnerabilities compromising confidentiality, integrity, or availability\n\nThis is a visual display/window management problem. It doesn't involve:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service (in the security sense - memory exhaustion, etc.)\n- Buffer overflow\n- Heap overwrite\n- Cross-context issues\n- JavaScript: protocol issues\n- Redirect vulnerabilities\n- Privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n\nThis is simply a UI bug where the window doesn't render correctly when maximized. There's no security impact mentioned or implied.\n\nThe classification should be: (A) Non-Security Bug"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 11315649998, "prompt_eval_count": 594, "eval_count": 594}}, {"id": "20037", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 20037 : notify osd gets covered by address bar drop-down menu 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   david.spike    Chrome Version : 4.0.202.2 OS + version : Jaunty 2.6.28-15 CPU architecture (32-bit / 64-bit):32bit window manager :compiz URLs (if applicable) : Behavior in Firefox 3.x (if applicable):N/A Behavior in Chrome for Windows (optional):N/A  ? 1.press down in address bar to extend it 2.have some sort of notification open at the time 3.  ? the notifications from notify osd should always appear on top of everything  ? in this case the extended address bar covers up the notification covering up its text on text-heavy notifications   Attach a screenshot and backtrace if possible. drop down bug.png 335 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:53:31.560462327Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze its content for security relevance.\n\n**Analysis:**\n- The issue describes a visual overlap problem where the address bar drop-down menu covers up notify-osd notifications\n- Key terms: \"covered by\", \"appear on top\", \"covering up its text\" - these all relate to visual display/z-ordering\n- The complaint is that notifications should appear on top of everything but are being visually obscured by the dropdown\n- This is fundamentally a UI/UX rendering issue about element layering on screen\n- No mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any exploitable vulnerability\n- No security-relevant keywords like \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", etc.\n\n**Domain Knowledge Check:**\n- Class 0 explicitly includes \"UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems\" with distinctive indicators being \"keywords related to visual display\"\n- This is purely a visual display problem where one UI element overlaps another undesirably\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThis is clearly a non-security bug - a UI rendering/z-ordering issue with no exploitable security impact.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe issue describes:\n- notify osd (on-screen display notifications) gets covered by the address bar drop-down menu\n- When the address bar is extended (drop-down appears), it covers up the notification\n- The user expects notifications to always appear on top of everything\n- This is essentially a visual/layout issue where UI elements overlap in an undesired way\n\nKey characteristics:\n- This is about visual display/rendering - the z-order of UI elements\n- Keywords like \"covered\", \"appear on top\", \"covering up its text\" all relate to visual rendering\n- It's about the window manager (compiz) and how overlay elements stack\n- There's no mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any exploitable security vulnerability\n- This is purely a UI/UX rendering issue about z-ordering of notification overlays vs dropdown menus\n\nBased on the domain knowledge:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): \"Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems\" with \"Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., 'render', 'CSS', 'layout'), user experience\"\n\nThis clearly falls under Class 0 as it's a UI/UX rendering issue about visual layering/z-ordering of UI elements. There's no security impact here - just a visual annoyance where notifications are hidden behind the address bar dropdown.\n\nThe answer is (A) Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 18151513358, "prompt_eval_count": 523, "eval_count": 548}}, {"id": "21041", "ground_truth": "1", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 21041 : Linux: Renderer using 23GB of virtual memory  6GB of resident memory 14      nbsp;   willchan@chromium.org    My friend (googler  running on ghardy workstation) was complaining to me that his chrome (4.0.203.2) was sucking up his memory  so I took a look: jyasskin 15127 4.6 0.4 572948 39236 ? Sl Aug31 209:35 /opt/google/chrome/google-chrome jyasskin 15149 0.0 0.0 284620 2332 ? S Aug31 0:00 \\_ /opt/google/chrome/google-chrome jyasskin 15150 0.0 0.0 160356 2196 ? S Aug31 0:00 \\_ /opt/google/chrome/chrome --type=zygote jyasskin 15159 6.2 75.7 23555972 6200944 ? Sl Aug31 281:18 | \\_ /opt/google/chrome/chrome --type=zygote jyasskin 15161 0.4 1.1 609592 94832 ? Sl Aug31 20:03 | \\_ /opt/google/chrome/chrome --type=zygote jyasskin 15163 0.0 0.3 351176 32384 ? Sl Aug31 1:36 | \\_ /opt/google/chrome/chrome --type=zygote jyasskin 15173 0.0 0.0 274364 5932 ? Sl Aug31 0:00 | \\_ /opt/google/chrome/chrome --type=zygote jyasskin 15588 0.0 0.1 290212 8584 ? Sl Aug31 0:01 | \\_ /opt/google/chrome/chrome --type=zygote jyasskin 15863 1.1 1.9 891092 156384 ? Sl Aug31 53:21 | \\_ /opt/google/chrome/chrome --type=zygote jyasskin 3001 0.0 0.4 289728 39708 ? Sl 14:02 0:00 | \\_ /opt/google/chrome/chrome --type=zygote As you can see  15159 is sucking up 23GB of virtual memory and 6GB of resident memory. That's pretty ridiculous. It looks to be one of his gmail tabs. He notes that he had an unanswered IM in the gmail tab for a few days  so the username would flash in the tab title. Not sure if that has anything to do with it. I've attached his /proc/15159/maps file which he cat'd out for me. It's got a ton of huge anonymous mmap regions. I don't know where these guys are coming from...v8 perhaps? I also see lots of mmap regions without any permissions. Sometimes these regions are 4MB  or 64MB or what not. It seems strange that we have so many of them and they're so large. My gut instinct is v8 is leaking  but I dunno. hugemaps 4.0 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:53:37.205152004Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\n**Key observations from the report:**\n\n1. A Chrome renderer process is consuming 23GB of virtual memory and 6GB of resident memory\n2. The `/proc/maps` file shows \"a ton of huge anonymous mmap regions\"\n3. There are numerous large mmap regions without permissions (4MB, 64MB sizes)\n4. The reporter explicitly states: \"My gut instinct is v8 is **leaking**\"\n5. The memory consumption is extreme and abnormal for a browser tab\n\n**Analysis against classification criteria:**\n\n- The domain knowledge explicitly lists **\"memory leak\"** as a distinctive keyword for Class 1 (Security Bug)\n- The domain knowledge also lists **\"exhaustion\"** as a security indicator - this is a case of resource exhaustion (memory exhaustion leading to potential DoS)\n- This level of memory consumption (23GB virtual, 6GB resident) can compromise **availability** of the system, which is explicitly mentioned in the Class 1 definition\n- Memory leaks that enable resource exhaustion are exploitable for Denial of Service attacks - an attacker could craft a page to trigger this leak and crash the browser or even the entire system\n\n**Counter-arguments to consider:**\n- Could this be just a performance/functional bug? While it does affect functionality, the severity (6GB resident memory) and the potential for DoS makes this a security concern. The domain knowledge explicitly calls out \"memory leak\" and \"exhaustion\" as security indicators.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThis is clearly a memory leak leading to resource exhaustion, both of which are explicitly listed as Class 1 indicators. The extreme memory consumption could be weaponized for DoS attacks.\n\n**Answer: (B) Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe report describes:\n1. A Chrome renderer process (PID 15159) using 23GB of virtual memory and 6GB of resident memory\n2. This is on a Linux system\n3. The process appears to be a tab related to Gmail\n4. The /proc/15159/maps file shows \"a ton of huge anonymous mmap regions\"\n5. There are many mmap regions without permissions, some being 4MB or 64MB\n6. The reporter suspects v8 is leaking memory\n\nNow, let me evaluate this against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug) indicators:**\n- \"memory leak\" - The domain knowledge explicitly lists \"memory leak\" as a distinctive keyword for security bugs\n- This could lead to Denial of Service (DoS) through memory exhaustion\n- The domain knowledge mentions \"exhaustion\" as a security indicator\n\nThis is a memory leak that causes extreme memory consumption (23GB virtual, 6GB resident). This is clearly a memory leak issue, which:\n1. Is explicitly mentioned as a Class 1 indicator (\"memory leak\")\n2. Can lead to availability issues (DoS through resource exhaustion)\n3. \"Exhaustion\" is also mentioned as a security indicator\n\nThe extreme memory consumption could potentially be triggered maliciously to cause Denial of Service, making this a security vulnerability. Memory leaks that lead to resource exhaustion are considered security issues because they can be exploited to make a system unavailable.\n\nTherefore, this should be classified as a Security Bug.\n\n**Answer: (B) Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 16474532474, "prompt_eval_count": 1065, "eval_count": 693}}, {"id": "10036", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 10036 : Add WebPlugin to the WebKit API 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   darin@chromium.org    Add WebPlugin to the WebKit API \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:53:40.497296548Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report:\n\n**Analysis:**\n- The report is titled \"Add WebPlugin to the WebKit API\"\n- This is clearly a feature request to add a new API component (WebPlugin) to the WebKit API\n- There is no mention of any vulnerability, memory corruption, information disclosure, or any exploitable security issue\n- No security-related keywords from Class 1 are present (no \"buffer overflow\", \"memory leak\", \"heap overwrite\", \"exhaustion\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", or privilege bypass)\n- The request is about adding functionality/API surface, not fixing a security vulnerability\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThis is a feature request to extend the WebKit API with a WebPlugin component. It falls squarely under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) as it represents a feature request with no evidence of security impact.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is:\n\"Issue 10036 : Add WebPlugin to the WebKit API 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes. nbsp; darin@chromium.org Add WebPlugin to the WebKit API\"\n\nThis is a request to \"Add WebPlugin to the WebKit API\". This appears to be a feature request to add a new API component (WebPlugin) to the WebKit API.\n\nLet me evaluate this against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display, user experience (e.g., \"feature request\"), and generic instability.\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nThis bug report is clearly a feature request - it's asking to add a new component (WebPlugin) to an API. There's no indication of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Any vulnerability being exploited\n- Any of the security-related keywords mentioned in Class 1\n\nWhile plugins can have security implications, the bug report itself is just a request to add an API component. This is a feature request, which falls under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug).\n\nThe answer should be (A) Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 9230981366, "prompt_eval_count": 369, "eval_count": 558}}, {"id": "17651", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 17651 : Image width/height separator is garbage 2 problem? 1. Open http://www.google.com/intl/en_ALL/images/logo.gif 2. Look in title bar  ? Says 276x110  ? See screenshot. This one is for XP. Vista does something similar     title.PNG 22.6 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:53:42.804358283Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze what the issue is about:\n\n**Bug Summary:**\n- The issue is about how image dimensions (width/height) are displayed in the title bar\n- When opening an image, the title bar shows dimensions like \"276x110\"\n- The complaint is that the separator between width and height is \"garbage\" - it's a visual display issue\n- The user compares behavior across XP and Vista\n\n**Analysis:**\n- This is clearly a **UI/display rendering issue** - the way image dimensions are formatted and shown in the title bar\n- There is no mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, information disclosure, or any exploitable security vulnerability\n- The keywords and patterns match Class 0 indicators: this is a \"visual display\" / \"rendering\" issue\n- No security-related keywords (overflow, bypass, disclosure, DoS, etc.) are present\n- The bug affects how information is *displayed* to the user, not whether unauthorized access or exploitation is possible\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThis is a functional/visual defect about how the dimension separator is rendered in the title bar. It has no security implications.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about \"Image width/height separator is garbage\". It describes:\n1. Opening an image URL (Google logo)\n2. Looking at the title bar which shows \"276x110\"\n3. There seems to be an issue with how the dimensions are displayed\n\nThis appears to be a UI/display issue where the width/height separator for an image is being displayed incorrectly or showing \"garbage\" in the title bar. The user is comparing how it looks on XP vs Vista.\n\nLooking at the classification criteria:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Keywords include \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\", etc.\n- Class 1 (Security Bug): Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or DoS. Keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nThis bug report is clearly about a UI display issue - the way image dimensions are shown in the title bar. There's no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Information disclosure\n- DoS\n- Any security vulnerability\n\nThis is purely a visual/UI defect about how the separator between width and height values is displayed in the title bar.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 13254317617, "prompt_eval_count": 404, "eval_count": 579}}, {"id": "8815", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 8815 : Chrome incorrectly switches input language 2 problem? 0.Ensure input language is English 1.Open a tab and go to any page 2.Open a tab with gmail 3.Click Compose Mail 4.Click in the text box 5.Select input language to (say) Hebrew 6.Type something 7.Click 1st tab  ? Input language should remain Hebrew  ? Input language switches to English Switching back to the 2nd tab leaves Input language at English     \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:53:43.660405653Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether it represents a security vulnerability or a functional/UI defect.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe bug describes:\n- Chrome switches the input language back to English when switching between tabs\n- The user expects the input language (Hebrew) to persist when moving between tabs\n- This is a behavior where tab focus changes cause the input method to reset\n\n**Evaluating against classification criteria:**\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) indicators:**\n- This is clearly a functional defect in how input language state is managed across tabs\n- It's a user experience issue - the language preference isn't being preserved as expected\n- No mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or DoS\n- No keywords like \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"sandbox bypass\", etc.\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug) indicators:**\n- None present. There's no evidence that this language switching behavior compromises confidentiality, integrity, or availability\n- No exploitable security impact is described\n- No advisory or vulnerability-related terminology used\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nThis is a straightforward UI/UX bug where input language state isn't properly preserved when switching tabs. It's an annoyance for multilingual users but has no security implications. The language resetting doesn't leak information, allow privilege escalation, or cause memory corruption.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Chrome incorrectly switches input language when switching between tabs\n- When user switches from a tab where they set the input language to Hebrew back to another tab, the input language switches back to English instead of remaining Hebrew\n- This is essentially a language/input method persistence issue across tabs\n\nLet me evaluate this against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- This is a functional defect - the input language doesn't persist correctly when switching tabs\n- It's a user experience issue - the language setting doesn't behave as expected\n- Keywords like \"input language\", \"switches\" in this context relate to UI/UX behavior\n- No mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, information disclosure, or any security-relevant vulnerability\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- Would need evidence of memory corruption, information disclosure, DoS, privilege escalation, etc.\n- Would need keywords like \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", sandboxing bypasses\n- None of these are present in this bug report\n\nThis is clearly a UI/UX functional bug where the input language state isn't properly maintained when switching between tabs. There's no security implication here - it doesn't compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability. It's just an annoying behavior where the language setting doesn't persist as expected.\n\nThe classification should be Class 0 - Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 6166990657, "prompt_eval_count": 426, "eval_count": 612}}, {"id": "9273", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classifi