bility concerns.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Issue 13027: auto-opening grayed out\n- Chrome Version: 3.0.182.3\n- Steps to reproduce: Go to Options > Clear auto-opening settings\n- Expected: A clickable 'clear auto-opening' button\n- Actual: Button is grayed out and not active\n\nThis is clearly a UI/UX issue where a button is grayed out and not clickable when it shouldn't be (or when the user expects it to be active). This is a functional defect related to user interface behavior.\n\nLooking at the classification criteria:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact.\n- Class 1 (Security Bug): Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS).\n\nThis bug report is about a button being grayed out - a UI issue. There's no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, DoS, or any security-related keywords like \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", \"sandboxing bypass\", etc.\n\nThe bug is purely a UI/functional issue where the \"Clear auto-opening settings\" button is not clickable when it should be. This falls squarely under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug).\n\nFinal answer: (A) Non-Security Bug"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 20299490131, "prompt_eval_count": 471, "eval_count": 560}}, {"id": "6306", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making yo