s is about browser compatibility\n   - \"Certain site functionality doesn't work in Google Chrome\" \u2014 functional defect\n   - The issue is about UI interactions (clicking buttons, page navigation) not working as expected\n   - Works in IE6 but not in Chrome/Firefox \u2014 browser compatibility issue\n\n3. **Security relevance check:**\n   - No mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap overwrite\n   - No information disclosure or data leakage concerns\n   - No Denial of Service (DoS) vulnerability\n   - No cross-context or sandboxing bypass\n   - No mention of javascript: URIs, redirects, or privilege escalation\n   - This is purely a functional/compatibility defect\n\n4. **Classification determination:** This is a website functionality problem where certain interactive elements don't work in specific browsers. It falls clearly under \"functional defects\" and \"general application\" issues with no exploitable security impact.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about an issue with the website airindiaexpress.in where:\n1. Users are unable to finalize flight bookings\n2. Changing currency doesn't work\n3. Clicking \"Confirm flights\" doesn't switch to the next page\n4. This is a browser compatibility issue - it works in IE6 but not in Chrome or Firefox\n\nThis is clearly a functional/compatibility issue with a website. The bug is about:\n- Site functionality not working in certain browsers\n- UI interaction problems (clicking buttons not performing expected actions)\n\nThere is no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Buffer overflow\n- Heap overwrite\n- Cross-context issues\n- JavaScript: protocol issues\n- Redirect vulnerabilities\n- Privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n\nThis is a browser compatibility issue where certain website features don't work properly in Chrome/Firefox but work in IE6. This falls squarely into the \"Non-Security Bug\" category as it's a functional defect related to web compatibility.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 11297685958, "prompt_eval_count": 679, "eval_count": 514}}, {"id": "15181", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 15181 : [www.terra.no]: Form not appearing where it should because of float:left attribute of  lt;div gt; 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   findit.role    URL: http://www.terra.no/Ei