 two issues:\n1. When dragging to a screen edge with an existing maximized window, the docking choices toggle unpredictably between \"window edge\" and \"screen edge\", with one being drawn half-offscreen.\n2. In Vista Aero, when docking to the side window edge of a maximized window, the newly-created full-height windows are a little too tall - the top and bottom edges of the frame are both slightly offscreen.\n\nLet me check this against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\" - this involves layout/rendering issues\n- UI/UX rendering issues\n- Visual display problems\n- \"glitchy\" visual behavior\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- Memory corruption, information disclosure, DoS\n- \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\"\n- \"cross-context\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\"\n- Privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n\nThis bug report is clearly about UI/UX rendering and layout issues - specifically about windows being drawn slightly offscreen and docking behavior being inconsistent. This is a visual display/layout problem that affects user experience but doesn't compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability.\n\nThere's no mention of:\n- Memory corruption or leaks\n- Buffer overflows\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of service\n- Privilege escalation\n- Any exploitable security impact\n\nThe issues described are purely cosmetic/UX glitches where windows aren't positioned correctly on screen. These are functional defects related to visual display and layout.\n\nTherefore, this should be classified as a Non-Security Bug (Class 0)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 13040093815, "prompt_eval_count": 499, "eval_count": 616}}, {"id": "15880", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vuln