 overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 17913 : Wrong description of printing-related string translation 2      nbsp;   evan@chromium.org     lt;message name= quot;IDS_DEFAULT_PRINTER_NOT_FOUND_WARNING_TITLE quot; desc= quot;Title of the warning dialog when no when no printer is available quot; gt; No printer found  Please install a printer.  lt;/message gt; Looking at the use of this  it seems this is not the title of the dialog  but rather the content. If it really is intended to be the title  it should be much shorter and have different capitalization. If it is intended to be the body of the dialog  it should not have a sentence splice. :) \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:56:11.314254653Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "This bug report is about a mislabeled string translation description for a printing-related dialog message. The issue is that a string described as a \"title\" actually appears to be dialog content, and has grammatical/style concerns (sentence splice, capitalization). \n\nThis is clearly a UI/localization issue - there's no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, DoS, or any other security vulnerability. It's purely about the accuracy of a string description and proper formatting of dialog text.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about:\n- A wrong description of a printing-related string translation\n- The string `IDS_DEFAULT_PRINTER_NOT_FOUND_WARNING_TITLE` has a description that says \"Title of the warning dialog when no when no printer is available\"\n- The actual content is \"No printer found. Please install a printer.\"\n- The issue is that this string appears to be the content/body of a dialog, not the title\n- If it is a title, it should be shorter and have different capitalization\n- If it is intended to be the body, it shouldn't have a \"sentence splice\"\n\nThis is clearly a UI/UX issue related to:\n1. String translation/description accuracy\n2. Proper labeling of dialog elements (title vs. content)\n3. Grammar/punctuation concerns\n\nThere is no security impact here - no memory corruption, no information disclosure, no privilege escalation, no DoS, no cross-context issues, no buffer overflow, etc. This is purely a localization/translation and UI description issue.\n\nThis falls clearly under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) as it's a functional/UI defect related to string descriptions and translations.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 7342483566, "prompt_eval_count": 467, "eval_count": 365}}, {"id": "1776", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 1776 : 100% CPU Utilization by Flash Movie On Idle 5 problem? 1. view flash movie from google video  youtube and other site 2. Wait for 4-5 minutes 3. Watch CPU utilization and the video output  ? The video should continuously play toward the end at the same system load.  ? The CPU will jump to 100% after no mouse activity for 4-5 minutes. The flash movie will hang and lag the system. Upon  quot;wiggling quot; the mouse  CPU use returns to normal and the video continues normally.     Shockwave Flash File name: npswf32.dll Shockwave Flash 9.0 r124 MIME Type Description Suffixes Enabled application/x-shockwave-flash Adobe Flash movie swf Yes application/futuresplash FutureSplash movie spl Yes This behavior occurs even if the user is using another application on the system and only keyboard input is being sent to that application. Chrome seems to want input periodically to keep the flash video behaving properly. Hardware: AMD Athlon XP 2800+ NVIDIA GeForce FX 5600 \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:56:15.225717988Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze the key characteristics:\n\n**Key Details from the Report:**\n- Flash movie causes 100% CPU utilization after 4-5 minutes of mouse inactivity\n- System hangs and lags, affecting other applications\n- CPU exhaustion continues until mouse activity resumes\n- This affects overall system availability\n\n**Analysis against classification criteria:**\n\nThe report describes **resource exhaustion** - specifically CPU exhaustion that:\n1. Reaches 100% CPU utilization (complete resource consumption)\n2. Causes system-wide lag affecting other applications (availability impact)\n3. Can be triggered simply by loading a Flash movie on a webpage\n\nThe Class 1 (Security Bug) definition explicitly mentions \"exhaustion\" as a distinctive keyword and includes \"Denial of Service (DoS)\" as a vulnerability type that compromises availability. This 100% CPU utilization pattern is a classic resource exhaustion DoS condition - a malicious webpage could embed such Flash content to render the system unresponsive.\n\nWhile this manifests during normal video playback, the fact that it exhausts system resources to the point of degrading other applications and requires specific user interaction (mouse wiggle) to recover indicates an availability-compromising vulnerability.\n\n**Answer: (B) Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n1. A Flash movie causing 100% CPU utilization after 4-5 minutes of no mouse activity\n2. The flash movie hangs and lags the system\n3. CPU usage returns to normal when the mouse is moved\n4. This occurs even when the user is in another application\n\nNow let me evaluate this against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug) indicators to look for:**\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service (DoS)\n- Memory leak\n- Buffer overflow\n- Heap overwrite\n- Cross-context issues\n- Exhaustion\n- javascript:\n- Redirect\n- Advisory\n- Privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) indicators:**\n- Functional defects\n- UI/UX rendering issues\n- Layout problems\n- Feature requests\n- General application crashes without memory corruption or exhaustion context\n\nThis bug involves:\n- 100% CPU utilization - this is a form of resource exhaustion\n- System lag/hang - this is essentially a Denial of Service condition\n\nThe key question is whether this constitutes a DoS vulnerability or just a performance bug.\n\nThe bug causes:\n- Complete CPU exhaustion (100%)\n- System lag affecting other applications\n- The flash movie hangs\n\nThis could be classified as a Denial of Service (DoS) issue because:\n- It exhausts CPU resources (mentions \"exhaustion\" as a Class 1 indicator)\n- It affects system availability by making the system lag\n- A malicious flash movie could potentially be used to tie up system resources\n\nHowever, I need to consider:\n- Is this just a performance/rendering bug in Flash, or is it exploitable?\n- The description seems more like a bug in how Chrome/Flash handles idle state rather than an intentional DoS vector\n- There's no mention of malicious intent or exploitation\n- It's a single flash movie the user is watching, not a mechanism for attacking others\n\nThe key Class 1 keyword mentioned is \"exhaustion\" - CPU exhaustion is a form of resource exhaustion that can lead to DoS. This fits the pattern of availability compromise.\n\nHowever, looking more carefully:\n- This is about a Flash plugin behavior during idle\n- It's more of a performance/compatibility issue\n- The user is choosing to watch a video - it's not an attack vector where someone else can trigger this\n- The 100% CPU is a side effect of idle handling, not an intentional DoS\n\nOn the other hand:\n- Resource exhaustion is explicitly mentioned as a Class 1 indicator\n- This does impact availability of the system\n- A malicious webpage could embed such a flash movie to consume CPU resources\n\nI think this is borderline, but the safer classification given the rules would be Security Bug because:\n1. It involves resource exhaustion (explicitly mentioned as a Class 1 indicator)\n2. It impacts system availability (DoS-like behavior)\n3. The instruction says \"When in doubt, prefer the safer option\" which would be Class 1\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The \"safer option\" in security context would typically mean classifying something as a security bug when uncertain, because missing a security vulnerability is more dangerous than over-reporting.\n\nThe 100% CPU utilization that affects the entire system and can be triggered by visiting a webpage with Flash content does have security implications as a DoS vector. It exhausts system resources and impacts availability.\n\nI'll classify this as a Security Bug due to the resource exhaustion/DoS nature of the issue."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 27515580002, "prompt_eval_count": 553, "eval_count": 994}}, {"id": "14085", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 14085 : Chrome on OS/X does not recognize input language 2 problem? 1. Start chrome 2. Log onto gmail 3. Click compose email 4. Set input language to Japanese hiragana. 5. Type the two letters n o 6. Expect to show the Japanese character \u9287?with prompting for kanjis. Actually see the English letters no 7. Set input language to French 8. Type option-e e 9. Expect to see \u951a; actually see e 10. Set input language to US English 11. Type option-u u. 12. Expect to see \u7709  actually see u  ? As above: \u9287?/ \u951a / \u7709  ? no / e / u     Picture 4.png 207 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:56:17.788688272Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether it involves a security vulnerability or is a functional defect.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe bug describes Chrome on OS/X not properly recognizing input language settings. Specifically:\n- Japanese hiragana input shows \"no\" instead of the Japanese character \u9287?\n- French accent input shows \"e\" instead of \u00ea\n- German umlaut input shows \"u\" instead of \u00fc\n\n**Key observations:**\n1. This is an **input method editor (IME) functionality issue** - the browser isn't properly processing foreign language input methods on OS/X\n2. The problem is about **visual display/input of characters** - users cannot type non-English characters as expected\n3. There is **no mention** of any security-related impact such as memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or denial of service\n4. No security keywords are present (no \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", or sandboxing bypasses)\n5. This is a **usability/functional defect** affecting internationalization support\n\nThe bug falls squarely under \"functional defects\" and \"UI/UX rendering issues\" as described in Class 0 - it's about the browser not properly handling input language switching, which is a user experience/feature functionality problem, not a security vulnerability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report describes an issue where Chrome on OS/X does not recognize input language properly. Specifically:\n\n1. When trying to type Japanese hiragana, the letters \"no\" appear instead of the Japanese character\n2. When trying to type French accented characters (\u00ea), just the letter \"e\" appears\n3. When trying to type German umlauts (\u00fc), just the letter \"u\" appears\n\nThis is clearly a functional/UX bug related to input method handling. The browser is not properly recognizing or processing the input language settings on OS/X.\n\nLet me check against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- This is a functional defect - input method/language support is not working correctly\n- It's related to user experience - users cannot type in non-English languages properly\n- There's no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any security-related impact\n- No exploitable security impact is described\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- None of the keywords or patterns mentioned are present (no \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", privilege/sandboxing bypasses)\n- No confidentiality, integrity, or availability compromise is described\n- No DoS, no information disclosure, no memory corruption\n\nThis is clearly a localization/input method bug that affects usability for non-English users. It's a functional defect that prevents users from properly entering text in other languages. There's no security implication mentioned or implied.\n\nThe classification should be: Non-Security Bug (Class 0)"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 8011380168, "prompt_eval_count": 504, "eval_count": 652}}, {"id": "16422", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 16422 : file:// doesn't take networked UNC paths 2 problem? 1. navigate to a file:////someserver//someshare//somefile URL  ? see the page/image  ?  quot;The webpage is not found quot; (ERR_FILE_NOT_FOUND) \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:56:18.646522647Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether the issue with file:// protocol not handling UNC paths represents a security vulnerability or a functional defect.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Navigating to a file:////someserver//someshare//somefile URL\n- Result: \"The webpage is not found\" (ERR_FILE_NOT_FOUND)\n\nThis is essentially reporting that UNC (Universal Naming Convention) network paths don't work when accessed via the file:// protocol scheme. The user is reporting a **functional limitation** - a feature that doesn't work as expected.\n\n**Security Considerations:**\n- The bug is about UNC paths NOT working (failing to load), not about them working in an exploitable way\n- There's no indication of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any exploitable vulnerability\n- No mention of bypasses, cross-context issues, or other security-sensitive patterns from the Class 1 indicators\n- While UNC path handling *can* have security implications (e.g., SMB relay attacks if authentication is triggered), this report is simply about the feature failing to function - a non-functional behavior, not a security bypass\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThis is a functional defect where a capability (accessing network shares via file:// protocol) doesn't work as expected. The user is essentially reporting a feature that isn't functioning properly, which aligns with Class 0 criteria for functional defects.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about:\n- Issue 16422: file:// doesn't take networked UNC paths\n- The problem is that when navigating to a file:////someserver//someshare//somefile URL, the page/image is not found (ERR_FILE_NOT_FOUND)\n\nThis appears to be a functional issue where UNC paths (Universal Naming Convention - network paths like \\\\server\\share) are not being properly handled when accessed through the file:// protocol scheme.\n\nLet me consider the security implications:\n\n1. UNC paths in browsers can have security implications - they can potentially be used for:\n   - Information disclosure (accessing files on network shares)\n   - SMB relay attacks (if the browser authenticates to the UNC path)\n   - Cross-origin issues\n\n2. However, the bug as described is about UNC paths NOT working - the browser fails to load them (ERR_FILE_NOT_FOUND). This is a functional defect, not a security vulnerability being exploited.\n\n3. The question is: is the fact that UNC paths don't work a security issue or just a functional bug?\n\nLooking at this more carefully:\n- If UNC paths are supposed to work and don't, this is a functional bug (feature doesn't work as expected)\n- If UNC paths are blocked for security reasons (to prevent SMB-based attacks or information leakage), then this would be expected behavior, not a bug at all\n- The way the bug is reported, it seems like the user expects UNC paths to work and they don't\n\nThis appears to be a non-security bug - it's about functionality not working (file:// with UNC paths not loading). The user is reporting that a feature (accessing network shares via file://) doesn't work.\n\nHowever, I should also consider: could the inability to properly handle UNC paths indicate a security issue? For example:\n- If the browser is incorrectly parsing UNC paths, could there be a path traversal or bypass issue?\n- Could the failure mode itself leak information?\n\nThe bug report doesn't suggest any of these security implications. It's simply reporting that UNC paths through file:// don't work - a functional issue.\n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, feature requests\n- Class 1 (Security Bug): memory corruption, information disclosure, DoS, etc.\n\nThis seems to fall under Class 0 - a functional defect where file:// with UNC paths doesn't work as expected.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. There could be a security angle here. If the browser is supposed to block UNC paths for security reasons (to prevent attacks like SMB relay), but the error message or behavior is inconsistent, that could be a security concern. However, the bug report doesn't indicate this - it's simply reporting that UNC paths don't work.\n\nActually, thinking more about this - UNC path handling in browsers has been a security concern because:\n1. Accessing UNC paths could trigger NTLM authentication to remote servers (SMB relay attack potential)\n2. It could allow reading files from network shares that should be restricted\n\nBut the bug here is that UNC paths DON'T work, not that they work in an exploitable way. This is a functionality issue, not a security vulnerability.\n\nMy conclusion: This is a Non-Security Bug (Class 0). It's about a feature not working properly (UNC paths via file:// protocol), which is a functional defect."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 20716243016, "prompt_eval_count": 386, "eval_count": 1011}}, {"id": "17236", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 17236 : text/javascript1.2 not being interpreted properly  nbsp;    nbsp;   johnathan.arsenault    Chrome Version :  lt;Copy from: 'about:version' gt; URLs (if applicable) :    : Safari 4:OK Firefox 3.x:OK IE 7:OK IE 8:OK  ? 1.go to http://www.anselm.edu 2.open the javascript console 3.enable resource tracking 4.attempt to mouse over current students.  ? should open a list made from an array  ? the debugger catches an unhandled exception. shows that the variable is null because the .js was not interpreted properly as script  but as text only.     \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:56:26.077532526Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether it represents a security vulnerability or a functional defect.\n\n**Key Details from the Report:**\n- Issue: \"text/javascript1.2 not being interpreted properly\"\n- The JavaScript file is being treated as text instead of being executed as script\n- This causes an unhandled exception because a variable is null\n- The expected behavior (opening a list from an array on mouse-over) doesn't work\n- Other browsers (Safari 4, Firefox 3.x, IE 7, IE 8) handle this correctly\n\n**Analysis:**\nThis is a MIME type handling/compatibility issue where Chrome fails to recognize and execute JavaScript with the \"text/javascript1.2\" content type. The JavaScript is being treated as plain text rather than being executed, causing the page functionality to break.\n\n**Checking against security indicators:**\n- No memory corruption (buffer overflow, heap overwrite, etc.)\n- No information disclosure\n- No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n- No denial of service (resource exhaustion)\n- No cross-context or cross-origin issues\n- No redirect or javascript: URI issues\n\nThe bug is a straightforward functional compatibility defect - Chrome doesn't properly handle a specific JavaScript MIME type variant that other browsers support. This breaks page functionality but doesn't introduce any exploitable security vulnerability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about \"text/javascript1.2 not being interpreted properly\" in Chrome.\n\nKey details:\n- When visiting http://www.anselm.edu\n- Opening the javascript console and enabling resource tracking\n- Attempting to mouse over \"current students\"\n- Expected: should open a list made from an array\n- Actual: the debugger catches an unhandled exception, the variable is null because the .js was not interpreted properly as script but as text only\n\nThis appears to be a functional bug where Chrome is not properly handling/interpreting JavaScript with a MIME type of \"text/javascript1.2\". Instead of executing the JavaScript, it's treating it as plain text.\n\nLet me consider whether this is a security bug or not:\n\nArguments for Security Bug:\n- The mention of JavaScript not being interpreted could potentially relate to security mechanisms (like content type sniffing, MIME type handling)\n- Improper handling of script interpretation could theoretically lead to security issues\n\nArguments for Non-Security Bug:\n- This is primarily a functionality issue - JavaScript isn't being executed when it should be\n- The bug is about a feature not working correctly (mouse over should show a list)\n- There's no mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, privilege escalation, or other security-related terms\n- The issue is that a script is being treated as text instead of being executed - this is a rendering/functional issue\n- No exploitable security impact is described\n- The comparison with other browsers (Safari, Firefox, IE) suggests this is just Chrome not handling a specific MIME type correctly\n\nThe core issue is that Chrome doesn't properly interpret JavaScript with the \"text/javascript1.2\" MIME type. This is a compatibility/functional bug, not a security vulnerability. The JavaScript is being treated as text rather than being executed, which breaks the functionality of the page but doesn't expose a security vulnerability.\n\nThere's no evidence of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Privilege escalation\n- Sandbox escape\n- Cross-context issues\n- Buffer overflow\n\nThis is a straightforward functional defect where a specific MIME type (\"text/javascript1.2\") isn't being handled correctly by Chrome.\n\nAnswer: (A) Non-Security Bug"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 10669451388, "prompt_eval_count": 475, "eval_count": 740}}, {"id": "12699", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 12699 : throbber is stuck and not animating 2 problem? 1. Go to a site that takes some time to load (i.e. www.naver.com and click on an article) What is the expected output? What do you see instead? The throbber should be spinning while loading. Now it is stuck with only a quarter of the circle displayed Please use labels and text to provide additional information. Picture 8.png 1.1 MB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:56:27.168764911Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\n**Bug Description Analysis:**\n- The issue is about a \"throbber\" (loading animation indicator) being stuck and not animating properly\n- The throbber should spin while loading but instead displays stuck with only a quarter of the circle visible\n- This is purely a visual/UI animation problem\n\n**Checking against classification criteria:**\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug) indicators - NOT present:**\n- No memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap overwrite\n- No information disclosure or confidentiality compromise\n- No Denial of Service (resource exhaustion) - the page still loads, just the indicator doesn't animate\n- No privilege escalation, sandbox bypass, or cross-context issues\n- No \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\" keywords\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) indicators - PRESENT:**\n- This is clearly a UI/UX rendering issue (\"throbber is stuck\