h applying a default theme in a browser (likely Chrome based on the screenshot name \"chrome-ss3.jpg\"). \n\nThe issue is:\n1. User installs a different theme\n2. User applies the default theme using a page\n3. A yellow bar appears saying \"Installed theme: Default\" with a button saying \"change back to default?\"\n4. The complaint is that the button shouldn't be required/shouldn't appear since the user is already applying the default theme\n\nThis is clearly a UI/UX issue - a confusing interface element that appears when it shouldn't. The user is complaining about:\n- A button appearing that says \"change back to default?\" when they're already on the default theme\n- The button is unnecessary and confusing in this context\n\nThis is a functional/UX bug related to theme management and user interface behavior. There's no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Any security vulnerability\n- Any exploitable condition\n\nThe keywords and patterns here are related to:\n- Theme application\n- UI buttons\n- User experience confusion\n\nThis falls squarely into Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) as it's a UI/UX issue with no security implications whatsoever.\n\nMy answer is (A) Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 21345586674, "prompt_eval_count": 437, "eval_count": 516}}, {"id": "4181", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e