lications whatsoever.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about:\n- When dragging a tab in Chrome while holding the Ctrl button, the tab should create a copy of itself\n- Instead, the tab just moves to another position without creating a copy\n- The user expects that holding Ctrl while dragging should duplicate the tab\n\nThis is clearly a feature request or a functional bug related to tab behavior in the browser. There's no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Privilege escalation\n- Sandbox bypass\n- Any security vulnerability\n\nThe keywords used are related to UI/UX functionality:\n- \"dragging the Tab\"\n- \"copy of Tab\"\n- \"Tab Window move its position\"\n\nThis is a feature request or a UI behavior bug - the user wants the Ctrl+drag behavior to duplicate tabs (which is a feature in some browsers). There's no security implication here.\n\nBased on the classification guidelines:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests\n- Class 1 (Security Bug): Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability\n\nThis is clearly a Class 0 - Non-Security Bug. It's a feature request or functional bug about tab duplication behavior."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 40527067276, "prompt_eval_count": 502, "eval_count": 502}}, {"id": "783", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 783 : Customisation of new tab page 3      nbsp;   Sam.Derbyshire    The new tab page should be able to be customised  in a way resembling the iGoogle personalised homepage ( http://www.google.com/ig ). For example  it would be nice to be able to choose what search engines to display on the right (some engines are there because I used the once but never use them again)  possibly some news feeds (with the upcoming RSS support) or just some favourited pages that would differ from the bookmarks and the most visited pages. Possibly even be able to chose a background or something ! \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:58:14.223151045Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. **Analyze the bug report content**: The report is about customizing the new tab page in a browser, specifically requesting features similar to iGoogle's personalized homepage.\n\n2. **Identify key themes and keywords**: \"customised\", \"personalised homepage\", \"search engines\", \"news feeds\", \"RSS support\", \"favourited pages\", \"background\" - all of these relate to user interface customization and personalization.\n\n3. **Check for security indicators**: There is no mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, information disclosure, privilege escalation, denial of service, cross-context issues, redirects, sandboxing bypasses, or any other security-related concepts.\n\n4. **Determine the nature of the request**: This is clearly a feature request asking for UI/UX enhancements to allow users to personalize their new tab page experience.\n\n5. **Apply domain knowledge**: According to the classification criteria, Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) encompasses \"feature requests\" and \"UI/UX rendering issues\" - this report falls squarely into both categories.\n\n6. **Final assessment**: There is zero evidence of security relevance in this report. It is purely a feature request for customization options.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about:\n- Customization of the new tab page\n- Wanting to personalize it like iGoogle\n- Choosing search engines to display\n- Adding news feeds/RSS support\n- Adding favorited pages\n- Choosing a background\n\nThis is clearly a feature request for UI/UX customization. There's no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Any security vulnerability\n- Any exploitable condition\n\nThe keywords and concepts here are:\n- \"customised\" / \"personalised\"\n- \"search engines\"\n- \"news feeds\"\n- \"favourited pages\"\n- \"background\"\n\nAll of these relate to user experience and feature requests, which fall under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug).\n\nThere is absolutely no security relevance in this bug report. It's purely a feature request for UI customization options.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 8094060980, "prompt_eval_count": 455, "eval_count": 458}}, {"id": "1933", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 1933 : Chrome seems to crawl my machine 2 problem? 1. When I launch Chrome it starts to scan my machine as if Google Desktop is installed. Don't like this  will not use chrome unless this is removed. Really like browser  better than IE  amp; FireFox because its faster and the layout is simple and maximizes space page can use  would be a pitty not to use it because of the above mentioned. 2. 3.  ?  ?     \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:58:26.54995532Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze what's being reported:\n\n**The Issue:**\nThe user is complaining that Chrome appears to \"scan their machine\" when launched, similar to how Google Desktop behaves. They find this behavior invasive and threaten not to use the browser unless this is removed.\n\n**Analysis:**\n- The complaint is about Chrome's behavior that the user finds invasive or unwanted\n- There's no mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, or any exploitable vulnerability\n- No indicators of confidentiality compromise through an attack vector (like information disclosure to an attacker)\n- No mention of cross-context issues, sandbox escapes, or privilege escalation\n- The \"scanning\" behavior the user is noticing could be Chrome indexing files or checking for certain items - this is more of a user experience/privacy concern about the application's intended or perceived behavior\n- The user is essentially objecting to a feature or behavior they dislike, not reporting a security vulnerability that could be exploited\n\nWhile there's a privacy dimension to the user's concern, this is fundamentally a complaint about unwanted application behavior, not a report of an exploitable security vulnerability. The user wants a feature removed because they find it invasive - this aligns more with a feature request/complaint than a security bug report.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe report is about Chrome scanning the user's machine when launched, which the user compares to Google Desktop behavior. The user doesn't like this behavior and says they won't use Chrome unless this is removed.\n\nLet me consider the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Keywords: \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\", \"feature request\", \"shortcut\", \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context.\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Keywords: \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nThe issue is about Chrome scanning the user's machine. This could potentially be seen as a privacy concern or an unwanted behavior. However:\n\n1. The user is complaining about the browser scanning their machine - this is more of a privacy/concern issue about data collection\n2. There's no indication of memory corruption, buffer overflow, or other traditional security vulnerabilities\n3. The user is essentially making a complaint about unwanted behavior that feels invasive - it's more of a privacy concern\n4. This isn't really a feature request per se, but it's also not clearly a security vulnerability in the traditional sense (like memory corruption, code execution, etc.)\n5. The concern is about the browser's behavior scanning the local machine - while this has privacy implications, it's more about unwanted functionality than an exploitable vulnerability\n\nThe concern about \"scanning my machine\" could potentially relate to information disclosure (a security concern about confidentiality), but the report doesn't describe:\n- Any exploitability\n- Any vulnerability that an attacker could use\n- Memory corruption\n- Code execution\n- Cross-context issues\n\nThis seems more like a user complaint about Chrome's behavior (possibly related to file indexing or scanning features) that they find invasive, rather than a report of a security vulnerability that could be exploited by an attacker.\n\nWhile there's a privacy angle, this doesn't fit the pattern of a security bug as defined - it's more of a behavioral concern/complaint about the application's design rather than a vulnerability.\n\nI'll classify this as a Non-Security Bug (Class 0)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 19053694208, "prompt_eval_count": 427, "eval_count": 787}}, {"id": "3798", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 3798 : Press Tab to search Google mode: doesn't work with some Chinese IMEs 5      nbsp;   xlyuan@chromium.org    Build: 0.3.154.8 (Official Build 3988) OS: Traditional Chinese Vista or XP Steps: 1. Launch Chrome 2. Type google.com at Ominibox 3.  quot;Press Tab to search Google quot; should show up now 4. Press  quot;Tab quot; then 5. Switch to  quot;Microsoft New Phonetic quot; IME 6. Try to type something at the Ominibox Result: Nothing happened after hit keyboard Expected: Correct candidate should be displayed Notes: 1. Change  quot;Keyboard Layout quot; of Microsoft New Phonetic from  quot;Standard quot; to  quot;Tanwan Pinyin quot;  Still it doesn't work 2. Under same condition  MS Pinyin IME 2007 for Simplified Chinese works For Traditional Chinese.jpg 193 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download For Simplifed Chinese.jpg 194 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:58:28.472905091Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether it contains security implications.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe bug describes an issue where:\n1. When using Chrome's Omnibox in \"Press Tab to search Google\" mode\n2. Switching to the \"Microsoft New Phonetic\" IME (Input Method Editor) for Traditional Chinese\n3. Keyboard input doesn't register - nothing happens\n\n**Key observations:**\n- This is a **functional defect** with IME input handling in a specific Omnibox mode\n- The issue is about **user experience and input compatibility** - the IME candidates aren't being displayed\n- There is **no mention** of memory corruption, buffer overflow, heap overwrite, information disclosure, or any exploitable condition\n- No security-related keywords are present (no \"memory leak\", \"cross-context\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", privilege bypass, sandbox escape, etc.)\n- The bug affects functionality but doesn't compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability in a security sense\n- It's a compatibility/interaction issue between Chrome's Omnibox and a specific Chinese IME\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThis is a straightforward functional bug related to IME compatibility. It doesn't involve any vulnerability that could be exploited to compromise the system. The issue is about a feature not working as expected for certain input methods, which falls clearly under functional/UI defects.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report describes an issue where:\n1. When using Chrome's Omnibox with \"Press Tab to search Google\" mode\n2. Switching to \"Microsoft New Phonetic\" IME (Input Method Editor) for Traditional Chinese\n3. Typing doesn't work - nothing happens when hitting the keyboard\n\nThis is essentially a functionality issue with Input Method Editors (IMEs) not working correctly in a specific mode of Chrome's Omnibox. \n\nLet me check against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- This is a functional defect - the IME input doesn't work as expected\n- It's about user experience with input methods\n- There's no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, or denial of service\n- No security-related keywords like \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"memory leak\", \"cross-context\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- None present in this report\n\nThis is clearly a functional bug related to IME compatibility with Chrome's Omnibox search mode. It affects the user's ability to input Chinese characters using a specific IME. There's no security implication here - no exploitation potential, no memory issues, no information disclosure, no privilege escalation.\n\nThe bug is about a specific input method not working in a specific context, which is a usability/functional issue, not a security vulnerability.\n\nTherefore, this should be classified as a Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 16302122509, "prompt_eval_count": 569, "eval_count": 612}}, {"id": "13", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 13 : Closing last tab also closes window 72 problem? 1. Have a window with one tab 2. Close that tab  ? The tab returns to the New Tab page or whatever the user set.  ? The window closes. Please make a setting that allows the user to keep the window open when closing last tab. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:58:39.389413925Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether it involves a security vulnerability or is a non-security issue.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe report describes:\n- A behavior where closing the last tab in a window also closes the window itself\n- The user expects the browser to return to a \"New Tab\" page instead\n- This is explicitly a feature request: \"Please make a setting that allows the user to keep the window open when closing last tab\"\n\n**Checking against classification criteria:**\n\n- **No security impact**: There is no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, sandbox bypass, or any exploitable condition\n- **No security keywords**: None of the indicators like \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", or \"advisory\" appear\n- **Feature request nature**: The user is requesting a configuration option/setting to change application behavior, which falls squarely under UI/UX and feature request categories\n- **No confidentiality, integrity, or availability impact**: The window closing when the last tab is closed is a design choice, not a vulnerability\n\nThis is a straightforward usability/feature request regarding browser tab behavior\u2014completely unrelated to security.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Issue: Closing the last tab also closes the window\n- Steps to reproduce: 1. Have a window with one tab, 2. Close that tab\n- Expected behavior: The tab returns to the New Tab page or whatever the user set\n- Actual behavior: The window closes\n- Request: Please make a setting that allows the user to keep the window open when closing last tab\n\nThis is essentially a feature request / behavior change request. The user wants the browser to remain open even when the last tab is closed, similar to how some browsers return to a \"New Tab\" page instead of closing the entire window.\n\nLet me check against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- This is a feature request\n- It's about user experience/behavior\n- Keywords like \"setting\", \"user set\" relate to user experience\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- No memory corruption\n- No information disclosure\n- No denial of service\n- No memory leak, buffer overflow, heap overwrite\n- No cross-context issues\n- No javascript: or redirect issues\n- No privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n\nThis is clearly a feature request about browser behavior - specifically about whether closing the last tab should close the window or keep it open with a new tab page. There's no security implication here at all.\n\nThe classification is clearly Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 10633846161, "prompt_eval_count": 393, "eval_count": 572}}, {"id": "6873", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 6873 : Middle-clicking address bar = mouse doesn't hover/click right until you click again  lsaquo; Prev 13565 of 14146 Next  rsaquo; 2      nbsp;   pkasting@chromium.org    * Start Chrome * Middle-click in the address bar Now try hovering the tab or window close buttons  or other UI components. Nothing hovers. Any click  including a middle click  including one on the address bar again  will get you out of this state  and until then _no_ clicks on anything work (just like hover doesn't work). I don't know  maybe the edit is somehow capturing the mouse on middle click or something? WTF. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:58:41.830232776Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether it involves a security vulnerability or a functional/UI defect.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe bug describes:\n- Middle-clicking the address bar causes hover and click functionality to stop working\n- UI components (tab close buttons, window close buttons) don't respond to hover\n- The mouse appears to be \"captured\" by the edit field\n- A single click releases the state and restores normal behavior\n\n**Evaluating against classification criteria:**\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug) indicators - NOT present:**\n- No memory corruption (buffer overflow, heap overwrite)\n- No information disclosure\n- No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n- No cross-context vulnerability\n- Not a DoS in the security sense - this is a temporary UI state that the user can easily escape with one click\n- No malicious exploitation potential\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) indicators - PRESENT:**\n- This is a UI/UX interaction issue - hover states and click responses\n- Keywords match: \"hover\", \"click\", \"UI components\" - all relate to visual display and user experience\n- This is a functional defect in mouse event handling/state machine\n- The speculation about \"capturing the mouse\" refers to a standard UI programming concept (mouse capture), not a security vulnerability\n- There's no exploitable security impact - just annoying behavior\n\nThe bug is a classic UI state machine error where the address bar incorrectly captures mouse input on middle-click and doesn't release it properly. It's an interaction/UX bug, not a security vulnerability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n1. Middle-clicking in the address bar causes the mouse hover and click functionality to stop working\n2. UI components like tab close buttons or window close buttons don't respond to hover\n3. No clicks work until you click again (any click, including middle click)\n4. The user speculates that maybe the edit is capturing the mouse on middle click\n\nLet me evaluate this against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- This is a UI/UX issue - hover states and click responses in the UI\n- This is about mouse interaction behavior - visual display and user experience\n- \"hover\", \"click\", \"UI components\" are keywords related to user interface behavior\n- This appears to be a functional defect in how the address bar handles middle-click events\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- Memory corruption? No evidence\n- Information disclosure? No evidence\n- Denial of Service? The UI becomes unresponsive temporarily, but this is more of a mouse capture issue than a DoS vulnerability\n- Buffer overflow, heap overwrite, cross-context? No evidence\n- Privilege/sandboxing bypass? No evidence\n\nThe bug is about a mouse capture issue where the address bar's edit field appears to capture the mouse on middle-click, causing hover and click events to not work properly until another click releases the capture. This is a UI interaction bug, not a security vulnerability.\n\nWhile one might argue that the UI becoming unresponsive could be considered a form of denial of service, this is more accurately described as a UI state machine bug where mouse events are being incorrectly handled. The user can easily recover by clicking again, and there's no evidence of memory corruption, privilege escalation, or data exposure.\n\nThis falls squarely into the \"functional defect\" and \"UI/UX\" category of Class 0 (Non-Security Bug).\n\nFinal answer: (A) Non-Security Bug"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 27199011662, "prompt_eval_count": 471, "eval_count": 743}}, {"id": "9765", "ground_truth": "1", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 9765 : Unlimited memory consumption with XMLHttpRequest  lsaquo; Prev 759 of 14153 Next  rsaquo; 2 problem? 1. Start a tool to monitor memory usage of a process (for example  Process Explorer) 2. Load attached  quot;TableRefresh.html quot; in a Chrome tab 3. Observe the memory consumption (Working Set) rising every second  ? Memory usage should stay at the same level  since the document loaded with XMLHttpRequest replaces itself every time.  ? Memory usage keeps rising. It will keep rising at least up to 1GB  at which point I decided to abort the process. I don't know whether it is a leak or fragmentation or an unexpected reference somewhere that prevents the memory being reused.     I let  quot;TableRefresh.html quot; and  quot;table.xml quot; be served by an Apache server on my local development machine. Any web server should work  as long as  quot;table.xml quot; is served with an XML mime type (text/xml or application/xhtml+xml). The speed at which memory usage increases is determined by the amount of data loaded  see attached  quot;tablegen.py quot; to generate different size tables. If you comment out the entire contents of the  quot;if (req.status == 200) quot; block  memory use still increases with every request  although much slower than when replacing the table. This suggests that the XMLHttpRequest itself is the cause of at least some the memory consumption. I tried explicitly deleting  quot;req quot; and  quot;req.onreadystatechange quot; when the request is done  to help garbage collection by breaking reference cycles. This did not make a difference. Konqueror 4.2.2 on Linux has this same issue. Safari 4 beta on Windows does not. Also in Firefox 3 on Windows the issue does not occur. The test case does not work in IE7 because that browser lacks document.importNode() and I do not have IE8 installed. TableRefresh.html 943 bytes  nbsp; Download table.xml 17.7 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download tablegen.py 373 bytes  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:58:44.576437433Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\n**Key Details from the Report:**\n1. The issue involves \"Unlimited memory consumption with XMLHttpRequest\"\n2. Memory usage keeps rising every second, at least up to 1GB\n3. The memory is never released - even explicitly deleting references doesn't help\n4. This can be triggered by a webpage repeatedly making XMLHttpRequest calls\n5. The XMLHttpRequest itself appears to be the cause of the memory consumption\n\n**Security Analysis:**\nAccording to the domain knowledge, Class 1 (Security Bug) involves vulnerabilities that compromise **availability**, such as Denial of Service (DoS). The distinctive keywords include \"**memory leak**\" and \"**exhaustion**\".\n\nThis bug report describes:\n- **Unlimited/unbounded memory consumption** - a form of resource **exhaustion**\n- A webpage can cause memory usage to grow without limit\n- This is essentially a **Denial of Service (DoS)** vulnerability - a malicious webpage could consume all available system memory, potentially crashing the browser or making the entire system unresponsive\n- The word \"leak\" is even mentioned in the report itself\n\nThis isn't just a functional bug - it has direct security implications because:\n1. An attacker can craft a webpage that exploits this to exhaust system memory\n2. This compromises the **availability** of the system\n3. Resource exhaustion is explicitly listed as a security vulnerability type\n\n**Answer: (B) Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe issue describes:\n1. Unlimited memory consumption with XMLHttpRequest\n2. Memory usage keeps rising (at least up to 1GB) when repeatedly making XMLHttpRequest calls\n3. The memory is not being freed/released properly\n4. This occurs in Chrome and Konqueror, but not in Firefox or Safari\n\nAccording to the domain knowledge provided:\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nKey points from the bug report:\n- \"Unlimited memory consumption\" - this relates to resource exhaustion\n- Memory usage \"will keep rising at least up to 1GB\" - this is a memory exhaustion issue\n- This is essentially a Denial of Service vulnerability - a webpage can consume unlimited memory, potentially crashing the browser or making the system unusable\n\nThe domain knowledge specifically mentions:\n- \"exhaustion\" as a keyword for security bugs\n- Denial of Service (DoS) as a security vulnerability\n\nThis is clearly a memory exhaustion / DoS vulnerability. A malicious webpage could use this to consume all available memory on a victim's system, causing the browser or even the entire system to become unresponsive.\n\nThis should be classified as a Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 17779797095, "prompt_eval_count": 784, "eval_count": 617}}, {"id": "19315", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 19315 : All keyboard shortcuts fail  if focus is on omnibox 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   venkataramana@chromium.org    ToT(r 23365) This is my first starter bug on Linux :) While focus is on omnibox  try to execute any keyboard shortcuts (ctr+t/ctrl+n..etc). All them fail. PS: Works fine on Mac. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:58:45.668161431Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether it describes a security vulnerability or a non-security issue.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Keyboard shortcuts (Ctrl+T, Ctrl+N, etc.) fail when focus is on the omnibox (address bar)\n- Thi