- None present in this bug report\n- No mention of memory leaks, buffer overflows, heap overwrites, cross-context issues, exhaustion, javascript:, redirect, advisory, or privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n\nThis is clearly a functional/UI bug where keyboard shortcuts aren't being processed correctly when the omnibox has focus. This is a usability issue, not a security vulnerability. There's no indication that this could be exploited to compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability of the system.\n\nThe classification is clearly Class 0 - Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 31337500416, "prompt_eval_count": 418, "eval_count": 592}}, {"id": "5056", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 5056 : Chinese portal site 163.com: Text wraps to the next line incorrectly  lsaquo; Prev 409 of 14151 Next  rsaquo; 2      nbsp;   xlyuan@chromium.org    Build: 0.4.154.31 (Official Build 6264) OS: Any Other Browsers: Safari: OK Firefox3: OK IE7: OK Steps: 1. Launch Chrome 2. Go to http://www.163.com/ 3. Find text  quot;\u7f01\u5fd4\u7979\u9483\u6fc8\u6bca\u6d94?quot; 4. Observe Result: The last character  quot;\u6d94?quot; wraps to the next line incorrectly Expected: Should not wrap here Notes: This should not be a regression Text_Wraps_Incorrectly.jpg 428 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:58:49.856519437Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze the nature of the issue described.\n\n**Analysis:**\n- The bug describes text wrapping incorrectly on a Chinese portal site (163.com)\n- Specifically, the last character of Chinese text wraps to the next line when it shouldn't\n- Other browsers (Safari, Firefox3, IE7) render this correctly\n- This is purely a visual display/layout issue\n\n**Checking against classification criteria:**\n\nClass 0 (Non-Security Bug) indicators:\n- \u2705 This is a UI/UX rendering issue (text display)\n- \u2705 This is a layout problem (incorrect line wrapping)\n- \u2705 Keywords align with visual display issues (\"wraps\", \"line\", text rendering)\n\nClass 1 (Security Bug) indicators:\n- \u274c No memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap overwrite\n- \u274c No information disclosure or cross-context issues\n- \u274c No denial of service, exhaustion, or privilege bypass\n- \u274c No exploitable security impact mentioned\n\nThe bug is clearly about incorrect text layout/rendering behavior - a visual display issue where Chinese characters wrap to the next line incorrectly. There is no indication whatsoever of any security vulnerability or exploitable impact.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about:\n- Issue 5056: Chinese portal site 163.com has text wrapping incorrectly\n- The text \"\u7f01\u5fd4\u7979\u9483\u6fc8\u6bca\u6d94?\" wraps incorrectly - the last character \"\u6d94?\" wraps to the next line\n- This is a visual/layout issue on a specific website\n- Other browsers (Safari, Firefox3, IE7) render it correctly\n- The expected behavior is that the text should not wrap there\n\nThis is clearly a text rendering/layout issue - a CSS or text layout bug where Chinese characters are wrapping incorrectly. \n\nLooking at the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability.\n\nThis bug fits perfectly into Class 0 because:\n1. It's a UI/UX rendering issue (text wrapping incorrectly)\n2. It's a layout problem\n3. There's no security impact mentioned\n4. Keywords like \"wraps\", \"text\", \"line\" are related to visual display/layout\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators like \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", privilege/sandboxing bypasses - none of these are present in this bug report.\n\nThis is clearly a non-security bug - it's a visual/text layout issue."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 10195979265, "prompt_eval_count": 517, "eval_count": 615}}, {"id": "9142", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 9142 : complement and comments 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   sunseeker.eddy72    (Describe security bug here  with as much details and steps to reproduce as possible. Security bugs are visible only to the reporter and to project members.) i like google chrome for its simplicity and ease of use. and most importantly  the start up of the browser was fast   and surfing to website is a breeze ... nice work   google team ... as compared to other browser like internet explorer  opera  firefox  and safari  it is definitely a good browser that stands out on its own. although  in some cases  there seems to be lack of features found in other browsers  it is however good enough for surfing and general browsing. although its good to have more useful features and configurable option  however  to add more of it might impair the google chrome slower and less responsive  and more error prone ? to conclude  i believe google chrome  with the rest of google service like google search  blog   reader etc  stands out to be one of the best free service option for users in the world... cheers google :) :) :) nice work : ) :) :) \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:59:02.114504917Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze its content for security relevance.\n\n**Analysis:**\n- The title is \"complement and comments\" - suggesting user feedback, not a bug report\n- The content contains the security bug template prompt (\"Describe security bug here...\"), but this appears to be the default template text that the reporter did not replace\n- The actual content is entirely a user praising Google Chrome for:\n  - Simplicity and ease of use\n  - Fast startup and browsing speed\n  - Favorable comparison to other browsers (IE, Opera, Firefox, Safari)\n  - General compliments about Google services\n- There is **no vulnerability described** - no memory corruption, no information disclosure, no DoS, no privilege escalation, no exploit steps\n- The only technical mention is a speculative concern that adding more features \"might impair\" Chrome's performance, but this is not a reported bug or security issue\n\n**Conclusion:** This is not a security bug. It's user feedback/compliment that was mistakenly submitted through a security bug reporting form. There is zero evidence of any vulnerability compromising confidentiality, integrity, or availability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is titled \"Issue 9142 : complement and comments\" and contains:\n\n1. A template prompt asking to \"Describe security bug here with as much details and steps to reproduce as possible. Security bugs are visible only to the reporter and to project members.\"\n\n2. The actual content is just a user praising Google Chrome for:\n- Its simplicity and ease of use\n- Fast startup\n- Fast browsing/surfing\n- Comparing it favorably to IE, Opera, Firefox, Safari\n- Acknowledging some lack of features but still good for general browsing\n- Mentioning it might slow down if more features are added\n- Praising Google services overall\n\nThis is clearly NOT a security bug. This is:\n- A compliment/feedback message from a user\n- No vulnerability described\n- No security impact mentioned\n- No memory corruption, buffer overflow, information disclosure, or any security-related issue\n- No exploit or steps to reproduce a security issue\n\nThe user appears to have used the security bug reporting form to submit a general comment/compliment about Chrome. This falls under \"Non-Security Bug\" - or more accurately, it's not a bug at all, just user feedback.\n\nAccording to the classification criteria:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact.\n\nThis report contains no security vulnerability evidence whatsoever. It's just praise and general feedback.\n\nAnswer: (A) Non-Security Bug"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 17212432936, "prompt_eval_count": 570, "eval_count": 570}}, {"id": "8640", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 8640 : http://www.flickr.com: Chrome cannot display large photos 2      nbsp;   karel.uyttendaele    Chrome Version : 2.0.169.0 URLs (if applicable) : http://www.flickr.com/photos/kuyttendaele/3344233195/sizes/o/    : Safari 3: unknown Firefox 3: unknown IE 7: OK  ? Chrome is displaying the broken bitmap icon     chrome-bug.jpg 203 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:59:05.617692977Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze the key details:\n\n**Issue Summary:**\n- Chrome cannot display large photos on Flickr\n- Shows a \"broken bitmap icon\" instead of the actual photo\n- IE 7 works correctly\n- This is a display/rendering problem\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe report describes a **visual rendering issue** where Chrome fails to properly display large images, showing a broken image icon instead. The keywords and patterns here are:\n\n- \"cannot display\" \u2192 indicates a rendering/display problem\n- \"broken bitmap icon\" \u2192 visual rendering failure\n- Comparison with other browsers working correctly \u2192 functional compatibility issue\n\n**Checking against security indicators:**\n- No memory corruption (no \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"memory leak\")\n- No information disclosure\n- No Denial of Service (no \"exhaustion\", no system-wide impact)\n- No cross-context issues, sandboxing bypasses, or privilege escalation\n- No redirect vulnerabilities or javascript: protocol issues\n\nThis is a straightforward **image rendering/decoding defect** \u2014 Chrome's image handling code fails to properly decode and display large photos. While image parsing bugs *can* sometimes be security-relevant (e.g., buffer overflows in decoders), this report only describes a visual symptom (broken icon) with no evidence of memory corruption, crashes, or exploitable behavior. The report frames it purely as a display problem.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Chrome cannot display large photos on Flickr\n- Chrome is displaying the broken bitmap icon instead of the photo\n- This appears to be a rendering/display issue\n- The URL is a photo on Flickr\n- Other browsers (IE 7) can display it correctly\n\nKey indicators:\n1. \"cannot display\" - this is a visual/display issue\n2. \"broken bitmap icon\" - this indicates a rendering problem\n3. The issue is about photos not rendering correctly\n\nAccording to the domain knowledge provided:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): \"Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems...\"\n- Keywords like \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\" are indicators of non-security bugs\n- This is clearly a display/rendering issue\n\nThere is no indication of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Any exploitable security vulnerability\n\nThis is purely a visual rendering bug where Chrome fails to display certain large images. It's a functional defect related to image rendering, not a security vulnerability.\n\nThe answer should be (A) Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 15200639806, "prompt_eval_count": 444, "eval_count": 545}}, {"id": "15252", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 15252 : [picasaweb.google.com]: Sometimes  Picasa Web doesn't change the picture  lsaquo; Prev 1093 of 14161 Next  rsaquo; 2      nbsp;   findit.role    URL: http://picasaweb.google.com Browser: Chrome 2.0.172.33 [Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US) AppleWebKit/530.5 (KHTML  like Gecko) Chrome/2.0.172.33 Safari/530.5] Severity: Certain site functionality doesn't work in Google Chrome What is wrong? Sometimes  you are seeing a large Picasa Web Album and  suddenly  you click on the  quot;next quot; button and it stays stuck in the same picture. Does it work properly in other browsers? Which did you test? Yes. FF and IE. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:59:09.74307096Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\n**Bug Summary:**\n- Issue with Picasa Web Albums where clicking the \"next\" button sometimes doesn't change the picture\n- The album navigation gets stuck on the current picture\n- Works properly in Firefox and IE, but not in Chrome\n- Categorized as \"Certain site functionality doesn't work in Google Chrome\"\n\n**Security Analysis:**\n- This is a navigation/UI functional defect in a web application\n- No indicators of memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap issues\n- No information disclosure or confidentiality breach\n- No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n- No denial of service (resource exhaustion)\n- No cross-context or javascript: URL issues\n- The bug is simply a broken \"next\" button functionality\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThis is a straightforward functional defect where a site feature (picture navigation) doesn't work properly in Chrome. It's a compatibility/UI issue with no security implications whatsoever.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Issue with Picasa Web Albums (picasaweb.google.com)\n- Sometimes when clicking the \"next\" button in a large Picasa Web Album, it stays stuck on the same picture and doesn't change to the next picture\n- The issue is about site functionality not working properly in Google Chrome\n- It works properly in Firefox and IE\n\nThis is clearly a functional defect - a UI/navigation issue where clicking \"next\" doesn't properly navigate to the next picture. This is a compatibility or rendering issue with Chrome, not a security vulnerability.\n\nThere are no security indicators mentioned:\n- No memory corruption\n- No information disclosure\n- No denial of service\n- No privilege escalation\n- No sandbox bypass\n- No buffer overflow\n- No cross-context issues\n- No javascript: URLs\n- No redirects\n\nThis is a straightforward functional bug where a website feature doesn't work properly in a specific browser. It falls clearly under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) as it's a functional defect lacking exploitable security impact.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 7330479699, "prompt_eval_count": 517, "eval_count": 443}}, {"id": "2175", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 2175 : Uses huge amount of CPU 2 problem? 1. open url http://mobile.younet.com/files/20/20149.html  ? The cpu usage should be low.  ? But chrome cost 100% cpu usage. With firefox 3.01  there about 20% cpu usage. And when the tab is not active the cpu will drop to 0. But with chrome  there are always 100% cpu  no matter the tab is active or not. It seems that the chrome still run the non-active tab. And the flash plugin use much more cpu than firefox \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:59:11.760770318Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze the security implications:\n\n**Key observations from the report:**\n1. A specific URL causes Chrome to use 100% CPU\n2. Firefox handles the same page with only ~20% CPU\n3. Chrome doesn't throttle CPU for inactive tabs\n4. Flash plugin consumes excessive CPU\n\n**Analysis against classification criteria:**\n\n- The report describes high CPU usage, which could theoretically relate to resource exhaustion/DoS\n- However, the keyword \"exhaustion\" in the security criteria refers to intentional, exploitable resource exhaustion attacks\n- This report reads as a **performance/efficiency complaint** - comparing Chrome's behavior to Firefox\n- There is no mention of:\n  - Memory corruption or buffer overflow\n  - Privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n  - Information disclosure\n  - Malicious exploitation or attack vector\n  - Any security advisory context\n- The core issue is that Chrome's tab throttling and Flash handling are inefficient compared to Firefox\n- This is a **functional defect** in resource management, not an exploitable vulnerability\n\n**Conclusion:** While CPU consumption issues *can* sometimes be security-relevant (DoS), this report clearly describes a performance/behavioral defect where Chrome handles the same content less efficiently than Firefox. There is no clear evidence of security impact or exploitability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n1. Opening a specific URL causes Chrome to use 100% CPU\n2. Firefox only uses about 20% CPU for the same URL\n3. When the tab is not active in Firefox, CPU drops to 0\n4. In Chrome, the CPU stays at 100% even when the tab is not active\n5. The flash plugin uses much more CPU than Firefox\n\nThis is essentially reporting:\n1. High CPU usage/usage problem\n2. Chrome not throttling background tabs properly\n3. Flash plugin consuming excessive CPU\n\nNow, let me evaluate this against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nKey considerations:\n- High CPU usage could potentially be a Denial of Service (DoS) issue\n- The keyword \"exhaustion\" is mentioned as a security indicator\n- However, the bug report seems to be about a performance issue with a specific webpage and Flash plugin\n- The 100% CPU usage is more of a resource management/performance issue\n- There's no mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, or typical security vulnerability indicators\n- The issue seems to be that Chrome doesn't properly throttle background tabs, which is more of a design/behavioral issue\n\nWhile CPU exhaustion could theoretically be a DoS vector, this particular report reads more like a performance/efficiency complaint rather than a security vulnerability. The user is comparing Chrome's behavior to Firefox and noting it's worse, but not indicating this is exploitable.\n\nHowever, I need to consider: Could this be considered a DoS vulnerability? If a malicious website can cause 100% CPU usage even when the tab is not active, that could be a denial-of-service concern. But the report doesn't seem to frame it that way - it's more of a \"this website uses too much CPU\" complaint.\n\nThe instructions say to \"heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance\" and \"when in doubt, prefer the safer option.\"\n\nThe safer option here is actually non-security, since the report doesn't clearly indicate a security vulnerability - it's a performance complaint about CPU usage with a specific website. There's no mention of this being exploitable, no mention of security implications, and the tone is more about Chrome being inefficient compared to Firefox.\n\nI'll classify this as a Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 25876483890, "prompt_eval_count": 450, "eval_count": 912}}, {"id": "717", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 717 : Firefox not recognised by Bookmark Import Feature 4      nbsp;   DaanDagevos      : 0.2.149.27 (1583) When trying to import my bookmarks  Chrome only gives me the option  quot;Microsoft Internet Explorer quot;. I've got Firefox v3.0.1 installed and working without any problems. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:59:17.238086903Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether it describes a security vulnerability or a functional defect.\n\n**Analysis:**\n- The issue describes Firefox not being recognized by the Bookmark Import Feature\n- The user can only see \"Microsoft Internet Explorer\" as an import option, despite having Firefox installed\n- This is a functional limitation where the software fails to detect/recognize Firefox as an available browser for bookmark import\n\n**Security Assessment:**\n- No memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap overwrite is mentioned\n- No information disclosure or confidentiality breach\n- No denial of service or availability impact\n- No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n- No cross-context issues or exploitable redirect\n- This is simply a missing feature/detection failure in the import functionality\n\nThis is clearly a functional defect where the bookmark import feature lacks proper browser detection for Firefox. It does not compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability in any exploitable way.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about:\n- Issue 717: Firefox not recognized by Bookmark Import Feature\n- The user is trying to import bookmarks from Firefox\n- The import feature only shows \"Microsoft Internet Explorer\" as an option\n- Firefox v3.0.1 is installed and working\n\nThis is clearly a functional defect - the bookmark import feature doesn't recognize Firefox as an available browser to import bookmarks from. This is a feature limitation or functional bug where the software doesn't detect/correctly identify Firefox as a browser from which bookmarks can be imported.\n\nLet me check against the classification criteria:\n\nClass 0 (Non-Security Bug): Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact.\n\nClass 1 (Security Bug): Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS).\n\nThis bug report is about a missing feature/functional defect in the bookmark import functionality. There's no indication of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Any security vulnerability\n- Memory leaks, buffer overflow, heap overwrite\n- Cross-context issues\n- javascript: or redirect issues\n- Privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n\nThis is purely a functional issue where the software doesn't recognize Firefox as an import source. This falls clearly under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug).\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 5360669378, "prompt_eval_count": 400, "eval_count": 518}}, {"id": "5395", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 5395 : Cannot right click on Exchange server webmail  lsaquo; Prev 3536 of 14151 Next  rsaquo; 2 problem? 1. log into an exchange server 2. got to your inbox 3. right click an email - in IE the correct right click menu opens  in Chrome - a standard menu opens up  ? An outlook based options menu will open  ? A standard options menu opens \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:59:41.058312182Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze the issue described:\n\n**Issue Summary:**\n- When using Exchange server webmail in Chrome, right-clicking on an email shows the browser's standard context menu instead of the expected Outlook-based custom context menu.\n- In Internet Explorer, the correct custom right-click menu appears as expected.\n\n**Analysis:**\n1. This is a browser compatibility issue where the custom context menu (likely implemented via JavaScript's `oncontextmenu` event) is not properly overriding the browser's default context menu in Chrome.\n2. The problem is purely a UI/UX behavior difference between browsers - a functional defect.\n3. There is no indication of:\n   - Memory corruption or buffer overflow\n   - Information disclosure\n   - Denial of Service\n   - Privilege escalation\n   - Any exploitable security vulnerability\n\n4. This falls squarely under UI/UX rendering issues and functional defects, which are classified as Class 0 (Non-Security Bug).\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report describes an issue where:\n1. User logs into an Exchange server webmail\n2. Goes to their inbox\n3. Right-clicks an email\n- In IE: The correct right-click menu opens (Outlook-based options menu)\n- In Chrome: A standard browser menu opens instead of the Outlook-based options menu\n\nThis is clearly a UI/UX issue where the custom right-click context menu is not working properly in Chrome. The web application is supposed to show a custom context menu (Outlook-based options) when right-clicking, but instead, the browser's default context menu appears.\n\nThis is a functional/UI defect - the custom context menu handling isn't working correctly in Chrome. There's no indication of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Privilege escalation\n- Any security vulnerability\n\nThis is simply a browser compatibility issue where the web application's custom right-click menu isn't overriding the browser's default context menu in Chrome.\n\nBased on the classification criteria:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems\n- This clearly falls under UI/UX rendering issue / functional defect\n\nThe answer should be (A) Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 23405783994, "prompt_eval_count": 417, "eval_count": 477}}, {"id": "21958", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 21958 : Flaky layout test LayoutTests/svg/hixie/error/012.xml  lsaquo; Prev 7343 of 14162 Next  rsaquo; 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   tyosh...@chromium.org    LayoutTests/svg/hixie/error/012.xml is flaky on Builder Webkit Linux (dbg)(3) and Builder Webkit Mac10.5 (dbg)(3) \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T21:59:51.282532637Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze the key details:\n\n1. **Issue Description**: A \"flaky layout test\" - `LayoutTests/svg/hixie/error/012.xml`\n2. **Context**: The test is inconsistently passing/failing on specific build configurations (Webkit Linux and Mac debug builders)\n3. **Keywords Present**: \"layout test\", \"flaky\"\n\n**Analysis**:\n- This is about a layout test that exhibits inconsistent behavior (flakiness), which is a test reliability/infrastructure issue\n- The keyword \"layout\" is explicitly listed in the domain knowledge as a distinctive indicator for Class 0 (Non-Security Bug), associated with \"visual display\" and \"layout problems\"\n- There are no security-related indicators present (no memory corruption, no information disclosure, no privilege escalation, no DoS, no buffer overflow, etc.)\n- The \"error\" in the path name (`/svg/hixie/error/`) refers to the test directory name, not a security vulnerability\n- Flaky tests are common development/quality issues, not security vulnerabilities\n\nThis is clearly a functional/testing infrastructure issue with no exploitable security impact.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Issue 21958: Flaky layout test LayoutTests/svg/hixie/error/012.xml\n- It's flaky on specific builders (Webkit Linux dbg and Webkit Mac10.5 dbg)\n- A layout test is failing inconsistently\n\nKey observations:\n1. This is about a \"flaky layout test\" - a test that sometimes passes and sometimes fails\n2. The test is related to SVG/layout (\"LayoutTests/svg/hixie/error/012.xml\")\n3. The keywords here are \"layout test\" and \"flaky\"\n\nAccording to the domain knowledge:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) includes \"UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems\"\n- Keywords like \"layout\" and \"CSS\" are distinctive indicators of non-security bugs\n- There's no mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, information disclosure, DoS, or any security-related keywords\n\nThis is clearly a non-security bug. It's about a flaky layout test that is failing inconsistently on certain build configurations. This is a functional/testing reliability issue, not a security vulnerability.\n\nFinal answer: (A) Non-Security Bug"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 9792054449, "prompt_eval_count": 427, "eval_count": 502}}, {"id": "4221", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 4221 : HANG [winqual] MsgWaitForMultipleObjectsEx (possible hookers involved) 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   cpu@chromium.org    version 0.3.154.9 Windows XP Version 2600 (Service Pack 3) MP (2 procs) Free x86 compatible Process Uptime: 0 days 0:01:20.000 Hang in UI thread: ntdll+0xe4f4 user32+0x95f9  lt;---calls MsgWaitForMultipleObjectsEx chrome_1000000!base::MessagePumpForUI::WaitForWork(void)+0x2c chrome_1000000!base::MessagePumpForUI::DoRunLoop(void)+0x6a chrome_1000000!base::MessagePumpWin::RunWithDispatcher(class base::MessagePump::Delegate * delegate = 0x002db9ae  class base::MessagePumpWin::Dispatcher * dispatcher = 0x01e44448)+0x38 chrome_1000000!MessageLoop::RunInternal(void)+0x80 chrome_1000000!MessageLoop::RunHandler(void)+0x