port is about:\n- Files in the download shelf remain flat on mouse-over unlike tabs\n- The issue is that when you mouse over a downloaded file in the download shelf, it doesn't change hue like tabs do\n- This is a UI/UX issue about visual feedback on hover\n- The suggested fix is either to highlight the item on mouse-over or to require a double-click to open it\n\nThis is clearly a UI/UX rendering issue - specifically about visual feedback (hover state/hue change) for user interaction. There's no security implication here at all:\n- No memory corruption\n- No information disclosure\n- No denial of service\n- No privilege escalation\n- No cross-context issues\n- No exploitable vulnerability\n\nThis is purely about user experience - whether a visual indicator (hover effect) appears when mousing over items in the download shelf.\n\nAccording to the classification criteria:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests - keywords include \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\", \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"\n- This bug clearly falls into UI/UX rendering issues\n\nThis should be classified as Class 0 - Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 9602616826, "prompt_eval_count": 473, "eval_count": 476}}, {"id": "7404", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 7404 : Do not close the browser if the last tab is closed 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   munjal@chromium.org    One annoying thing that often happens to me when using Chrome is that if I have just one tab open and want to close that but not close the browser then I have to first open a new tab and then close the tab. it will be nice if we can open the new tab page when the user closes the last tab. We can still close the browser when the user presses X on the browser window  but if he presses X on the last tab we can either have an empty window or open new tab page. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:02:46.695607614Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, it's clearly a **feature request** about browser tab behavior. The user wants Chrome to open a new tab page when the last tab is closed, rather than closing the entire browser. \n\nKey indicators:\n- \"it will be nice if we can\" - this is a feature request/UX enhancement\n- The issue is about user convenience (\"annoying thing\") regarding tab/window behavior\n- No security keywords like memory corruption, buffer overflow, information disclosure, DoS, sandbox bypass, etc.\n- No mention of confidentiality, integrity, or availability being compromised\n\nThis is purely a UI/UX behavior request with no security implications whatsoever.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe issue is about the behavior of Chrome browser when the last tab is closed. The user is requesting a feature/behavior change where:\n- Currently: When the last tab is closed, the browser closes\n- Requested: When the last tab is closed, open a new tab page instead of closing the browser\n\nThis is clearly a feature request about user experience/behavior. There's no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Cross-context issues\n- Sandbox bypasses\n- Any security vulnerability\n\nThe keywords used are about:\n- \"annoying thing\" - UX complaint\n- \"nice if we can\" - feature request\n- \"new tab page\" - UI behavior\n\nThis falls squarely under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) as a feature request related to user experience.\n\nAnswer: (A) Non-Security Bug"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 6087872066, "prompt_eval_count": 469, "eval_count": 338}}, {"id": "5181", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defect