d up but clicking \"Select ALL\" still works\n3. After refreshing or reloading, it returns to normal\n4. The user has noticed this since version 2.0.171.0\n5. They have some lab features enabled like \"undo send\"\n\nThis is clearly a UI rendering/visual display issue. The checkboxes are not being rendered properly, but they still function when \"Select ALL\" is clicked. This is a visual glitch, not a security vulnerability.\n\nKey indicators that this is a non-security bug:\n- It's about visibility/rendering of UI elements (\"not visible\", \"area is filled up\")\n- It's intermittent (\"sometimes\")\n- It resolves itself after refresh/reload\n- It affects the visual display, not the functionality or security of the system\n- Keywords like \"visible\", \"rendering\", \"checkmarks\" point to UI/UX issues\n\nThere is no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Privilege escalation\n- Cross-context attacks\n- Any exploitable security impact\n\nThis is clearly a Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) - a UI rendering issue where checkboxes are not displaying properly."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 8400060732, "prompt_eval_count": 451, "eval_count": 540}}, {"id": "4258", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 4258 : javascript not open on google chrome 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   thyeun    Chrome Version :  lt;REPLACE this with your version. Ex: 0.3.154.9 gt; URLs (if applicable) :    : Safari 3: ok Firefox 3: ok IE 7: ok  ? 1. 2. 3.  ?  ? i have visited the below side http://comixo.com/index.php?option=com_content amp;view=article amp;id=11 amp;Itemid=14 when i try to open the link from picture1.jpg (with the red-retangular)  the javascript is not allow on google chrome so i try it on firefox and you can see the result Picture2.jpg (with the red-retangular). picture1.jpg 149 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download picture2.jpg 162 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:04:05.602700354Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze what's being described:\n\n**Issue Summary:**\n- A user reports that JavaScript isn't working/allowed on Google Chrome when visiting a specific website (comixo.com)\n- The same JavaScript functionality works in Safari 3, Firefox 3, and IE 7\n- This is essentially a browser compatibility issue where Chrome is not executing JavaScript that other browsers execute\n\n**Analysis against classification criteria:**\n\nFor **Class 1 (Security Bug)**, I look for vulnerabilities compromising confidentiality, integrity, or availability, with keywords like \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"javascript:\", \"cross-context\", \"sandbox bypass\", etc.\n\nWhile the report mentions \"javascript,\" it's in the context of JavaScript not executing on a particular website - a functional compatibility problem, not a security vulnerability. There's no mention of:\n- Memory corruption or information disclosure\n- Cross-context or cross-origin issues\n- Sandbox or privilege bypass\n- Any exploitable security impact\n\nFor **Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**, this involves functional defects, UI/UX issues, and general application behavior problems without exploitable security impact.\n\nThis report clearly falls into Class 0: it's a compatibility/functional issue where Chrome isn't properly executing JavaScript on a specific site, while other browsers handle it correctly. The user is experiencing a functional defect, not a security vulnerability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about:\n- Issue 4258: \"javascript not open on google chrome\"\n- The user is visiting a website (comixo.com)\n- When they try to open a link from a picture, the JavaScript is not allowed on Google Chrome\n- The same functionality works in Firefox, Safari, and IE\n\nThis appears to be a compatibility/functional issue where JavaScript is not working in Chrome for a specific website. The user is essentially reporting that certain JavaScript functionality isn't working in Chrome, while it works in other browsers.\n\nLooking at the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact.\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nThe report mentions \"javascript\" but in the context of it not working/being allowed on a website - this is a functional compatibility issue, not a security vulnerability. The user is just saying that JavaScript doesn't work on a particular site in Chrome.\n\nThis is clearly a Non-Security Bug - it's a functional compatibility issue where JavaScript isn't executing properly on a specific website in Chrome, while working in other browsers. There's no indication of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Privilege bypass\n- Sandbox escape\n- Any exploitable security impact\n\nThis is simply a website not functioning correctly in Chrome due to JavaScript not being allowed/executing properly.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 18775196020, "prompt_eval_count": 534, "eval_count": 695}}, {"id": "12020", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout