 \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nWhile the report mentions \"javascript bug\", this is about a visual rendering issue where a function name is being displayed incorrectly on a button. This is a UI/display bug, not a security vulnerability.\n\nThe keyword \"javascript:\" mentioned in Class 1 typically refers to javascript: URI scheme injection attacks, which is different from what's described here - this is just a display bug where a JavaScript function name is showing up in the UI text.\n\nThis is clearly a UI/UX rendering issue - the button text is not displaying correctly. This falls under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug).\n\nAnswer: (A) Non-Security Bug"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 23833415637, "prompt_eval_count": 424, "eval_count": 695}}, {"id": "15224", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overfl