absolutely no security implications.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report states:\n\"Issue 256 : Linux Version 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes. nbsp; defconoii It is extremely important that a linux version be released because people will start screaming\"\n\nThis is clearly a feature request asking for a Linux version of some software to be released. The person is requesting that the software be made available on Linux.\n\nKey indicators:\n- \"Linux Version\" - requesting a version for Linux\n- \"It is extremely important that a linux version be released\" - this is explicitly a feature request\n- \"because people will start screaming\" - indicating demand/urgency from users\n\nThis falls clearly under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) which includes:\n- \"feature requests\"\n- General application requests\n\nThere is no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Buffer overflow\n- Any security vulnerability\n- Any exploit\n- Any compromise of confidentiality, integrity, or availability\n\nThis is purely a feature request for a Linux port/version of the software. It has zero security implications.\n\nThe classification should be (A) Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 4788583412, "prompt_eval_count": 367, "eval_count": 437}}, {"id": "6880", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 6880 : Find box is not reparented until tab dragging ends  lsaquo; Prev 535 of 14146 Next  rsaquo; 2      nbsp;   venkataramana@chromium.org    See  lt;b/1303363 gt; Make sure  quot;Always show bookmark bar quot; is set to OFF --Open 2 tabs. --Ctrl + F on both of them to Find-In Box. --Drag the 1st tab below to Omnibox and hold it. --Ctrl +B to set  quot;Always show bookmark quot; bar to ON --Notice the tab which is visible contains two find-in box as show in the screenshot. PS: Still reproduces on Stable build: 1.0.154.43 (Official Build 7746) CosmeticIssueFindBox.PNG 72.5 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:06:28.762448758Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to assess whether it has security implications.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe bug describes a UI issue where:\n1. Two \"Find-In-Page\" boxes appear on a tab when performing specific actions (dragging a tab while toggling the bookmark bar visibility)\n2. The find box is not properly reparented during tab dragging\n\n**Key observations:**\n- The report explicitly labels this as a **\"Cosmetic Issue\"**\n- This is purely a **visual rendering problem** - duplicate UI elements appearing on screen\n- There is **no mention** of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, sandbox bypass, or any exploitable condition\n- The bug involves UI state management during tab dragging - a functional/visual defect\n- No security-relevant keywords from the Class 1 criteria are present\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThis is a UI/UX rendering issue - the find box widget is not being properly managed during tab drag operations, resulting in a visual glitch (duplicate find boxes). This falls squarely under Class 0 as a non-security bug with no evidence of confidentiality, integrity, or availability impact.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Issue 6880: Find box is not reparented until tab dragging ends\n- The steps to reproduce involve:\n  1. Setting \"Always show bookmark bar\" to OFF\n  2. Opening 2 tabs\n  3. Ctrl + F on both to open Find-In Box\n  4. Dragging the 1st tab below to Omnibox and holding it\n  5. Ctrl + B to set \"Always show bookmark bar\" to ON\n  6. The visible tab shows two find-in boxes (shown in screenshot)\n\nThe bug is described as a \"Cosmetic Issue\" right in the report. It's about a UI rendering problem where two find boxes appear when they shouldn't. This is a visual/UI bug - the find box is not being properly reparented when tabs are being dragged and bookmark bar visibility changes.\n\nKey indicators:\n- \"Cosmetic\" is explicitly mentioned\n- This is about visual rendering - find boxes appearing incorrectly\n- No mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any security vulnerability\n- No exploitable security impact\n\nThis clearly falls under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) as it's a UI/UX rendering issue/visual display problem without any security implications.\n\nThe answer should be (A) Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 11938987875, "prompt_eval_count": 515, "eval_count": 528}}, {"id": "17093", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 17093 : default browser change not updated under Wrench  gt; Options  gt; Basic 4 problem? 1. Launch Chrome 2. Go to Wrench  gt; Options  gt; Basic 3. Select  quot;Make Google Chrome my default browser quot; 4. Now Make firefox default browser 5. Optional - Close chrome and restart What is the expected output? Chrome should not be selected default browser.  quot;Make Google Chrome my default browser quot; button should be active What do you see instead? Under Wrench  gt; Options  gt; Basic   Chrome is still default browser. Please use labels and text to provide additional information. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:06:33.693153037Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze what the actual issue is:\n\n**Analysis:**\n- The bug describes a UI synchronization problem where Chrome's settings page (Wrench > Options > Basic) doesn't update to reflect that the default browser has been changed externally to Firefox\n- After changing the default browser outside of Chrome, the Chrome settings page still displays Chrome as the default browser\n- The \"Make Google Chrome my default browser\" button doesn't become active again as expected\n\n**Evaluating against classification criteria:**\n\n*Class 1 (Security Bug) indicators:*\n- No memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap overwrite\n- No information disclosure or confidentiality breach\n- No Denial of Service or resource exhaustion\n- No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n- No cross-context issues, javascript: URLs, or redirect vulnerabilities\n\n*Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) indicators:*\n- This is clearly a UI/state synchronization issue\n- The settings page is not refreshing to reflect external system state changes\n- Keywords like \"Options\", \"Basic\", \"button should be active\" relate to UI/UX display\n- This is a functional defect - the UI is stale and doesn't poll for changes\n\nThe bug is simply that Chrome's options page doesn't detect or reflect when another application has changed the system's default browser setting. This is a UI consistency issue, not a security vulnerability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report describes an issue where:\n1. User sets Chrome as the default browser\n2. User then changes the default browser to Firefox externally\n3. Chrome's settings page (Wrench > Options > Basic) still shows Chrome as the default browser\n\nThis is essentially a UI synchronization issue - Chrome's settings page doesn't refresh/update to reflect the actual system default browser status when it's changed externally.\n\nLet me evaluate this against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) indicators:**\n- This is a UI/state synchronization issue\n- The problem is that the UI doesn't update to reflect external changes\n- Keywords like \"Options\", \"Basic\", \"button should be active\" suggest UI/UX issues\n- This is a visual display/state consistency problem\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug) indicators:**\n- No memory corruption mentioned\n- No buffer overflow, heap overwrite\n- No information disclosure\n- No Denial of Service\n- No privilege escalation\n- No sandbox bypass\n- No cross-context issues\n- No javascript: or redirect vulnerabilities\n\nThis is clearly a UI consistency/staleness issue where Chrome doesn't properly detect when its default browser status has changed externally. It's a functional defect related to the user interface not updating properly - a classic non-security bug.\n\nThe bug doesn't compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability. It's just a display/state synchronization issue in the settings UI.\n\nMy classification: Non-Security Bug"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 6369143197, "prompt_eval_count": 464, "eval_count": 603}}, {"id": "16021", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 16021 : Seeking while paused should still display a frame 2     Blocked on: issue 16014  nbsp;   scherkus@chromium.org    1) Open a video with  lt;video gt; 2) Either never start playing it or play it for a bit then pause 3) Seek somewhere (set currentTime or use the UI) Expected: The new frame should be displayed. Acutal: The old frame remains until playback is resumed. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:06:35.515788156Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let me analyze this bug report step-by-step.\n\n**Bug Summary:**\nThe issue describes a video playback behavior problem where seeking (changing position) while a video is paused does not update the displayed frame. The old frame remains visible until playback resumes.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\n1. **Nature of the bug**: This is a functional defect in the video playback behavior. When a user seeks to a new position while paused, the video display should update to show the frame at the new position, but it doesn't.\n\n2. **Keywords and patterns**: The report deals with visual display of video frames (\"display a frame\", \"the new frame should be displayed\", \"the old frame remains\"). This aligns with UI/UX rendering issues.\n\n3. **Security impact assessment**: \n   - No memory corruption (buffer overflow, heap overwrite)\n   - No information disclosure vulnerability\n   - No denial of service potential\n   - No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n   - No cross-context or injection vulnerabilities\n   - This is simply a visual update that doesn't happen when expected\n\n4. **Classification criteria**: This falls squarely under Class 0 as a functional defect/UI rendering issue. The video frame display doesn't update correctly - it's a user experience problem, not a security vulnerability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about:\n- Issue 16021: When seeking while a video is paused, the new frame is not displayed. Instead, the old frame remains until playback is resumed.\n\nThis is clearly a functional defect related to video playback behavior. Specifically:\n1. It's about the visual display of video frames\n2. The issue is that when seeking (changing currentTime) while paused, the video doesn't update to show the new frame\n3. This is a user experience/functional issue - the video frame should update when seeking, but it doesn't\n\nLooking at the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability.\n\nThis bug clearly falls into Class 0 because:\n- It's a functional defect in video playback behavior\n- It relates to visual display (frames not being displayed correctly)\n- There's no security impact - no memory corruption, no information disclosure, no DoS, no privilege escalation\n- It's purely a UX/rendering issue where the video frame doesn't update properly when seeking while paused\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators are not present:\n- No memory corruption, buffer overflow, heap overwrite\n- No information disclosure\n- No DoS\n- No cross-context issues\n- No javascript: protocol issues\n- No redirect issues\n- No privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n\nThis is clearly a non-security bug - it's a functional/UX issue with video playback behavior."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 7085829505, "prompt_eval_count": 424, "eval_count": 639}}, {"id": "7233", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 7233 : PokerNews content invisible (flash issue?) 2 problem? 1. Visit http://www.pokernews.com  ? Content should show up  ? Non-flash content doesn't show up \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:06:52.749286986Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report:\n\n1. **The issue**: Content on pokernews.com is not visible (specifically non-flash content doesn't