s:\n1. When Chrome is set as the default browser\n2. And no Chrome window is already open\n3. Clicking a link in Windows Live Messenger causes Chrome to launch (process appears) but the Chrome window doesn't display\n4. The user has to use Task Manager to kill chrome.exe\n5. This only happens with Live Messenger - other programs can successfully open a new Chrome window\n\nThis is essentially a UI/display bug where:\n- Chrome process launches (evidenced by status bar appearing and process in Task Manager)\n- But the Chrome window doesn't render/display properly\n- The issue is specific to the interaction between Live Messenger and Chrome\n\nLet me evaluate against the criteria:\n\nClass 0 (Non-Security Bug) indicators:\n- This is a UI/UX rendering issue - the window doesn't show up\n- It's a functional defect - the browser doesn't properly display when launched from Live Messenger\n- \"cannot show\" is a display/rendering issue\n- This is generic instability without memory corruption or exhaustion context\n\nClass 1 (Security Bug) indicators:\n- There's no mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, heap overwrite\n- No information disclosure\n- No denial of service (the process hangs but it's not a DoS vulnerability - it's just a broken interaction)\n- No \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", privilege bypass, sandboxing issues\n- No cross-context issues\n- No exhaustion\n\nThis is clearly a compatibility/integration bug between Live Messenger and Chrome where the browser process starts but the window doesn't render. It's a functional defect related to UI rendering, not a security vulnerability.\n\nThe user has to kill the process in Task Manager, but this is just because the application is in a broken state (not showing its window), not because of any memory corruption or exploitable condition.\n\nThis should be classified as Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 22506160800, "prompt_eval_count": 482, "eval