ndow. New windows cannot be put back into the old window. Tabs cannot be relocated  they just come out into new windows.     Problem occurs on a dual monitor setup  I don't know if that is the source of the problem(Haven't checked it on any other system). Older version was working fine  however. Debug clue - The Chrome windows can't  quot;detect quot; each other. The docking to screen works(top  left  bottom  amp; right)  each of the two screens  but docking to another Chrome window doesn't work. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:12:29.283197885Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\n**Bug Description Summary:**\n- Tab dragging and tabstrip docking problems\n- Dragging a tab results in it becoming a new window\n- Tabs cannot be relocated back into the original window\n- Chrome windows cannot \"detect\" each other for docking purposes\n- Docking to the screen edges works, but docking to other Chrome windows doesn't\n- Issue occurs on dual monitor setup\n\n**Analysis:**\n1. **Nature of the bug**: This is a UI interaction/functional defect related to tab management and window docking behavior in the browser interface.\n\n2. **Keywords present**: \"dragging\", \"docking\", \"tabstrip\", \"window\" - these all relate to UI/UX behavior and layout management.\n\n3. **Security indicators**: There are NO security-relevant indicators present:\n   - No memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap issues\n   - No information disclosure or confidentiality concerns\n   - No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n   - No denial of service or resource exhaustion\n   - No cross-context issues or malicious redirect concerns\n\n4. **Impact