ion disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS).\"\n\nThis is a browser compatibility issue - a functional defect where the web interface doesn't load in a particular browser. There's no indication of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Any security vulnerability\n- Any exploitable security impact\n\nThis is purely a functionality issue where the WebClient Interface doesn't support Safari browser. It's a non-security bug.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 9530706266, "prompt_eval_count": 483, "eval_count": 561}}, {"id": "1333", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 1333 :  quot;Esc quot; key does not work on Omnibox  if [New Tab] is set as home page. 2      nbsp;   venkataramana@chromium.org    I don't have repro steps right now. It mostly to deal with hitting Tilda key couple of times on the page. I installed Chrome on my personal laptop. After using for a while...when Navsuggested drop-down list is visible  Hitting  quot;Esc quot; key did not close the drop down list. I thought the  quot;Esc quot; key is gone on my laptop. Created new window  Esc key works fine as expected. Observations: -Can't cancel the drop-down list. -Can't cancel the edits in the URL -Happens on all the tabs of the same browser window. -If a tab is drag and dropped to new window -- Then I can't repro this. -Can cancel Context menu This is happened today also on my work machine and showed demo to Mark. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:13:00.664395737Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to determine if the described issue has security implications.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe bug describes:\n1. The \"Esc\" key doesn't work on the Omnibox when \"New Tab\" is set as home page\n2. Can't dismiss the navigation suggestion drop-down list\n3. Can't cancel edits in the URL bar\n4. The issue is specific to a browser window and doesn't persist when tabs are moved to new windows\n5. Context menus can still be cancelled normally\n\n**Key observations:**\n- This is a keyboard shortcut/UI functionality issue where a specific key (Esc) doesn't perform its expected action in a specific configuration\n- There's no mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, or any memory-related vulnerabilities\n- No information disclosure or data leakage is involved\n- No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n- No denial of service through resource exhaustion\n- The issue is purely about user interaction behavior - a key not working to dismiss UI elements\n\n**Classification reasoning:**\nThis falls squarely under functional defects and UI/UX issues. The Esc key not dismissing a dropdown or canceling URL edits is a usability/functional bug. There's no indication that this could be exploited to compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability of the system.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully to determine if it's a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug.\n\nThe issue describes:\n1. The \"Esc\" key does not work on the Omnibox (URL bar) when \"New Tab\" is set as home page\n2. This happens when the navigation suggested drop-down list is visible\n3. Hitting \"Esc\" doesn't close the drop-down list\n4. Can't cancel edits in the URL\n5. Happens on all tabs of the same browser window\n6. If a tab is dragged to a new window, the issue doesn't reproduce\n7. Can still cancel Context menu\n\nThis is a UI/keyboard functionality issue - the escape key not working properly to dismiss a drop-down list or cancel URL edits. This is clearly a functional/UX bug, not a security vulnerability.\n\nLooking at the classification criteria:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Keywords include visual display, user experience, etc.\n\n- Class 1 (Security Bug): Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nThis bug is clearly a UI/functional issue where a keyboard shortcut (Esc key) doesn't work as expected. There's no evidence of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any security-relevant impact. The inability to close a drop-down list with the Esc key is a user experience issue, not a security vulnerability.\n\nThe answer is clearly (A) Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 13824393680, "prompt_eval_count": 537, "eval_count": 657}}, {"id": "16717", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless th