tton once, the menu shows (expected behavior)\n- When clicking the down arrow again, the menu should hide, but it doesn't\n\nThis is clearly a UI/UX functional issue - the menu doesn't hide when clicking the toggle button a second time. \n\nLooking at the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nThis bug is:\n- A functional defect in the UI (drop-down menu not hiding)\n- A user experience issue\n- No security implications mentioned\n- No keywords related to security vulnerabilities\n- No indication of memory corruption, information disclosure, DoS, or any other security impact\n\nThis clearly falls under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) as it's a UI/UX functional defect without any exploitable security impact."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 9196311861, "prompt_eval_count": 424, "eval_count": 545}}, {"id": "5302", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 5302 : page cycler http tests are too flaky on vista  lsaquo; Prev 423 of 14151 Next  rsaquo; 4      nbsp;   nsylvain@chromium.org    When running the page cycler http tests on vista  at least 2 builds out of tree fail somewhere with this error : 081202 00:07:55 httpd_utils.py:120 INFO Starting http server Traceback (most recent call last): File  quot;../../../scripts/slave/runtest.py quot;  line 165  in ? sys.exit(main_win(options  args)) File  quot;../../../scripts/slave/runtest.py quot;  line 116  in main_win raise google.httpd_utils.HttpdNotStarted('%s. See log file in %s' % google.httpd_utils.HttpdNotStarted: Failed to start httpd on port 8000. See log file in C:\\b\\slave\\chromium-rel-vista-perf- dual\\httpd_logs\\page_cycler_tests program finished with exit code 1 In the error log there is this: fork: Resource temporarily unavailable httpd: unable to fork new process [Tue Dec 2 07:05:14 2008] [warn] pid file /cygdrive/c/b/slave/chromium- rel-vista-perf-single/httpd_logs/page_cycler_tests/httpd.pid overwritten -- Unclean shutdown of previous Apache run? 6 [main] httpd 1412 c:\\b\\slave\\chromium-rel-vista-perf- single\\build\\src\\third_party\\cygwin\\usr\\sbin\\httpd.exe: *** fatal error - unable to remap c:\\b\\slave\\chromium-rel-vista-perf- single\\build\\src\\third_party\\cygwin\\usr\\lib\\apache\\mod_log_config.dll to same address as parent(0x270000) != 0x350000 6 [main] httpd 1288 fork: child 1412 - died waiting for dll loading  errno 11 [Tue Dec 2 07:05:14 2008] [error] (11)Resource temporarily unavailable: fork: Unable to fork new process [Tue Dec 2 07:05:24 2008] [notice] Apache/1.3.33 (Cygwin) configured -- resuming normal operations [Tue Dec 2 07:05:24 2008] [notice] Accept mutex: pthread (Default: pthread) 11150952 [main] httpd 3896 c:\\b\\slave\\chromium-rel-vista-perf- single\\build\\src\\third_party\\cygwin\\usr\\sbin\\httpd.exe: *** fatal error - unable to remap c:\\b\\slave\\chromium-rel-vista-perf- single\\build\\src\\third_party\\cygwin\\usr\\lib\\apache\\mod_include.dll to same address as parent(0x2D0000) != 0x3D0000 11178082 [main] httpd 1288 fork: child 3896 - died waiting for dll loading  errno 11 [Tue Dec 2 07:05:25 2008] [error] (11)Resource temporarily unavailable: fork: Unable to fork new process 22209518 [main] httpd 3992 c:\\b\\slave\\chromium-rel-vista-perf- single\\build\\src\\third_party\\cygwin\\usr\\sbin\\httpd.exe: *** fatal error - unable to remap c:\\b\\slave\\chromium-rel-vista-perf- single\\build\\src\\third_party\\cygwin\\usr\\lib\\apache\\mod_log_config.dll to same address as parent(0x270000) != 0x350000 22231481 [main] httpd 1288 fork: child 3992 - died waiting for dll loading  errno 11 [Tue Dec 2 07:05:36 2008] [error] (11)Resource temporarily unavailable: fork: Unable to fork new process [Tue Dec 2 07:05:49 2008] [error] [client 127.0.0.1] File does not exist: /cygdrive/c/b/slave/chromium-rel-vista-perf- single/build/src/data/page_cycler/favicon.ico 35408404 [main] httpd 2360 c:\\b\\slave\\chromium-rel-vista-perf- single\\build\\src\\third_party\\cygwin\\usr\\sbin\\httpd.exe: *** fatal error - unable to remap c:\\b\\slave\\chromium-rel-vista-perf- single\\build\\src\\third_party\\cygwin\\usr\\lib\\apache\\mod_negotiation.dll to same address as parent(0x2B0000) != 0x3B0000 35460744 [main] httpd 1288 fork: child 2360 - died waiting for dll loading  errno 11 [Tue Dec 2 07:05:50 2008] [error] (11)Resource temporarily unavailable: fork: Unable to fork new process 46575969 [main] httpd 320 c:\\b\\slave\\chromium-rel-vista-perf- single\\build\\src\\third_party\\cygwin\\usr\\sbin\\httpd.exe: *** fatal error - unable to remap c:\\b\\slave\\chromium-rel-vista-perf- single\\build\\src\\third_party\\cygwin\\usr\\lib\\apache\\mod_autoindex.dll to same address as parent(0x2F0000) != 0x3F0000 \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:19:13.53090801Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether it represents a security vulnerability or a non-security issue.\n\n**Key observations from the report:**\n\n1. The title indicates this is about \"page cycler http tests are too flaky on vista\" - this is about test infrastructure instability.\n\n2. The core errors are:\n   - \"fork: Resource temporarily unavailable\"\n   - \"unable to fork new process\"\n   - \"fatal error - unable to remap [DLL] to same address as parent\"\n\n3. These are Cygwin-specific issues with fork() on Windows Vista, where DLLs cannot be remapped to the same memory address in child processes.\n\n4. The context is clearly a build/test infrastructure problem - the paths reference slave/build machines running automated performance tests.\n\n**Analysis against security criteria:**\n\n- While there are memory addresses mentioned (DLL remapping failures), this is NOT memory corruption or a heap overwrite - it's a known Cygwin fork() limitation on Windows\n- The \"Resource temporarily unavailable\" is not a DoS vulnerability exploitable by an attacker - it's a resource constraint in test infrastructure\n- No evidence of information disclosure, privilege escalation, or sandbox bypass\n- No attacker-controlled input or exploitable condition\n- This is purely a reliability/stability issue with test infrastructure on a specific OS version\n\nThe inability to fork processes is a well-known Cygwin compatibility issue, not a security vulnerability. It affects test reliability, not the security of the actual browser product.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully to determine if it's a security bug or non-security bug.\n\nThe bug report is about \"page cycler http tests are too flaky on vista\" - this is about test infrastructure instability.\n\nLooking at the error details:\n1. The httpd server fails to start on port 8000\n2. The errors show \"fork: Resource temporarily unavailable\" and \"unable to fork new process\"\n3. There are errors about \"unable to remap\" DLL files to the same address as parent - this is related to DLL address space remapping in Cygwin\n4. The main issue is that the Apache httpd server on Cygwin can't fork new processes properly because of DLL remapping issues\n\nLet me evaluate against the security bug criteria:\n- \"memory corruption\" - No, this isn't about memory corruption\n- \"information disclosure\" - No\n- \"Denial of Service (DoS)\" - The \"Resource temporarily unavailable\" and inability to fork could theoretically be seen as resource exhaustion, but this is in the context of a test infrastructure, not a production vulnerability\n- \"memory leak\" - No\n- \"buffer overflow\" - No\n- \"heap overwrite\" - No\n- \"cross-context\" - No\n- \"exhaustion\" - The \"Resource temporarily unavailable\" could be seen as resource exhaustion, but again this is about test infrastructure, not an exploitable vulnerability\n- \"javascript:\" - No\n- \"redirect\" - No\n- \"advisory\" - No\n- \"privilege/sandboxing bypasses\" - No\n\nThe DLL remapping issue (\"unable to remap ... to same address as parent\") is a Cygwin compatibility issue, not a security vulnerability. It's about the fork() implementation on Windows where DLLs need to be at the same address in parent and child processes. This is a known limitation of Cygwin's fork() implementation.\n\nThis is essentially a test infrastructure reliability issue - the tests are flaky because of Cygwin's limitations on Vista. This is a non-security bug - it's about test infrastructure stability, not about a vulnerability that could be exploited to compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability of the actual product.\n\nThe \"Resource temporarily unavailable\" error is about process creation failure due to DLL remapping issues, not about a DoS vulnerability that an attacker could exploit.\n\nThis should be classified as a Non-Security Bug (Class 0)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 18537173474, "prompt_eval_count": 1559, "eval_count": 821}}, {"id": "17788", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 17788 : Facebook Javascript doesn't work after restoring tabs  have to refresh twice 3 problem? 1. Having a Facebook tab open 2. Closing Chrome 3. Opening Chrome  restoring tabs 4. Trying to interact with Facebook  but everything that is based on Javascript (so basically everything  Links  Comment  Chat  ...) is not working. 5. Refreshing page (F5)  still not working 6. Refreshing again  now it's working.  ? Commenting  Links  Chat works  ? It doesn't     \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:19:19.684135037Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "## Analysis\n\n**Understanding the Bug:**\nThis report describes a functional issue where JavaScript functionality on Facebook doesn't work after Chrome restores previously open tabs. The user must refresh the page twice to get JavaScript-based features (links, comments, chat) working again.\n\n**Evaluating Security Relevance:**\n- The mention of \"Javascript\" here is about JavaScript **functionality not executing** after tab restoration \u2014 not a security concern like \"javascript:\" URI injection or cross-context scripting.\n- There is no indication of memory corruption, buffer overflow, heap overwrite, or any exploitable vulnerability.\n- No information disclosure, privilege escalation, or sandbox bypass is involved.\n- This is a **session/tab restoration issue** causing degraded user experience \u2014 the page simply doesn't function correctly until refreshed.\n- The bug describes generic instability/functional failure, which falls under Class 0 per the domain knowledge.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThis is a standard functional defect related to tab restoration and JavaScript re-initialization. There is no evidence of security impact on confidentiality, integrity, or availability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n1. A Facebook tab is open\n2. Chrome is closed\n3. Chrome is reopened, restoring tabs\n4. JavaScript-based functionality on Facebook doesn't work (links, comments, chat)\n5. First refresh doesn't fix it\n6. Second refresh fixes it\n\nThis is clearly a functional bug related to:\n- Tab restoration functionality\n- JavaScript not executing properly after tab restore\n- User experience issue where the page needs to be refreshed twice\n\nThis does not involve:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service (DoS)\n- Privilege escalation\n- Sandbox bypass\n- Cross-context issues\n- Any exploitable security vulnerability\n\nThe mention of \"Javascript\" in the report is about JavaScript functionality not working properly (a functional bug), not about a \"javascript:\" URI attack or any security-related JavaScript issue.\n\nThis is a classic Non-Security Bug - a functional defect where tab restoration doesn't properly restore JavaScript execution context, requiring a manual refresh twice.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 6279736699, "prompt_eval_count": 439, "eval_count": 456}}, {"id": "11347", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 11347 : Search Wikipedia Feature