eld has no scroll bar when text exceeds the box\n2. Selecting text in the free text field is way off -- it seems to select text backwards\n\nThese are clearly usability/UX issues:\n- Missing scroll bar is a UI/layout issue\n- Text selection being \"off\" and selecting backwards is a UI/interaction bug\n\nLooking at the classification criteria:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability.\n\n- Class 1 (Security Bug): Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nThis bug report is clearly about usability problems with a dialog - a missing scrollbar and text selection issues. These are UI/UX issues, not security vulnerabilities. There's no indication of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- DoS\n- Privilege escalation\n- Any exploitable security impact\n\nThe title even explicitly says \"usability problems.\"\n\nThis should be classified as Class 0 - Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 10634185881, "prompt_eval_count": 419, "eval_count": 591}}, {"id": "3696", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 3696 : VK_OEM_3 should not be used as accelerator. 4      nbsp;   thatan...@google.com    Chrome uses ALT+VK_OEM_3 as an accelerator to invoke JavaScript debugger. VK_OEM_3 is '~' on JP layout + 101 keyboard. (or on US layout + any keyboard) ALT+~ is Windows standard key combination to activate Japanese Input Method Editor (IME) on 101 keyboard. So  for Japanese 101 keyboard users  JavaScript debugger key and IME activation key conflict. Chrome 0.3.154.6 c.f. This problem was reported at Japanese U2U: http://groups.google.com/group/google-chrome-help-jp-report-a- bug/browse_thread/thread/94990c1b81f6ae35 \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:20:52.707125398Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze the core issue:\n\n**The Problem:**\n- Chrome uses ALT+VK_OEM_3 (ALT+~) as a keyboard shortcut to invoke the JavaScript debugger\n- ALT+~ is the standard Windows key combination for activating the Japanese Input Method Editor (IME)\n- This creates a shortcut key conflict for Japanese 101 keyboard users\n\n**Analysis:**\n1. The primary issue is a **keyboard shortcut conflict** - the \"shortcut\" keyword is explicitly listed under Class 0 indicators\n2. The JavaScript debugger invocation here is about developer tool accessibility, not about a vulnerability being exploited\n3. This is fundamentally a **usability/functionality issue** - users cannot activate their IME because Chrome has claimed the key combination for a different purpose\n4. There is no evidence of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or DoS\n5. The \"JavaScript debugger\" mention refers to a built-in browser developer tool, not an exploitable security vulnerability\n\nWhile the word \"JavaScript\" appears, the context is about the debugger keyboard shortcut, not about \"javascript:\" URI exploitation or cross-context scripting vulnerabilities that would fall under Class 1.\n\nThe bug is about a user experience conflict where a standard system key combination is overridden by Chrome's accelerator key assignment.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report states:\n- Chrome uses ALT+VK_OEM_3 as an accelerator to invoke JavaScript debugger\n- VK_OEM_3 is '~' on JP layout + 101 keyboard\n- ALT+~ is the Windows standard key combination to activate Japanese Input Method Editor (IME) on 101 keyboard\n- This creates a conflict where the JavaScript debugger key and IME activation key conflict for Japanese 101 keyboard users\n\nNow let me evaluate this against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes without exploitable security impact. Keywords include \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\", \"feature request\", \"shortcut\", and generic instability.\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nThis bug report is about:\n1. A keyboard shortcut conflict\n2. The JavaScript debugger being invoked when users try to activate their IME\n\nLet me think about whether this has security implications:\n\n1. The JavaScript debugger being accessible via a keyboard shortcut could potentially be a security concern - it might expose debugging information or allow manipulation of web content. However, this is a browser developer tool, not a vulnerability in a web application.\n\n2. The primary issue is a UX/shortcut conflict - the key combination intended for one purpose (IME activation) is being used for another (debugger activation). This is fundamentally a functional/UX issue.\n\n3. The mention of \"JavaScript debugger\" doesn't automatically make this a security bug. The debugger is a developer tool, and the issue is about when it gets activated, not about a vulnerability in the debugger itself.\n\n4. This is more of a keyboard shortcut conflict issue - users can't use their standard IME activation key because Chrome has claimed it for the debugger. This is a usability/functionality issue.\n\nWhile the JavaScript debugger could theoretically have security implications if it were being triggered maliciously or in a way that exposes sensitive information, the core of this bug is about a shortcut key conflict that affects user experience. The \"shortcut\" keyword is explicitly listed as a Class 0 indicator.\n\nHowever, I should consider: could invoking the JavaScript debugger inadvertently be a security issue? If the debugger is being invoked when the user doesn't intend it, this could potentially expose debugging information or allow unintended interaction with web content. But the bug report is primarily about the conflict preventing IME activation, not about the debugger being a security vulnerability.\n\nGiven the classification criteria and the emphasis on needing CLEAR evidence of security relevance, this appears to be a Non-Security Bug - a keyboard shortcut conflict affecting usability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 39525623581, "prompt_eval_count": 492, "eval_count": 897}}, {"id": "5900", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 5900 : text area not enabled in new message in hotmail 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   lezmou    Chrome Version : 1.0.154.36 URLs (if applicable) :   IE  Firefox  : Safari 3: Firefox 3:OK IE 7:OK  ? 1. New message in hotmail 2. 3.  ? message text area enabled  ? message text area not active (disabled) Entring message text impossible Hotmail.jpg 90.8 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:21:00.731989747Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let me analyze this bug report step-by-step:\n\n1. **Issue Description**: The text area in Hotmail's \"new message\" compose window is disabled/not active when using Chrome browser.\n\n2. **Key Details**:\n   - The text area cannot be interacted with (disabled state)\n   - Works correctly in Firefox 3 and IE 7\n   - Fails in Chrome version 1.0.154.36\n   - This is a browser compatibility/rendering issue\n\n3. **Classification Analysis**:\n   - This is a **functional defect** - a UI element (text area) is not properly enabled for user interaction\n   - This is a **UI/UX issue** - the interface isn't rendering/behaving correctly in a specific browser\n   - There is **no evidence** of memory corruption, buffer overflow, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any exploitable vulnerability\n   - There is **no security impact** mentioned - no confidentiality, integrity, or availability compromise from a security perspective\n   - The issue is about **browser compatibility**, not about an attacker being able to exploit anything\n\n4. **Conclusion**: This is clearly a non-security bug - a functional/UI defect where a form element isn't properly interactive in a specific browser version. It falls squarely under Class 0 criteria which includes \"functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues.\"\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Issue with Hotmail's new message feature\n- The text area is not enabled/disabled when composing a new message\n- Users cannot enter message text\n- This is a functionality/UI issue in Chrome browser\n- Works fine in Firefox 3 and IE 7, but not in Chrome 1.0.154.36\n\nThis is clearly a functional defect - a UI/UX issue where a text area element is not properly enabled for user interaction. It's a browser compatibility issue with how Chrome renders or handles the text area in Hotmail.\n\nLooking at the classification criteria:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): \"Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact.\"\n- Class 1 (Security Bug): \"Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS).\"\n\nThis bug report is about:\n1. A text area being disabled when it shouldn't be\n2. A functional defect in the browser's handling of the Hotmail interface\n3. A UI/UX issue - the text area isn't interactive\n\nThere's no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Information disclosure\n- Cross-context issues\n- Sandbox bypasses\n- Any exploitable security vulnerability\n\nThis is purely a functional/UI defect - the text area isn't working properly in Chrome. This falls clearly under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug).\n\nAnswer: (A) Non-Security Bug"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 12861006023, "prompt_eval_count": 454, "eval_count": 627}}, {"id": "5886", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 5886 : margin: auto is causing the misalignment  lsaquo; Prev 461 of 14170 Next  rsaquo; 2 problem? 1. Visit the above website  ? See the website in Firefox  IE 7 or Opera for expected result. All tables aligned correctly.  ? The table called  quot;Things You Need To Know quot; does not correctly center with reference to the right column div. Instead it centers itself in the middle of the column.     HTML and CSS are both standards compliant. This is a most peculiar bug  but one I thought must have already been reported because it seems like a very simple rendering error on the surface. However  it is still not fixed after a number of months of me using Google Chrome. I apologise if this error has already been reported. I have just assumed that the length of time it has been broken means no one else has spotted it. hrfc.png 88.1 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:21:02.614841447Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "This bug report describes a CSS rendering issue where `margin: auto` causes a table to misalign within a column div in Google Chrome, while other browsers (Firefox, IE 7, Opera) render it correctly. The reporter explicitly calls it a \"very simple rendering error\" and the issue is purely about visual layout/alignment.\n\nKey indicators pointing to a non-security bug:\n- Keywords: \"margin: auto\", \"misalignment\", \"rendering error\", \"center\" - all CSS/layout terms\n- The problem is a visual display issue with no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any exploitable security impact\n- This is a standards compliance rendering difference between browsers\n\nThere is absolutely no evidence of security relevance in this report.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report step by step.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n1. A CSS/layout issue with `margin: auto` causing misalignment\n2. A table called \"Things You Need To Know\" not centering correctly with reference to the right column div\n3. It's described as a \"very simple rendering error\"\n4. The issue is about visual alignment in different browsers (Firefox, IE 7, Opera work correctly, Chrome doesn't)\n5. The report mentions HTML and CSS are standards compliant\n\nThis is clearly a CSS rendering/layout issue - a visual display problem. The keywords involved are:\n- \"margin: auto\" - CSS property\n- \"misalignment\" - layout issue\n- \"rendering error\" - visual display problem\n- \"center\" - layout/positioning concern\n- Browser compatibility issue\n\nAccording to the classification guidelines:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems... Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\")\n- Class 1 (Security Bug): Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability\n\nThis bug report contains no security implications whatsoever. It's purely a visual/layout rendering issue with CSS. There's no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, DoS, privilege escalation, or any other security concern.\n\nThe answer should be (A) Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 10985938044, "prompt_eval_count": 533, "eval_count": 466}}, {"id": "18802", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 18802 : Flaky LayoutTest 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   pkasting@chromium.org    WIN : LayoutTests/http/tests/misc/referrer.html = TIMEOUT PASS \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:21:06.728853254Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze the key details:\n\n1. **\"Flaky LayoutTest\"** - This indicates a test that inconsistently passes or fails, which is a test infrastructure/reliability issue\n2. **\"LayoutTests/http/tests/misc/referrer.html = TIMEOUT PASS\"** - The test sometimes times out and sometimes passes, indicating inconsistent behavior\n\nWhile the test is related to \"referrer\" (which can have security implications regarding information disclosure via the Referer header), the bug report itself is about the **test being flaky** - not about a referrer vulnerability. A flaky test is a test reliability/infrastructure issue where results are inconsistent (TIMEOUT vs PASS), not a report of an exploitable security vulnerability.\n\nThere are no indicators of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or other security vulnerabilities mentioned. The issue is purely about test stability and consistency.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is:\n\"Issue 18802 : Flaky LayoutTest 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes. pkasting@chromium.org WIN : LayoutTests/http/tests/misc/referrer.html = TIMEOUT PASS\"\n\nLet me break this down:\n1. This is about a \"Flaky LayoutTest\" - flaky tests are tests that sometimes pass and sometimes fail inconsistently\n2. The specific test is \"LayoutTests/http/tests/misc/referrer.html\"\n3. The behavior is \"TIMEOUT PASS\" - meaning the test sometimes times out and sometimes passes\n\nNow, let me consider the security implications:\n\nThe test is about \"referrer\" - which is related to the HTTP Referer header. The Referer header can have security implications as it can leak information about what page a user came from. However, this bug report is specifically about a flaky test - meaning the test is inconsistent in its results (sometimes timing out, sometimes passing).\n\nThis is a test infrastructure/reliability issue, not a security vulnerability itself. A flaky test means the test is unreliable, not that there's necessarily a security vulnerability being exposed. The test might be testing referrer behavior (which could have security implications), but the bug itself is about the test being flaky, not about a security vulnerability.\n\nThe keywords here are:\n- \"Flaky LayoutTest\" - this is about test reliability\n- \"TIMEOUT PASS\" - this is about inconsistent test results\n- No mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, information disclosure, DoS, or other security vulnerability indicators\n\nThis appears to be a test infrastructure issue where a test is not consistently passing or failing. This is a common non-security issue in development. The test happens to be about referrers, but the bug is about the test being flaky, not about a referrer-related security vulnerability.\n\nBased on the domain knowledge provided, Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) includes \"functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact.\" A flaky test falls under functional/infrastructure issues without clear security impact.\n\nTherefore, I classify this as a Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 14734164815, "prompt_eval_count": 371, "eval_count": 654}}, {"id": "21628", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 21628 : Security: Should not show basic HTTP auth dialogs for subresource loads  particularly images  lsaquo; Prev 7289 of 14163 Next  rsaquo; 2      nbsp;   scarybeasts    This is a generic bug affecting all browsers -- but perhaps something we can trivially tighten up in Chrome for a good benefit. The issue is that many many web apps (Orkut in the case that was brought to our attention) but also Gmail etc. permit the rendering of an  lt;img gt; tag with an arbitrary  quot;src quot; parameter. The issue is that if the  quot;src quot; parameter refers to a resource which responds with a HTTP 401 plus HTTP header indicating basic auth -- the browser will pop-up a little login dialog. This might be abused for phishing as the appearance to the untrained eye would be that the trusted site popped up the dialog. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:21:07.00266808Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze the security implications:\n\n**Key Points from the Report:**\n1. The issue involves HTTP basic auth dialogs being shown for subresource loads (specifically images)\n2. An attacker can craft an `<img>` tag with a `src` parameter pointing to a resource that returns HTTP 401 with basic auth headers\n3. This causes the browser to display a login dialog that appears to originate from the trusted site\n4. The report explicitly mentions this can be \"abused for phishing\"\n5. The title explicitly starts with \"Security:\"\n\n**Analysis:**\n- This is a **phishing/spoofing vulnerability** - the browser's behavior allows an attacker to trick users into believing a login dialog comes from a trusted domain\n- It compromises **confidentiality** by potentially tricking users into revealing credentials\n- It involves **authentication dialog abuse** - a security mechanism (basic auth) being misused for deception\n- The issue is explicitly framed as a security concern in the title\n\n**Classification:**\nThis clearly falls under Class 1 (Security Bug) because it describes a vulnerability that can compromise confidentiality through phishing attacks. The auth dialog spoofing represents a trust boundary violation where an attacker can leverage browser behavior to deceive users about the origin of authentication prompts.\n\n**Answer: (B) Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report describes an issue where:\n1. Web apps permit rendering of `<img>` tags with arbitrary `src` parameters\n2. If the `src` refers to a resource that responds with HTTP 401 plus a header indicating basic auth, the browser will pop up a login dialog\n3. This could be abused for phishing because the untrained eye would think the trusted site popped up the dialog\n\nThis is clearly a security concern because:\n- It involves potential phishing attacks (spoofing/authenticity issues)\n- It involves a way to trick users into revealing their credentials\n- The dialog appears to come from a trusted site, compromising user trust and potentially leading to credential theft\n- It's explicitly marked as \"Security:\" in the title\n- It involves authentication dialogs being abused\n\nThis falls under Class 1 (Security Bug) because:\n- It compromises confidentiality (potential credential theft through phishing)\n- It involves a bypass/deception mechanism related to authentication\n- It could lead to information disclosure if users enter credentials into the spoofed dialog\n- The bug explicitly mentions phishing abuse\n\nThe report clearly indicates this is a security vulnerability - it's about authentication dialogs being shown in inappropriate contexts that could lead to phishing attacks. This is a security concern involving trust and authenticity.\n\n**Answer: (B) Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 13898345540, "prompt_eval_count": 517, "eval_count": 552}}, {"id": "15098", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 15098 : Download shelf : 'Open'  'Always Open Type'  'Reveal in Finder' and 'Cancel' options should be handled in a appropriate way 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   rohitbm@chromium.org    Platform: Hostname: rohitbm-macbookpro15.local Mac OS X Version 10.5.7 (Build 9J61) Processor: 2 Intel 2.33 GHz RAM: 2048 MB Chromium: Chromium version: 3.0.191 SNV Revision: 19022  lt; lt; lt;Release gt; gt; gt; QuickTime Player: 7.6.2 Flash Player: 10.0.22.87  ? 1. Start downloading of any file and wait till file is downloaded. 2. Open popup menu for that file from download bar. 3. Start downloading of any big file ( gt;100 MB). During downloading  click on 'Cancel' option from dropdown menu from download bar. 4. Reopen dorpdown menu for download in step-3. Expected result: 2.1 For any downloaded file  'Cancel' option is disabled. 3.1 For any canceled download  other options like 'Open'  'Always Open Type'  'Reveal in Finder' and 'Cancel' should be disabled. Actual result: 2.2 Cancel button is enabled in menu option. 3.2 All options are enabled. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:21:12.364337709Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether it describes a security vulnerability or a functional/UI defect.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe bug describes an issue with the download shelf menu in Chromium where:\n1. The 'Cancel' option remains enabled for already-downloaded files (when it should be disabled)\n2. For canceled downloads, options like 'Open', 'Always Open Type', 'Reveal in Finder', and 'Cancel' remain enabled (when they should be disabled)\n\nThis is fundamentally a **UI state management issue** - menu items are not being properly enabled/disabled based on the current state of the download. The bug is about incorrect interactive element states, which falls squarely under functional defects and UI/UX issues.\n\n**Checking against security indicators:**\n- No memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap overwrite\n- No information disclosure vulnerability\n- No Denial of Service (the \"cancel\" functionality isn't causing exhaustion or crashes)\n- No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n- No cross-context or redirect issues\n- No advisory or exploit references\n\nWhile one could stretch an argument that allowing \"Open\" on a canceled download might have unintended consequences, the core issue described is simply about menu option state management - a UI/functional defect. There is no clear evidence of security impact on confidentiality, integrity, or availability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug is about the download shelf in Chromium browser. Specifically:\n\n1. For a fully downloaded file, the 'Cancel' option is enabled in the menu when it should be disabled (since the download is already complete).\n2. For a canceled download, other options like 'Open', 'Always Open Type', 'Reveal in Finder', and 'Cancel' are all enabled when they should be disabled.\n\nThis is essentially a UI/UX issue where menu options are not being properly enabled/disabled based on the state of the download. The bug is about:\n- Menu items not being grayed out appropriately based on context\n- Incorrect state management of UI elements\n\nLet me check against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display, user experience, and generic instability.\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nThis bug report is clearly about UI state management - menu options not being disabled when they should be. It's a functional defect / UI/UX issue. There's no indication of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Privilege escalation\n- Any exploitable security vulnerability\n\nWhile one might argue that allowing 'Open' on a canceled download could have some security implications, the core issue is about UI state management, not about a security vulnerability. The options are enabled/disabled incorrectly based on the download state - this is a functional/UI bug.\n\nThis should be classified as a Non-Security Bug (Class 0)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 11406359126, "prompt_eval_count": 643, "eval_count": 702}}, {"id": "4413", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 4413 : Error Displaying a Page with Animated GIF 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   hkust.isaac    Chrome Version : 0.3.155.0 (build 5441) URLs (if applicable) : http://sites.clsmss.edu.hk/computer/s-3-ct-0809    : Safari 3: OK Firefox 3: OK IE 7: OK  ? 1. Open the page: http://sites.clsmss.edu.hk/computer/s-3-ct-0809 2. 3.  ? The page should be loaded successfully without problem.  ? During or after loading the page  an error message appear:  quot;Something went wrong while displaying this webpage  ... quot;     The page can be displayed in Google Chrome (0.3.154.9) without problem. But there is problem when using Chromium. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:21:14.898521154Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze the details carefully:\n\n**Key Details:**\n- Issue title: \"Error Displaying a Page with Animated GIF\"\n- The page loads successfully in Safari 3, Firefox 3, IE 7, and even an older Chrome version (0.3.154.9)\n- In the newer Chromium build, an error message appears: \"Something went wrong while displaying this webpage...\"\n- This is a display/rendering problem specific to a page containing an animated GIF\n\n**Analysis:**\n- This is fundamentally a **rendering/display issue** - the browser fails to properly render a webpage containing an animated GIF\n- The report contains keywords related to visual display problems (\"Displaying\", \"webpage display\")\n- There is **no evidence** of memory corruption, buffer overflow, heap overwrite, information disclosure, or privilege escalation\n- There is no mention of \"exhaustion\", DoS conditions, or sandbox bypass\n- The crash/error is described as a generic display failure, not an exploitable condition\n- The bug appears to be a regression between Chrome versions (works in 0.3.154.9 but fails in 0.3.155.0)\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThis is a functional rendering defect - a page with an animated GIF causes a display error. While browser crashes can sometimes indicate security issues, this report lacks any indicators of exploitable security impact (no memory corruption context, no privilege escalation, no information disclosure). Per the classification guidelines, visual display issues and generic crashes without memory corruption context fall under Class 0.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n- Issue 4413: Error Displaying a Page with Animated GIF\n- Chrome Version: 0.3.155.0 (build 5441)\n- A specific URL causes an error message \"Something went wrong while displaying this webpage...\"\n- The page works fine in Safari 3, Firefox 3, IE 7, and even in Google Chrome (0.3.154.9)\n- But there's a problem when using Chromium (newer version)\n\nThis appears to be a rendering/display issue with an animated GIF that causes the browser to crash or display an error. The report doesn't mention:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Heap overwrite\n- Cross-context issues\n- Information disclosure\n- Privilege bypass\n- Any exploitable security impact\n\nThe keywords in this report are:\n- \"Error Displaying\"\n- \"rendering\" (implied by \"displaying a page\")\n- \"webpage display\" issue\n- \"crash\" is implied by the error message\n\nThis seems like a functional defect - a page with an animated GIF causes a display error. While browser crashes can sometimes be security-related (if they involve memory corruption), the report doesn't provide evidence of memory corruption or other security-relevant patterns.\n\nAccording to the classification guidelines:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems... and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\").\n\nThis bug is clearly about a display/rendering issue with an animated GIF. There's no clear evidence of security relevance like memory corruption, information disclosure, or DoS with exhaustion context.\n\nTherefore, this should be classified as a Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 11967393745, "prompt_eval_count": 525, "eval_count": 720}}, {"id": "6238", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 6238 : Can't copy any text from Chromium to Evernote 11 problem? 1. Select any text on page (or in textarea) 2. Ctrl+C 3. Alt+Tab on Evernote  Ctrl+V  ? Text appears in Evernote note  ? No text pasted in Evernote     Evernote is latest. 3.0.0.981 (41070) I've tried on my second computer (1.0.156.0  build 7477). The same problem. In others application text pasted succesfully. I don't know  is it Chromium problem or Evernote problem? :-) My friend with Google Chrome 2.0.156.1 don't confirm problem. Any ideas to find source of problem? :-) \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:21:16.16616523Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether it describes a security vulnerability or a functional defect.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe issue describes:\n- Inability to copy text from Chromium and paste it into Evernote\n- Copy-paste works fine with other applications\n- The user is uncertain whether the problem is with Chromium or Evernote\n- This is essentially a clipboard interoperability issue between two specific applications\n\n**Security relevance assessment:**\n\n- No mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, or heap issues\n- No information disclosure vulnerability described\n- No privilege escalation or sandbox bypass\n- No denial of service (system exhaustion) concerns\n- No malicious code execution vectors\n- This is simply a functional defect where copy-paste isn't working between two specific applications\n\nThe bug is a usability/functional issue - a clipboard integration problem between Chromium and Evernote. While clipboard issues *could* theoretically have security implications (e.g., if sensitive data leaks across contexts), this report only describes a feature not working as expected, with no evidence of exploitable security impact.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe issue describes:\n- A user cannot copy text from Chromium to Evernote\n- The copy-paste functionality is not working between these two specific applications\n- The user is unsure if it's a Chromium or Evernote problem\n- Other applications work fine with pasting\n- The user's friend with a different Chrome version doesn't have this problem\n\nThis is clearly a functional/usability issue - a clipboard/copy-paste problem between two applications. There's no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Privilege escalation\n- Denial of Service\n- Security bypass\n- Any exploitable vulnerability\n\nThe bug is about a feature (copy-paste) not working correctly between two applications. This is a functional defect, not a security vulnerability.\n\nAccording to the classification guidelines:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact.\n- Class 1 (Security Bug): Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability.\n\nThis bug report falls squarely into Class 0 - it's a functional defect where copy-paste isn't working properly. There's no security implication mentioned or implied.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 8794368604, "prompt_eval_count": 488, "eval_count": 500}}, {"id": "15489", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 15489 : cannot copy cells using CTRL+C  amp; CTRL-V on google docs spreadsheets 3 problem? 1. open a spreadsheet in google docs 2. Copy one or more cells using CTRL+C 3. Try to paste in other cells using CTRL+V  ?  ? Not working   Attach a screenshot and backtrace if possible. \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:21:22.137847837Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to assess whether it describes a security vulnerability or a non-security functional issue.\n\n**Analysis:**\n- The bug describes an inability to use CTRL+C and CTRL+V keyboard shortcuts in Google Docs spreadsheets\n- This is a functional defect - copy-paste functionality via keyboard shortcuts is not working\n- There is no mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any exploitable security impact\n- The keywords involved (\"copy\", \"paste\", \"CTRL+C\", \"CTRL+V\") relate to user experience and feature functionality\n- No security-relevant patterns such as buffer overflow, exhaustion, cross-context issues, or sandbox bypasses are present\n\nThis is a straightforward UX/functional bug where a feature (keyboard shortcut copy-paste) is broken, not a vulnerability that compromises confidentiality, integrity, or availability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug is about:\n- Cannot copy cells using CTRL+C and CTRL-V on Google Docs spreadsheets\n- It's a functional issue where copy-paste keyboard shortcuts are not working\n- Steps to reproduce: open spreadsheet, copy cells, try to paste - it doesn't work\n\nThis is clearly a functional/UX bug - a feature (copy-paste via keyboard shortcuts) is not working as expected. There's no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Privilege escalation\n- Sandbox bypass\n- Any security-related keywords or patterns\n\nThis falls squarely into Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) as it's a functional defect related to user experience/keyboard shortcuts not working properly.\n\nAnswer: (A) Non-Security Bug"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 6772868356, "prompt_eval_count": 398, "eval_count": 355}}, {"id": "20500", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 20500 :  quot;View full history quot; link should always be in the same place  lsaquo; Prev 1427 of 14163 Next  rsaquo; 4      nbsp;   prog...@chromium.org    In the old NTP  the recently closed list was a vertical list where the distance between the first entry and the last entry was always pretty small In the new NTP  that list is horizontal -When empty  the  quot;View full history quot; link will appear in the left part of the screen -When full  the  quot;View full history quot; link will appear in the right side of the screen This is pretty far away places to find the exact same link. For some reason I tend to go to the history page from the NTP  and in the new NTP I feel like I waste valuable seconds on deciding whether I should move my cursor to the left or to the right \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:21:23.947955517Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze whether it describes a security vulnerability or a non-security issue.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe bug report describes an issue where the \"View full history\" link on the New Tab Page (NTP) changes position depending on whether the recently closed list is empty or full:\n- Empty \u2192 link appears on the left\n- Full \u2192 link appears on the right\n\nThe user complains about:\n- Inconsistent placement of the same link\n- Wasting time deciding which direction to move the cursor\n- Poor user experience compared to the old vertical list layout\n\n**Key observations:**\n- This is entirely about **UI/UX layout inconsistency**\n- Keywords: \"left part of the screen\", \"right side of the screen\", \"move my cursor\", \"waste valuable seconds\"\n- No mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any exploitable condition\n- No security impact on confidentiality, integrity, or availability\n- This is a design/usability concern about link positioning\n\nThis clearly falls under the category of UI/UX rendering issues and layout problems, which are explicitly classified as Class 0 (Non-Security Bug).\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about the \"View full history\" link on the New Tab Page (NTP) in Chrome. The issue is:\n\n1. In the old NTP, the recently closed list was vertical, so the distance between entries was small\n2. In the new NTP, the list is horizontal\n3. When empty, the \"View full history\" link appears on the left side of the screen\n4. When full, the \"View full history\" link appears on the right side of the screen\n5. The user finds this inconvenient because they have to decide whether to move their cursor left or right to find the link\n\nThis is clearly a UI/UX issue about the placement of a link on the page. The user is complaining about:\n- The position of a link changing based on content\n- Having to waste \"valuable seconds\" deciding where to move their cursor\n- The inconsistency of link placement\n\nThis is a classic UI/UX design concern. There is no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Security bypass\n- Cross-context issues\n- Any exploitable vulnerability\n\nThe keywords here are about visual placement (\"left part\", \"right side\"), user experience (\"waste valuable seconds\", \"move my cursor\"), and layout inconsistency.\n\nAccording to the classification guidelines:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests\n- Class 1 (Security Bug): Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability\n\nThis clearly falls under Class 0 - it's a UI/UX issue about link placement consistency.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 11355229523, "prompt_eval_count": 519, "eval_count": 616}}, {"id": "13530", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.