on/` - text selection behavior\n   - `editing/style/` - style editing\n   - `fast/lists/` - list rendering\n\n2. **Nature of failures**: All tests show `= FAIL`, indicating they are functional regressions from the WebKit merge. These are layout/rendering test failures.\n\n3. **Security indicators check**: \n   - No memory corruption indicators (buffer overflow, heap overwrite, use-after-free)\n   - No information disclosure keywords\n   - No DoS/exhaustion indicators\n   - No sandbox/privilege bypass mentions\n   - No cross-context or javascript: concerns\n   - These are purely functional test failures for editing UI features\n\n4. **Classification criteria**: This falls squarely under Class 0 - functional defects and UI/UX issues. The failing tests are about document editing behavior (pasting text, inserting content, selection handling, style application) - all functional aspects of the browser's editor component.\n\n**Conclusion**: This is a functional regression report from a WebKit merge, documenting that various editing/pasteboard/selection tests are failing. There is no evidence of security vulnerability such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or exploitable conditions.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about \"Failing tests from WebKit merge 45873:45916\". It lists a large number of failing layout tests related to:\n- Editing operations (execCommand, inserting, pasteboard, selection, style)\n- These are LayoutTests which test the rendering/layout functionality of the browser\n\nThe keywords and patterns I see:\n- \"LayoutTests\" - these are tests related to layout and rendering\n- \"editing/pasteboard\" - clipboard/paste functionality tests\n- \"editing/inserting\" - text insertion tests\n- \"editing/selection\" - text selection tests\n- \"editing/style\" - style editing tests\n- \"FAIL\" - test failures\n\nThis appears to be a regression report from a WebKit merge where multiple layout/editing tests started failing. The tests are related to:\n1. Text editing operations\n2. Pasteboard/clipboard operations\n3. Text selection\n4. Style operations\n\nLooking at the classification criteria:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, general application crashes without exploitable security impact\n- Class 1 (Security Bug): memory corruption, information disclosure, DoS, \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", privilege/sandboxing bypasses\n\nThis bug report is clearly about functional test failures related to editing/pasteboard/layout functionality. There's no indication of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Buffer overflow\n- Information disclosure\n- DoS\n- Sandbox bypass\n- Any security vulnerability\n\nThe failing tests are all related to UI editing functionality - how text is pasted, inserted, selected, and styled. These are functional/rendering issues, not security issues.\n\nTherefore, this should be classified as a Non-Security Bug (Class 0)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 9422453506, "prompt_eval_count": 1700, "eval_count": 767}}, {"id": "17196", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 17196 : Orange.co.il: Visual glitches on homepage in Chrome on OS X  lsaquo; Prev 1199 of 14162 Next  rsaquo; 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   jeremy@chromium.org    Steps to reproduce: Navigate to orange.co.il in Chrome or Safari on OS X. See arrows in attached screenshot for issues: * Lower nav menu has text overflowing the border. * Mousing over the  quot;\u6ce8\u8bea\u8b9c\u8b9a  \u8b98\u8bac\u8f6c quot; link causes the link to wrap. * Left menu item wraps over two lines orange_homepage.png 289 KB  nbsp; View  nbsp; Download \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:23:10.608386109Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze the nature of the issue described.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe report describes issues on orange.co.il:\n1. \"Visual glitches\" on the homepage\n2. Text overflowing the border in the lower nav menu\n3. A link wrapping when mousing over it\n4. A left menu item wrapping over two lines\n\n**Key indicators:**\n- The term \"Visual glitches\" directly relates to UI/rendering issues\n- Text overflow and wrapping problems are classic CSS/layout problems\n- No mention of memory corruption, information disclosure, privilege escalation, or any exploitable security impact\n- No security-related keywords like \"buffer overflow\", \"memory leak\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", or \"advisory\"\n\nThis is clearly a visual/layout issue affecting how elements are displayed on the page. The problems described (text overflow, link wrapping on hover, menu item wrapping) are all cosmetic/rendering defects with no security implications.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully to determine if it's a security bug or a non-security bug.\n\nThe bug report describes:\n1. Visual glitches on a website (orange.co.il) in Chrome on OS X\n2. Lower nav menu has text overflowing the border\n3. Mousing over a link causes the link to wrap\n4. Left menu item wraps over two lines\n\nThese are clearly visual/UI rendering issues. The keywords and indicators here are:\n- \"Visual glitches\" - related to visual display\n- Text overflowing border - layout issue\n- Link wrapping - UI/layout issue\n- Menu item wrapping - layout issue\n\nAccording to the domain knowledge provided:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug) includes \"UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems\" and keywords like \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"\n- Class 1 (Security Bug) involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability\n\nThis bug report is clearly about visual/layout issues, not security vulnerabilities. There's no mention of:\n- Memory corruption\n- Information disclosure\n- Denial of Service\n- Buffer overflow\n- Any exploitable security impact\n\nThis is purely a visual display/layout problem.\n\nThe answer is (A) Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 5518448584, "prompt_eval_count": 476, "eval_count": 465}}, {"id": "5484", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 5484 : No Chrome on Solaris or OpenSolaris 2      nbsp;   chekristo    Chrome Version : ALL   Opera  amp; Firefox  ? A version of Chrom for OpenSolaris  preferable available via IPS  ? There is currently no version of Chrome for Solaris/OpenSolaris and derivative platforms \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provi