s clearly a UI/UX issue about the visual appearance of an icon/keyboard representation. It's about making the visual display of a tab key icon look more native to Mac OS X styling.\n\nLooking at the classification guidelines:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability.\n\nThis bug is clearly about UI/UX rendering - specifically about how an icon looks visually. There's no security implication here at all - no memory corruption, no information disclosure, no DoS, no privilege escalation, no sandboxing bypass.\n\nThis is clearly a Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 24257675163, "prompt_eval_count": 593, "eval_count": 513}}, {"id": "7767", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of 