buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\" mentioned\n- No \"cross-context\" or sandboxing bypass\n- No \"javascript:\" or \"redirect\" vulnerability\n- No \"exhaustion\" or DoS context\n- No \"advisory\" or security-related keywords\n- No evidence of confidentiality, integrity, or availability compromise\n\nThis appears to be a non-reproducible, generic tab crash - the kind of instability that falls under Class 0 (Non-Security Bug). The \"Aw, Snap\" page is just Chrome's way of telling the user that a tab's renderer process died, but without any specific memory corruption or exploitable condition mentioned, this is just generic instability.\n\nAccording to the classification criteria:\n- Class 0 includes \"generic instability (e.g., 'crash' without memory corruption or exhaustion context)\"\n- This fits that description perfectly\n\nTherefore, this is a Non-Security Bug."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 13158841313, "prompt_eval_count": 531, "eval_count": 717}}, {"id": "13373", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS