cribes:\n1. Type \"two one three\" into a renderer page text-input\n2. Select \"two\" and drag text to before \"three\"\n3. Expected: \"one two three\"\n4. Actual: \"one three\" (i.e., \"two\" disappears)\n\nThis is a bug where dragging text in a text input field causes the dragged text to disappear instead of being moved to the new location. This is a functional defect related to text editing behavior in the browser.\n\nLet me check against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)** indicators:\n- Functional defects \u2713 (text drag-and-drop doesn't work correctly)\n- UI/UX rendering issues - partially related (input field behavior)\n- No exploitable security impact\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)** indicators:\n- Memory corruption - not present\n- Information disclosure - not present\n- Denial of Service - not present\n- Keywords like \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", privilege/sandboxing bypasses - none present\n\nThis is clearly a functional bug where drag-and-drop text manipulation in an input field causes text to disappear rather than be repositioned correctly. There's no security implication mentioned - no memory corruption, no information disclosure, no privilege escalation, no DoS potential. It's simply a UI behavior bug.\n\nThe answer should be Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 12244285276, "prompt_eval_count": 487, "eval_count": 588}}, {"id": "675", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulnerability detection.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Non-Security Bug)**: Encompasses functional defects, UI/UX rendering issues, layout problems, feature requests, and general application crashes that lack exploitable security impact. Distinctive indicators include keywords related to visual display (e.g., \"render\", \"CSS\", \"layout\"), user experience (e.g., \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"), and generic instability (e.g., \"crash\" without memory corruption or exhaustion context).\n\n**Class 1 (Security Bug)**: Involves vulnerabilities that compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability, such as memory corruption, information disclosure, or Denial of Service (DoS). Distinctive patterns and keywords include \"memory leak\", \"buffer overflow\", \"heap overwrite\", \"cross-context\", \"exhaustion\", \"javascript:\", \"redirect\", \"advisory\", and privilege/sandboxing bypasses.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Non-Security Bug, (B) Security Bug\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence of security relevance. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Bug Report:\nIssue 675 : Chrome Gear Application does not have a visible BACK button 1 person starred this issue and may be notified of changes.    nbsp;   varman72      :Official Build 1583 URLs (if applicable) : www.titook.net    : Safari 3: NA Firefox 3: NA IE 7: NA  ? 1. Go to www.titook.net 2. Create an application shortcut for a web application 3. Click on the Application shortcut on Windows Desktop 4. Click on the Blog button  ? The application launched properly and I was able to access the www.titook.net web site. However  when clicking on many links inside the web application  the web application brings the user to a different page or domains.  ? When this happens  their is no visible button that allows a user to go back to the original application. The only mechanism is to RIGHT-Click then select the Back option. It would be nice to have something (like a button) that allows users to go back to the original application or that they see a back button that allows user to go back to the previous page.     \n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify whether this is a Security Bug or Non-Security Bug. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T22:31:52.703398499Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this bug report, I need to analyze its nature carefully.\n\n**Key observations from the report:**\n\n1. **Core issue**: The Chrome Gear Application lacks a visible BACK button when users navigate to different pages or domains within the application.\n\n2. **Language indicators**: \n   - \"It would be nice to have something (like a button)\" - This is clearly a feature request\n   - \"visible button\" - UI/UX concern\n   - The user is describing a usability gap, not an exploitable vulnerability\n\n3. **What's happening**: When users click links that take them away from the original application, they can only navigate back via right-click context menu. The request is for a more accessible navigation option.\n\n4. **Security assessment**: While the report mentions navigation to \"different page or domains,\" this is not framed as a security vulnerability (like an open redirect attack). It's describing normal web navigation behavior where a UI element (back button) is missing for user convenience. There's no mention of memory corruption, privilege escalation, information disclosure, sandbox bypass, or any exploitable condition.\n\n**Conclusion**: This is a UI/UX feature request about missing navigation controls - a classic Non-Security Bug per the classification guidelines which explicitly include \"feature requests\" and \"user experience\" issues under Class 0.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this bug report carefully.\n\nThe bug report is about:\n- A Chrome Gear Application that doesn't have a visible BACK button\n- When a user clicks on links inside a web application that takes them to different pages or domains, there's no visible way to go back\n- The user has to right-click and select Back\n- The request is for a visible back button to navigate back to the original application or previous page\n\nKey aspects:\n1. This is essentially a UI/UX issue - the absence of a visible back button\n2. The user is requesting a feature (a back button) for better navigation\n3. Keywords like \"visible button\", \"It would be nice to have something\" indicate this is a feature request/UX improvement\n4. There's no mention of memory corruption, buffer overflow, information disclosure, or any security vulnerability\n5. The issue is about user experience and navigation convenience, not about security\n\nWhile the report mentions that the application \"brings the user to a different page or domains,\" this isn't framed as a security vulnerability (like an open redirect issue). It's framed as a usability problem where users can't easily navigate back.\n\nAccording to the classification guidelines:\n- Class 0 (Non-Security Bug): Includes UI/UX rendering issues, feature requests, and keywords like \"feature request\", \"shortcut\"\n- Class 1 (Security Bug): Involves vulnerabilities like memory corruption, information disclosure, DoS, with keywords like \"redirect\" in a security context\n\nThis report is clearly a UX/feature request issue - the user wants a visible back button for easier navigation. It's not reporting a security vulnerability.\n\n**Answer: (A) Non-Security Bug**"}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 16822618420, "prompt_eval_count": 560, "eval_count": 632}}, {"id": "14004", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in bug report classification for security vulner